      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>MUNITIONS COURT.</head>
        <p>MUNITIONS COURT.</p>
        <p>More Certificates Refused To Self-Discharged Workers.</p>
        <p>POINTS WORTH NOTING.</p>
        <p>At a sitiing of the local Munitions Tribunal, held at the Labour Exchange ontMonday after- noon—before Mr. T. J. Ridgway (chairman) and Messrs. H. P. Daglish. of St. Helens, and Mr. Berry, of Warrington (assessors)—a man who had been working at a well-known local estab- lishment for eleven :months applied for a cer- tificate entitling him to give his services else- where. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>TRAINED BY THE FIRM.</head>
        <p>TRAINED BY THE FIRM.</p>
        <p>A representative of the .firm concerned said that the man was taken on twelve months ago as a general labourer at 225. a week. The firm had trained him as a producer man and had d'dvanced his wage until he had been getting over 355. a week. Then the firm changed their type of producer .and put 'him on other work. All the men who were changed objected at the moment, but subsequently every one, including the man 'before tie tribunal, said they were perfectly. satisfied and worked for a week at the new job. On the Friday night, however, when the man came in to 'do his ehift he refused to do the work, but on that oecasion nothing what- ever was said about refusing his certificate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>Denial That Certificate Was Refused.</head>
        <p>Denial That Certificate Was Refused.</p>
        <p>The head of the departmentiin which the man was working denied that he ever applied for ~n certificate and was refused.</p>
        <p>One of the managers said that the man was a link in the chain 'in the 'manufacture of an ingredient necessary in 'high explosives. The Chairman: Is he a neceSsary link? The Manager: One man is never necessary, but if all the men doing 'his particular class of work left we should be distinctly impeded. The work is such as can be done by only highly trained men. The certificate sought was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>ENLISTED-AND WAS SENT BACK TO</head>
        <p>ENLISTED-AND WAS SENT BACK TO</p>
        <p>WORK.</p>
        <p>Another applicant for a certificate was a man who enlisted in August last, but who had been sent back to his work, because what he had been doing was necessary for the turning out of the requisite munitions. He complained, however, that after being sent back he had not been given the work which he had previously been doing, but was put on a labouring job, which meant a reduction in his wages of about 14s. a week. He had asked for his old work back, but had been refused. He had also asked for his transfer to the iron and steel trade, at which he had worked for seven years.</p>
        <p>The applicant in this ease did not appear in person, but was represented by his father, who said that, his son was willing to go back to his old firm provided he was given his old job. It was pointed out that when the man enlisted he was doing work which was of the greatest importance from the munitions, point of Moreover, he enlisted without giving any sort of notice and another man was taken on in his place to do the work which he had been doing. His average wages had been £1 9s. 4d. per week, although it was true that he drew £1 15s. dur- ing the last few weeks he worked. He would have made more than the ..t1 9s. 4d. since he came back had he worked full time.</p>
        <p>Again the certificate sought was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>LEFT BECAUSE OF FOREMAN'S OBSCENITIES.</head>
        <p>LEFT BECAUSE OF FOREMAN'S OBSCENITIES.</p>
        <p>The third; and last, applicant for a certificate was a`lialmn who said that he had left his work because his foreman had used obscene language toward him. "But," added the applicant. amid laughter, "I used some to him afterwards and I gave notice. The language used to me was not fit for a dog to hear."</p>
        <p>A letter from the firm concerned denied that the man's certificate would have been -withheld had he applied for it, but they had cancelled it because they learned that he had applied to that Tribunal and pending the decision of the Chairman and Assessors. If the man would tender an apology for the swear words he used the firm were willing to grant him his certifi- cate, hut they, did not want to restart him. • Who Should Apologise?</p>
        <p>The applicant contended that it was not his place to apologise; the foreman started cursing him first. "And if," the man added, "he will apologise to me, I will apologise to him. Other- wise I will only apologise to the manager. The Certificate was refused, the Clerk to the Court (Mr. G. F. F. White) remarking "If you are prepared to apologise they will give you your certificate at the works, but this Tribunal cannot do it !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>THANKING THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THANKING THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>Council's Appreciation Of His War-Year Service.</p>
        <p>'LEFT NOTHING UNDONE."</p>
        <p>The last meeting of the Warrington Town Council for the official year was held at the , Town Hall on Tuesday evening, and was at- tended,by the Mayor (Alderman peter Peacock), the Deputy Mayor (Dr.. loseph), Alderineit. Batton, Tinniaa, Smethtirgt and Beiinett, and Councillors Broadhurst, Roberts, Dickenson,' Gough, Pemberton, Cunnington, Skelland,' Wilson, 'Wilkinson, Chantler, Locker, Hen-11 shall, Dale, Barton, Firth, Lindley, Archer areal Chrimes.</p>
        <p>" UNVA RYING COURTESY AND I MPAIRTIALITY."</p>
        <p>As is customary, at the conclusion of the business a vote of thanks was accorded .to his Worship for his services during the past trying twelve months. The vote was proposed by. Alderman Bovroxznd was couched in he foi/Ow- ing terms :--" That the best thanks of this Council be presented to Alderman Peter Pea- cock, for the satisfactory manner in which he has discharged 'the responsible duties devolving upon him as Mayor and Chief Magistrate of the borough during this his second year Of office, and for the izienvarying courtesy and impartiality displayed by -him when presiding over the de- liberations if the Council, and in his inter- course with its members." The Mayor, the mover said, had had a very trying year of office. 1 but had got through his many duties in a man- ner that was most creditable, just as they hoped he would be able to get through the duties of the year to come. His Worship had not given the members of the Council, nor the inhabi- tants of fhe town, cause for a single complaint, but had discharged the work of his high office in a way that was creditable both to himself and to t'he town. Not only had the Mayor done well, but the Mayoress had also done well, and he wished to take that opportunity of thank- ing the lady for the able manner in which she had helped her husband and for the services she had rendered to the town; 'The town had every reason to be satisfied whilst it had such a Mayor and Mayoress, both of whom were deserving of all the expressions of gratitude that could be given Them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>AN ARDUOUS TWELVEMONTH.</head>
        <p>AN ARDUOUS TWELVEMONTH.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tuo'lox seconded the resolution, remarking that he thought that much as former occupants of the Mayoral chair had been de- serving of praise at the end of their terms of office, the present Mayor was specially deserv- ing of the Council's approval, because of the very arduous, trying work which had fallen on his shoulders during the past twelve months, and because of the most satisfactory manner in which he had discharged that work. Alderman Peacock was a positive glutton for work— (laughter and applause)—and he never regarded the • doing of anything as too much trouble. Moreover, whenever he set out to do a thing he did it well. (Hear, hear.) The time that he had given—to say nothing of the amount of money he had given; that was also considerable -represented a great amount of effort and sacri- fice, for he had left nothing undone that it was within his power to do. He had done his best not only for the town, hut for the country generally, his outlook being wider than the con- fines of the borough. The question he had ad- dressed to himself did not stop at "What can I do Tor Warrington?" He had gone further and asked himself "What can I do for my King and for my country, which is fighting for its national, 'its Imperial existence?" To a great extent his 'Worship had been assisted by the Mayoress, and he (the Alderman) regretted that it was not in order to associate the name of that lady in the vote of thanks, as it would appear in the official records. (Applause.) DUTIES DOUBLED AND TREBLED.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT supported, remarking that in normal,times the office of Mayor of a borough like Warrington was by no means a sinecure, for each succeeding year saw the duties become increasingly onerous. But the months during which Alderman' Peacock had occupied• the Mayoral chair had been by no means normal months, and the ,duties appertaining to the office had been doubled and trebled, for on top of all the ordinary duties he had had all the locarburdens and anxieties of the present hor- rible Ivan Alderman Peacock had risen to the occasion, and had .well led their patriotic aspira- tions; 'indeed, he (the speaker) doubted whether they could have been led in a better or more public-.spirited manner. (Hear, hear.) And they must remeniber that his Worship had done his work under the pressure of constant per- sonal anxiety, for he had given a son to the ser- vice of the country—(applause)—and everybody must know what that entailed. Alderman. Be- nnett went on to pay a graceful compliment to the Mayoress, and concluded by saying: "I hope in the. coming year the clouds which have hung so heavily over us will have passed away, and that the close of, his Worship's term of office will be happier for him and all of us." (Applause:) OUR KINDLY MAYORESS. •</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST also eulogised the ser- vices of Alderman and Mrs. Peacock, and ga,jr it as his opinion that the responsibilities NI-Nur devolved upon the Mayoress were almo,-. to those which the Mayor had hacrl,oages, ar. He should never forget one act of kin, small ;•hich had been performed by the May 11J Oawchich he saw himself. She had been a go( ".d faithful servant to the town.</p>
        <p>Councillor ARCHER said that no Mayor could have conducted himself with greater aptitude during the past troublous year than had Alderman Peacock, and his name wmild be honoured in Warrington for years to come. (Hear, hear.) Councillor LOCKER referred to the Mayor's well-known sympathy with the sorrows of all classes of the community, and said that the kindly manner in which he had met people of all sorts on all occasions had won him great popularity. The DEPUTY-MAYOR (Dr. Joseph) paid testi- mony to the Mayor's capacity for and deep interest in the public work of the town, and said that he did not think anyone could have done the work thb.t Alderman Peacock had done had he any other work to do. The Doctor went on to compliment the Mayoress upon the able support which she had given her hus- band, and expressed the opinion that a great deal of the success attending any Mayoralty depended upon the Mayoress. (Hear, hear.) He would not give the exact proportion in which such success depended upon the Mayoress, but it was somewhere between five per cent. and 50 per cent., for a good deal depended upon it. (Hear, hear, and laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WHOLE COMMUNITY APPRECIATIVE.</head>
        <p>WHOLE COMMUNITY APPRECIATIVE.</p>
        <p>Councillor CHRIMES said that the Mayor's services were not only appreciated by the members of the Council, but also by the whole community. Councillor BROADHURST said that Alderman Peacock, whilst' Mayor, had always meted out justice to every member of the Council. One good feature about the present Mayor was that he was not too ready to apply the out-of-order rule as if it were one of the laws of the Medes and Persians. The whole of the people of Warrington owed the Alderman an everlasting debt of gratitude. lam proud of you," con- tinued the Councillor, "and if I may, for another year, put my shoulder to the wheel along with your Worship and help to make things go, you may -depend upon it that I shall do so."</p>
        <p>Councillor HENSIIALL said "ditto, ditto," to all that previous speakers had said. Alderman BOLTON then put his proposition, and it was carried unanimously. • THE MAYOR'S RESUME.</p>
        <p>In returning thanks the MAYOR, briefly re- viewed the principal local happenings of the past year. He said: I thank you very sincerely for the manner in which you have just given me this unanimous vote of thanks. The evident sincerity of the speakers leads me to believe that the vote is not a mere empty • form, but is a sign of the good friendship that exists between the .chair and the members of the Connell. (Hear,- hear.) The latitude which I give to menaberS is, at times, undoubt- edly abused—(" Oh; oh!")—but I do think, withal, that it serves a good purpose. Some- times I think that the latitude which I give to some members is enough to hang them, but I go on giving' it, feeling that the town's best interests are served by overlooking little pieces of irregularity. During the past year I have attended 269 meetings of the Council and its committees, and this is not taking into account</p>
        <p>I do not propose to say anything further with regard to recruiting, but I shall have some fur- ther remarks to make on the 9th November, and I sincerely hope that the young men of the town will do their duty again, and give us all cause to be very proud of them. I thank you, on behalf of the Mayoress, for the very kind words you have spoken. She, indeed, has had an exceptional and trying year, and by visiting the wounded and helping in charitable and social work, has been an ideal helpmate. In conclusion, the Mayor thanked the Deputy Mayor, the Town Clerk and his staff, the, Borough Treasurer and his staff, the other offi- cials of the Corporation, and his colleagues in the Council, for the help they had given him, and the meeting closed with the singing of "God save the King," which was led by Alderman Smethurst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDA f, OCTOBER 3, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDA f, OCTOBER 3, 1915.</p>
        <p>the 'meetings of the Education Committee. I have also .had over 200 public engagements out- sae, and 102 have been in. connection with war purposes only, During. the year, which has been overshadowed by the clOud.of war which, at present is on the European horizon, there have beem Very feiv municipal events to com- memorate.. I was very happy to be ,able to officiate et the laying of the :faundation4tone of the Oakwood-avenue. Council School in Nov- ember taFit, arid again to be able, in April, to • the tablet which cciiiainemerrked the convietiOn of the Warrington Bridge, a noble and aignified.,:'struCture„ which I hope 'will cope with the 'thiffic betiVeen the two counties for many years toccnit. We all regret the death of Mr. J. J.Webster, the engineer, and are sorrx ihdt he was not -spared to witness the operting. (Hear, hear.) The Municipal Rifle Range was opened .in January at the back of The baths, and 'kindly furnished with rifles by Alderman Bolton, and, I believe, it has been very extensively used. A TribUte'-to Father Swarbreck.</p>
        <p>The opening of the new St. Benedict's Church was the occasion of Warrington being honoured by the visit of Cardinal Bourne. He was re- ceived by, myself and the Mayoress at the Town Hall, and while mentioning that incident, it may not be inappropriate for me to allude to the untimely death of Father Swarbreck, to whose life amongst us the new church of St. Benedict will always be a lasting memorial. He was, a man of a singular charm of manner, and his death leaves a gap which will be hard to fill.</p>
        <p>During the year, Warrington has been very active in connection with charities on behalf of the'Avar. A successful Flag Day was held, and a motor ambulance named after the town was subscribed for and presented to the British Red Cross Society for service at the Front, the Mayoress having the honour of being presented to Her Majesty the Queen on that occasion. (Hear, hear.) Warrington's New Knight.</p>
        <p>The well-deserved honour which was conferred by His Majesty the King upon Sir Arthur Cros- field, a member of a well-known Warrington family, has given much pleasure to all, and has been recorded upon your minutes. The bust of Mr. John Crosfield has recently been unveiled, and now stands in the hall, a striking likeness, and a gift which, I am sure, will be appreciated by all. The town, in July, was greatly honoured by the conferring of the Victoria Cross upon Corporal J. IL Tombs for conspicuous bravery in Flanders. The story of his action and gallantry is now too well known to be repeated. He is, I understand, now wounded in England, and I am sure that every one of you will join me in wishing him a speedy recovery. (Hear, hear.) Warrington's Fallen Braves._</p>
        <p>Lastly, I feel that a resume of the year's proceedings will be incomplete without refer- ring to the brave Warrington lads who have given their lives for King and country. The toll has been heavy, and I have had the sad task of writing to the parents of those who have fallen, endeavouring, by kindly words of sym- pathy, to lighten their sorrows as much as pos- sible. Their names will ever be honoured amongst us. We also have to think with feel- ings of pride of those Warringtonians who to- day are serving their King in the different arenas of this terrible war. We are very proud of them, too, and also of the fact that Warring- ton is not lagging behind when honour and duty has called. But we want' more men and still more men, and an appeal is now being made to men, not only in Warrington, but all over the country, and it entirely depends upon them as to whether the splendid system of voluntaryism. of which we have all been justly proud, shall be maintained.</p>
        <p>A Word to the Young Men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CONSTiTUTIONAL ?</head>
        <p>CONSTiTUTIONAL ?</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough's Question To The Mayor.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the General Purposes Com- mittee, submitted for confirmation at Tuesday evening's meeting of the Warrington Town Council contained the following items : "The Town Clerk reported that the term of office of each —nk the following members of the Education ' rmittee will expire on the dates stated ,hat Councillor Osborne had inti- mot inability to continue his service on ..a4.8 Lindy I _...ttee : Councillor Chrimes (9th Novem- Ltney„ 1915), Councillor Jenkins (9th November, a• 111;), Councillor Lindley (9th November, 1915), Councillor Osborne (9th November, 1915), Coun- cillor Dickenson (4th December, 1915). It was resolved that the following members be appointed representative members of the Educa- tion Committee: Alderman John Evans (as and from the 9th November, 1915), Councillors Chrimes, Jenkins and Lindley (as and from the 9th November. 1915), and Councillor Dickenson (as and from the 4th December, 1915)." Councillor Gough asked whether the election of rdhresentative members reported. that even- ing was constitutional?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>MAYOR'S RULING –</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S RULING -</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock) : My ruling is that it is in order. If it is unconsti- tutional you can alter it to-night by referring it back:</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough: I should like to point out that supposing an election were to take place on November Ist it is just possible that after- wards it might be found impossible to appoint some of these men!</p>
        <p>The Mayor: I have given my ruling and I am not going to alter it. Councillor Gough: I move that the minute be referred back, and I do so because I think it is an injustice to members of this Council that the chairman of any committee should propose the election or re-election of any member of that committee.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: It is quite in order, I tell you. Councillor' Gough: It does not look very creditable for any chairman -</p>
        <p>- AND THE MAYOR'S HOPE!</p>
        <p>The Mayor (interrupting): I do hope, Coun- cillor Gough, that at this, the last, meeting of the year you will not introduce any subject that is going to be personal. Don't do it, for my sake! (Hear, hear, and a voice: "Get on! it's quite in order!") Councillor Gough: I move- that this matter be referred back because I think it would give a chance to other, members of the Council to be proposed for this committee. The Councillor's proposition found no seconder, and consequently the matter dropped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>TIIE "%VAR BUDGET.</head>
        <p>TIIE "%VAR BUDGET.</p>
        <p>The tragedy of 'a midnight 'vigil is pictorially presented with striking effect in the current issue of the "War Budget," which gives an almost ghostly appearance to the form of the gigantic /German sniper, stalking unconsciously toward the British bayonet that is about to behave as a British bayonet should. Another arresting picture, of an entirely different char- acter, sets our thoughts wandering through the mazes of Balkan policy, as it shows the Allies hastening to Serbia's aid through Salonika. The .super-heroism of the late Captain Car- michael forms the subject of the big picture or centre-piece, and a thrilling chapter on. War by Night adds :to the interest of the number. "For night attacks," the writer points out, ." our Ghurkas and other Indian troops are frequently employed, not because of their darker visages, . but because they can See so much better at night than their English comrades." In addition to the full tale of pictures and other regular features, this week's "War Budget" caters for the " home " reader • with an article on "Women's War Clubs." illustrated from "life," and a double page of foreign war cartoons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>REJECTED OFFER.</head>
        <p>A REJECTED OFFER.</p>
        <p>Council Refuse To Sell Site For Picture Show.</p>
        <p>PRINCIPLE OR PRICE?</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Finance and Estate Committee submitted for approval at Tues- day's meeting of the Town Council contained the following item: "An offer to purchase a site in Buttermarket-street for the purposes of a picturedrome was considered, and it was resolved that the offer be not entertained for that purpose." The minute quoted gave rise to a discussion of some length.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON'S VIEWS.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON'S VIEWS.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON moved that the minute be referred back for reconsideration. In an- other form, he said, the matter came before the Street Improvement Committee, and that body, with the help of the Surveyor, spent a considerable time in devising ways by which the amount of land required for a picture- drome could be "schemed." They eventually hit upon a site which they thought would satisfy the prospective purchasers. And then came this resolution of the Finance and Estate Committee! It seemed to him that the Council ought to try in every possible way to got the surplus lands which they at present held off their hands, because such lands would then become profit-earning and would pay rates. The proposed picturedrome, along with the two shops which it was also proposed to erect, would look a great deal better than a piece of land with tumble-down houses, half up and half down, such as were to be seen there at present. His only fear with regard to the surplus land in Buttermarket-street was that they were going to have real difficulty in dis- posing of it. Such an offer as the committee had received ought not to be treated in a cavalier manner. He did not think that the proper way to treat the offer was for the com- mittee to have said "We won't entertain it!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>COUNtILLOR GOUGH WANTS TO KNOW</head>
        <p>COUNtILLOR GOUGH WANTS TO KNOW</p>
        <p>THE FIGURE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gotron seconded Councillor Pem- berton's proposition. The very least, he thought, that the committee could have done was to make the Council acquainted with the offer that they had received. As it was the Council were absolutely in the dark; they did not know whether the oiler was a good or a bad one, nor did they know why it had been re- jected. He trusted that in their own interests the Council would send the matter back. Councillor Wn.xncsorr, as chairman of the Finance and Estate Committee, said that the price offered for the land was exceedingly low —much too low—and it was also felt that it would not be desirable to have a picturedrome in that particular part of the town. Alderman BOLTON thought that had the price offered been satisfactory the committee would not have been justified in refusing the offer, simply on the ground that it was proposed to build a picturedrome on the site.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>ALDERMAN TINNION WOULD LIKE MORE LIGHT.</head>
        <p>ALDERMAN TINNION WOULD LIKE MORE LIGHT.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION said he was not quite sure that the explanation given by Councillor Wil- kinson was as full as they would like. Of course, he did not like to over-ride the delib- erate decision of the committee, but he would point out that the Council were not told exactly what land it was the picturedrome people wanted, nor what was the price they offered. They had to take into account the point that the erection of a picturedrome in the locality suggested might conceivably depre- ciate the value of the surrounding land. Any- way, they might have a little more informa- tion before they finally decided, for, if they passed the resolution put before them that night% they finally closed the door on the business.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST agreed with the .min- utes. Whatever price had been offered for the land, he said, he would not have agreed to its I use for the erection of a picturedrome. They might get a good price and yet, by allowing! the land to be devoted to that purpose, destroy , the happiness of many homes. Only last even- ing a lady told him she, would like to invite members of the Council to stand near the door of one of these picture halls and see poor children coming out of them.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: I want you to keep to the point.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST : I am giving the reason why I vote against the selling of this land. You see the poor children- The MAYOR: It is the only entertainment they have.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST : You see women coming out with children in their arms and yet we are always hearing about poverty. We have got quite sufficient picture houses already.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>"FIT AND PROPER SITE." SAYS COUNCILLOR BROADHURST.</head>
        <p>"FIT AND PROPER SITE." SAYS COUNCILLOR BROADHURST.</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADHURST supported. the amend- ment that the matter be referred back, be- cause he was of opinion that the site named was a fit and proper one for the erection of a picture house. They could have a beautiful building entirely in harmony with the develop- ment of the district. In the past the Watch Committee had granted licences for places which should never have been picture houses. In his opinion a great amount of education could be gained from a picture house. Councillor PEMBERTON : Hear, hear! The DitAYoR : The question is as to whether the site suggested is suitable or not. Councillor BROADHURST went on to say that he hoped that every member who had the best interests of the rising generation at heart would vote with him, remembering the educa- tion that could be given to children at a picturedrome, and remembering also that the Watch Committee would have control over any, picture house that might be erected and would see to it that the pictures shown were of the right sort—of the sort that would make the children better citizens. Children could sit in "these places" and enjoy a nice even- ing. such as it was impossible for them to get under any other conditions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>PRICE UNREASONABLE.</head>
        <p>PRICE UNREASONABLE.</p>
        <p>Councillor LOCKER said the committee, as a body, did not oppose- the sale of the land on the ground that it was unsuitable for the erec- tion of a picturedrome. Their only reasou was that the price offered was extremely • unreason- able for the amount of land required. Councillor PEMBERTON : Then why does the resolution state "that the offer be not enter- tained for that purpose"? The MAYOR: I was at the meeting and that was the point of refusal. Councillor WILKINSON thought the Council might very well leave the matter in the hands of the committee, seeing that it consisted of one-third of the whole of the members. The price offered for the site of the proposed picturedrome was ridiculously low for the part of the town.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH.: We want to judge of that. Councillor WILKINSON: We; as members of the committee, knew the prices we had charged other people for land. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: And this offer was below them?</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON': Oh, tremendously! A ridiculous price! It is not right for it to be mentioned in public. It was so ridiculous that if we did treat for the land at that price, we might as well give the next site away. Councillor PEMBERTON : I think the Council are entitled to know the price, and on a point of order I ask for it.</p>
        <p>The ArAYOR : That is not _a point of order.</p>
        <p>• On a vote being taken the amendment only round six supporters and was therefore de- clared lost.</p>
        <p>An Anti-dote. Jack: But I thought you doted on Miss Peachley ? Tom: So I did—until I met her mother. Jack: Well? Tom: The old lady proved to be an anti-dote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Not Just What She Meant.</head>
        <p>Not Just What She Meant.</p>
        <p>The little girl was very fond of pleasant days,' and at-the close of a heavy rain storm petitioned in her prayer for fine weather; when, the next morning, the sun shone bright and 'clear she became jubilant and -told her prayer to her grandmother, who said Well, dear, why can't you pray to-night that' it may be warmer to-morrow, so that grandma':; rheumatism will be better?" "All right, I will," was the quick response; and that night as she knelt she said: "0 Lord, please make it hot for grandma."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NEW PRINCIPAL.</head>
        <p>THE NEW PRINCIPAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. S. Broome Formally Appointed.</p>
        <p>The matter. of the appointment of Mr. Jas. S. Broome to succeed the late Mr. E. S. A. Robson as principal of the Secondary School and Technical Institute, was among .the items of business dealt with at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee, held at the Town Hall on Monday evening. The School Management Sub-committee reported that they had interviewed the following half dozen candi- dates for the post: Mr. Richard L. Ager (Prestwich), Mr. Arthur B. Archer (Liverpool), Mr. Jas. S. Broome (Warrington), Mr. Thos. Dean (Swindon), Mr. Douglas J. Kay (Bootle), and Mr. Walter Ritchings .(St. Helens). "After carefully considering their respective qualifications," the sub-committee's report read "It was resolved that Mr. James S. Broome be appointed principal of the school and institute at a commencing salary in accordance with the committee's scale, his engagement to date from the Ist October, 1915, and to be determinable by three months' notice, to expire on the last day of any calendar month, given by or to the committee."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>SUB-COMMITTEE'S WISE CHOICE.</head>
        <p>SUB-COMMITTEE'S WISE CHOICE.</p>
        <p>In submitting the sub-committee's report for confirmation, the Rev. Canon Stevenson re- gretted that he was unable to be present at the meeting of the -sub-committee at which the appointment was made, but said he could claim to have done the spade work, having gone through some hundred and fifty applications and reduced them to six. He had not the slightest doubt that the sub-committee, whom he knew gave very long and careful attention to the matter of that appointment, had made a wise choice. Mr. Broome was not an untried mail. Mr. Broome had been at the school for some time, and during the last few years—when, un- fortunately, the late principal's health was such as not to enable him to give the time that he would otherwise have done to his duties—a great deal of the work of the school had devolved upon him, and he (the Canon) thought that all the members of the committee would agree that Mr. Broome had risen to a very difficult situation and had carried on the work with a degree of loyalty to the late principal that was admirable and that was satisfactory also as regards the efficiency of the school. They trusted that Mr. Broome would continue to build up on the foundation so well laid an excellent Secondary School that would be a lasting credit to the town. He was quite certain that the best wishes of the committee went to Mr. Broome. (Hear, her.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>RIGHT MAN IN RIGHT PLACE.</head>
        <p>RIGHT MAN IN RIGHT PLACE.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Upson said that the sub-committee, when interviewing the gentlemen named, could not help but feel that each one of them would have made an excellent principal, but they only had one post to offer and, taking all things into consideration he thought the choice the com- mittee finally made was the right one. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennetts opined that Mr. Broome would find it difficult to follow in the footsteps of the late Mr. Robson, but he was sure that he would justify the choice which the com- mittee had made.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Councillor H. Roberts) hoped that the good wishes that had been expressed would be a source of great encouragement to Mr. Broome in his new appointment. The committee's recommendation was endorsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>LATE MR. F. DAINTITH.</head>
        <p>LATE MR. F. DAINTITH.</p>
        <p>Education Committee's Praise</p>
        <p>And Sympathy.</p>
        <p>Sympathetic references were made at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee on Monday evening to the recent death of Mr. Frederick, Daintith, certificated assistant master in the boys' department' of ,the Parochial School, and with whose relatives the School Management 'Sub-committee re- ported having passed a resolution of sympathy and condolence.</p>
        <p>Canon Morley Stevenson said that the late Mr. Daintith was one of the very best of the assistant teachers in the town. He was a man who threw himself wholeheartedly into his work and never spared himself trouble, nor did he spare his strength, in carrying out that work to the best ofahis power. Not only were the relatives to be g-ympathised with, but also the headmaster and managers of the school at which he taught, for they, too, had sustained a deep loss. The educational work of the town was the poorer by the loss of one who was an able and earnest teacher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>"Spent Himself At His Work."</head>
        <p>"Spent Himself At His Work."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. -Upson endorsed the Canon's remarks concerning the late Mr. Daintith, adding that the deceased gentleman spent himself at his work, and, perhaps, he might have been spared to them yet had he not worked SO arduously; had he not been so utterly devoted to his duty. He had worn himself out in the work which he had at heart, and now he had gone to his rest and reward. Alderman Bennett said Mr. Daintith's com- paratively early death was little short of a tragedy.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. Fairclough, on behalf of the man- agers of the school to which the late Mr. Damn- tith was attached, paid his testimony to the worth of the deceased gentleman as a teacher. The sub-committee's action in passing the resolution of sympathy was endorsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>DEARER COKE!</head>
        <p>DEARER COKE!</p>
        <p>Alderman's Amusing Confession Al Council Meeting.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's meeting of the Warrington Town Council the Gas Committee reported their decision to increase the price of coke to 16s. Bd. per•ton, on and after October Ist _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett raised a number of smiles by confessing that the day before the advance was to come into operation he wanted to be in time and so he telephoned to the Gas Office and ordered a ton of. coke. Much to his sur- prise, however, he was told that it would be supplied at the enhanced price. He was not quarrelling at the price, but he thought the notice of the committee was misleading, and others, who 'were not so generously-minded as himself, might object to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SMALL QUANTITIES FOR THE POOR?</head>
        <p>SMALL QUANTITIES FOR THE POOR?</p>
        <p>Councillor Wilson complained that the com- mittee had not carried out the arrangement which it was stated last year was being made to supply poor people with coke in small quan- tities and added that in one case he knew of an order was sent to the Gas Offices for coke to be delivered in a certain part of the town, but the order was never even acknow- ledged. He could quite understand why poor people would not go down to the Gas Offices for coke; they were only cold-shouldered when they got there. He thought that some arrange- ment should be made by which the people who only wanted their one cwt., or two cwts., should be able to get it.. Councillor Broadhurst could not see why the price of crushed coke should be 21s. 4d. a ton. against 16s. Bd. a ton for ordinary coke. From what he could hear it was not because' the committee did not want to supply the poor people with coke that they were not getting it, but simply because there had been a shortage. It would be a pleasure to the committee to supply the poor people with all they wanted, were they able to do so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>COURTESY FOR LARGE OR SMALL CONSUMERS.</head>
        <p>COURTESY FOR LARGE OR SMALL CONSUMERS.</p>
        <p>Coun.cillor Locker, alluding to Alderman Bennett's reference to the increase in the price of coke, said that so many orders for coke were received on the day before the advance took effect that it was impossible to make deliveries that day. The price charged was that obtain- ing when the coke was delivered. With regard to the supply of coke in small quantities, when an undertaking had to deal with 2,000 tons of coke monthly there was always a risk of mis- hap, but the committee did their best to treat customers with 'the utmost courtesy, whether they wanted one cwt. or 5,000 tons. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>VALUE OF SOOT AS MANURE.</p>
        <p>Most farmers are familiar with the use of soot as a fertiliser—in fact, it is probable' that in many cases its value is overrated. Its com- position varies greatly in different samples, but its manurial value depends on the amount of sulphate of ammonia it contains, usually about 20 per cent. in good samples,, yielding up to 5 per cent. of ammonia, or about 4 per cent. of nitrogen. It is, therefore, a nitrogenous manure, like nitrate of soda or sulphate of ammonia, and should be applied in spring, generally as a lop dressing. In this way it is frequently uSed for wheat or oats, at the rate of 40 to 60* bushels per acre. Considered strictly as a fertiliser, .0 is far less valuable than sulphate of ammonia, and should not be used unless it can be obtained cheaply. But it has on heavy clays an important mechanical action that is highly valued by many farmers. It sweetens and opens the soil, and renders it darker in colour, and therefore warmer. It is also discouraging to slugs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>OVER-HEATED HAY.</head>
        <p>OVER-HEATED HAY.</p>
        <p>Heated or " mow-burnt " hay is also easily recognised. There is a certain demand for it in the trade, and it is esteemed for cutting into chaff, being used to give a " nose " to inferior or weather-beaten stuff. A eertain amount of fermentation in the stack is needful for the development of, sound hay, but when carried too far it is injurious, and has a bad effect on the urinary organs. Most horses will eat this kind of hay with avidity, but it quickly affects their condition, and those who are foolish enough to give it preference, because their horses seem to like it at first, or use it extensively for cutting up with chaff, eventu- ally learn that it is far from being a whole- some food for horses. Mouldy hay is readily recognised by its white appearance and strong peculiar smell. It should never be fed to horses, and is not even safe for bedding, be- cause greedy feeders pick it over.—" Veteran" in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>PLOUGHING.</head>
        <p>PLOUGHING.</p>
        <p>Never perhaps in the memory of any among us has the work of ploughing caused so much anxiety, and this in spite of the wonderful weather we have had. The Minister of Agri- culture has announced that ploughmen will be allowed to return from the Front, and if this return is not delayed it will help. Motor ploughs of several makes have been publicly tried, but so far farmers have not been much impressed with their work, and so for this season at least they will have to rely upon the old tool. I make the suggestion that the broad-breasted plough does the best work, espe- cially when knives are attached for cutting the slice of soil into two or three strips, and thus breaking it up, and permitting it to lie rougher and looser for the action of the frost. There is one question in connection with the point which is worthy of attention, and which I learned to appreciate from an old workman of my own. He held the opinion that failure in crops was chiefly due to unhealthy soil, and that when limed and left in the rough for wintering it acquired a vigorous tone. This is certainly true, for acidity is followed by a sweeter condition, while destructive pests, whe- ther of an insect or fungoid character, are themselves destroyed. Where it is possible to run a manure distributing machine over the land, while still in the rough, 10 or 12 cwt. of finely ground chalk lime to the acre will prove most advantageous, where there is any indica- tion of an unhealthy condition, or where pests have damaged the root, potato, or corn crops. There is, too, a great and continuous tendency to plough too shallow. I am conscious of the danger of bringing up the subsoil to the sur- face, but every old man on the farm is aware of the importance of deep cultivation, which adds to the quantity of soil available for plant growth. To this end, therefore, the plough which has been accustomed to a few inches may go slightly deeper each year, until two or three inches are added to the cultivated soil. The object is to provide more mineral food; a greater area of fine soil for the roots of the plants, which will travel further to find and appropriate it, and to resume healthy condi- tions.—Jas. Long, in "Farm and Florae."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>CARNATIONS AND PICOTEES.</p>
        <p>These beautiful plants are not cultivated half as much as they should be. Why, I can- not say. Their culture is of the easiest and the flowers are greatly admired, always look- ing well in the garden, vase or coat. They are quite hardy and produce large spikes of blooms of various shades, the flowers lasting well when cut. For soils there is no fast rule, although a light, rich soil suits them best, but I have seen good plants and grand blooms grown on stiff soil. Such soils should have road grit or crushed oyster shells mixed with it to lighten it somewhat. Lime and soot is also very good dug in. If for exhibition make your beds about 9 inches high, so that the water will drain off, and plant about 12 inches to 18 inches, according to variety. In the Spring give each plant a slight dressing of two-parts bone meal and one-part sulphate of ammonia or sulphate of potash,. and lightly prick it in the soil. Then as the flower-stems appear stake up with 2ft. canes or spiral springs. The former look the neater, but the latter are much used. _ _</p>
        <p>A good plan is not to grow more than six grasses for layering, so that the flowers can have as much nourishment as possible. When the buds appear, carefully pinch off the side- buds, retaining the crown-bud; that is, if size of bloom is desired; but if only for decoration pinch a few of the crowded ones off. The later buds, when they open, generally give some grand coloured blooms. Plants should now be ordered from carna- tion specialists, and best results are obtained from plants planted in October and November, or potted up in 3-inch pots and kept in a cold frame till Spring. When planting, see that each plant is made firm, so that high winds will not loosen the roots.</p>
        <p>There are various kinds of carnations, but I will only deal with selfs and fancies (bizarres and flakes are not so popular as the former). A few of the best self-coloured (in pairs) are:— Bookham White and Amy Robsart, white; W. 11. Parton and Mrs. G. Marshall, crimson and maroon; Cardinal and Brigadier, scarlet; Miss Schiffner and Wyatt, rose; Queen Mary and Lady Hermoine, salmon scarlet; Daffodil and Miss A. Campbell, yellow; Duchess of Wellington and Silver Fox, heliotrope; Mrs. R. Morton and F. Samuelson, apricot. Other grand varieties are :—Eliz. Shiffner (orange), Benbow (buff), La Milo (dog-rose pink), Miss Willmot (coral pink). Fancies.—Sam Weller, yellow ground, marked red and crimson; Rony Buchanan,- terra-cotta, suffused rose; Linkman, .yellow, barred bright scarlet; Queen Eleanor, buff, heavily marked; John Ridd, yellow, suffused'. and *narked .rosy red; Lord Steyne, yellow, marked with crimson; Manderin, yellow, rosy crimson; En l King, buff bright rose; Delicia, white, pink and maroon; King Edward, white, red and maroon; King Solomon, apricot, curiously marked crimson; Midas, orange huff, •suffused scarlet. Picotees.—These are quite distinct from the above, the flowers having edges of various colours without any bare or markings on the petals and are generally weak growers; the grasses not much unlike a "Pink" in habit, although some are strong growers. The flowers are .most beautiful and are quite large enough for bouquets and buttonholes. The following varieties are about the best of this - section, and no carnation- grower should be without a few :</p>
        <p>John Ruskin, yellow ground, light edge of rose; Exquisite, yellow ground, light edge of scarlet; Favourite, white ground, light edge of scarlet; Lady D. Galton, yellow ground, wire edge of pu'rple; Her Majesty, yellow ground, heavy edge of purple; Lavinia, white ground, wire edge of purple; Margaret, white ground, heavy edge of purple; Mark Twain, yellow ground, medium edge of rose; Daniel Defoe, yellow ground, heavy edge of rose; Togo, yel- low ground, heavy edge of crimson; M. Dal- rymple apricot ground, wire edge of bright red; White Heather, white ground, heavy edge of scarlet.</p>
        <p>I find that A. and C. Taylor, carnation specialists, Somercotes, Derbyshire, send some remarkably fine strong plants out, and are very trustworthy to leave the selection of customers' wants to, and are very reasonable in xice,_</p>
        <p>CEVO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>AN EXCITING JOURNEY.</head>
        <p>AN EXCITING JOURNEY.</p>
        <p>Night Scuffle In A Railway Carriage Leads To Fines.</p>
        <p>WHAT THE WHISKY DID!</p>
        <p>The story of an exciting scene in a railway carriage was related at the Lymm Petty, Sessions on Wednesday morning, Ishen Frederick Wm. Berry, of 8, Birch-avenqe, Brooklands, Sale; Joseph Curran, of 11, Bank-street, Wharfe-road, Sale; James Henry Wright, of Thompson-street, Wellington-F1 reet, Manchester; and Wm. Lomas, of 10, Braddon- street, Clayton, Manchester, were si, mmoned for disorderly behaviour in a railway carriage, to the annoyance of railway officials The defendants are all engaged on Government work.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. IL Swallow (for Mr. Arthur .3rywne) prosecuted on behalf of the L. and N.-W. Rail- way Co.)</p>
        <p>. • The inagistrates on the Bench were Messrs. A Watkin (presiding), W. Peter Rylands, J. Coxon, A. W. Callender and R. H. Gotobed. Berry, Wright and Lomas pleaded not guilty, and Curran "guilty to certain charges." • Heard The Men. Quarrelling.</p>
        <p>Outlining the facts of the case, Mr. Swallow said that the offence was committed in contra- vention of the -bylaws of the L. and N.-W. Rail- way Co. On August 14th the guard of the last train from Lime-street Station, Liverpool, noticed, at Heatley, that the men were quarrel- ling and saw them get in a first class compart- ment. On arriving at Dunham Massey, the guard, hearing a noise, opened a carriage door and found the men fighting in the compartment. One of the men was on the floor and the others were on top of him. He asked what they were doing in a first class compartment. The stationmaster was called and the two Manches- ter men were taken into the guard's van. The other two were ordered to get into a third class compartment which was empty. They were got in and a signalman locked the door. The two defendants, Berry and Curran, commenced to struggle again, ad Curran climbed out of the window. He went to the stationmaster and threatened to strike him, but was prevented from doing so by the signalman. When the train was starting he climbed back through the carriage window and went on with his friend. Had Him By The Throat.</p>
        <p>Samuel Stubbs, a railway brakesman, of War- rington, stated that when he opened the carriage door he found that three of the defendants had "the little man" by the throat In answer -to the Chairman, the witness said the four men were not sober. Mr. Luke Wood, the stationmaster at Dun- ham Massey, said that on the ,night in question, when the 10.12 from Liverpool had arrived, he heard a disturbance and went to a first class compartment. As he passed along the train he heard brawling and shouting. He went to the first class compartment and saw the defendants. With the assistance of the guara he got the two men who seemed to be getting the worst of it out of the compartment, and took them into the guard's van. One of them was rather agitated and wanted to go back. Witness went back to the first class compartment, which was next but one to the van. He asked the other two defendants what tickets they had and there was no response. Berry went into a third class car- riage without any trouble, tut the. other defendant would not until he was forced to. A signalman locked the. door and Berry did his best to keep; Curran in the third class compart- ment. Curran, however, jumped straight through the window which was open and walked. across the platform to get hold of witness. Curran made two attempts to strike the witness, but the signalman sttod between, them. When the train was about 200 yards out the communication cord was pulled and it was stopped.</p>
        <p>Questioning the witness, Curran suggested ,that he was excited because he was put in a different compartment to the one that Berry was in. Ile also said that his state of health would not have permitted him to climb through the window as stated.</p>
        <p>Climbed Out Of Carriage Window.</p>
        <p>Richard Sandbach,. signalman at Dunhara Massey, said that after Curran had climbed out of the window he seized the stationmaste.x-hY_ the collar and said that he would not get back. into the compartment until he was ready.</p>
        <p>What The Defendants Said.</p>
        <p>In his defence, Berry said that he and Curran, arrived at Heatley Station at a few minutes, past 10 o'clock. They went to get their tick..fets" and saw a girl who appeared to be ill. Ple (Berry) offered her a drink of soda water. Wright seerbed to be a bit quarrelsome and asked for a .drink. He refused to give it hini- Soon afterwards the train came into the station- He (defendant) opened a carriage door without thinking which class it was. The other twr) defendants, who had been wrestling on the plat- form, followed him into the compartment. They all sat down talking. He was asked for a drink again, and mixed a drop of whisky with the soda water, and had a drink first. Curran next had a drink, and then this other fellow took a pull And emptied the bottle. The last drinker said that he had been drugged, and lifted the bottle up to strike witness. Two of the men were taken out and the carriage door was locked.</p>
        <p>Curran said that it was no use him saying anything as "it was on the same principle." Wright stated that on August 14th he went to Heatley. He, with Lomas, went into the waiting-room at the station. He was having a bit of fun and was asked to have a drink of soda water. He would not have a drink at first, and when be did he found that it was whisky and soda. He and his companions had to rush to a compartment and he did not notice that it was a first class one. He told his friend that he had had some whisky, whereupon the other chap jumped up and put him on the floor and nearly strangled him. He was taken out and put in the guard's van. He had only had two I" beer-shandies " prior to the whisky. . Lomas had nothing to say. The Penaltips.</p>
        <p>After consulting with his colleagues, the Chairman said that the case was a serious one. Curran was distinctly the worst, and the fines wniild he as follows —Curran, 20s. and 6s. 3cl‘ costs; Wright ,158. and 6s. 3d. costs; Berry, 10s, and 6s. 3d. costs; Lomas, 10s. and 6s. 3d. costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>AUTUMN CHEST PERILS.</head>
        <p>AUTUMN CHEST PERILS.</p>
        <p>An Up-10-Date Treatment for Coughs,.</p>
        <p>Colds, and Bronchilis.</p>
        <p>Most people have good reason to dread the arrival of Autumn with its chilly nights, and damp, clammy air. There is not only the Usual crop Of bad colds, coughs, tender throats, or sore chests to contend with, but the danger of bronchitis, pleurisy, or pneumonia is also, present to-day. Breathe-able Peps, however, rob the Weather of its terrors. With a 'box of Peps always, ,handy you may defy coughs, colds, and bron- chitis even on the most chilly or changeable days.</p>
        <p>You take a Peps tablet out of the silver jacket in which it is sealea, and place it on tho , tongue. As the tablet dissolves, powerful heal- ing, strengthening, and germ-destroying fumes. arise, and are breathed direct into the throat, brochial tubes, and lungs. In this unique faMion the throat and chest are cleared of phlegm and other impurities; lurking germs of disease are destroyed; the membranes, injured and inflamed by incessant coughing, are soothed and healed,, and all the, breathing -passages strengthened and toned-up. The Uselessness of liquid remedies for lung troubles is obvious to all, since it is quite impossible to swallow a liquid into the lung All liquids pass straight into the stomach, and most old-fashioned cough mixtures and cough- cures are dangerously loaded with °pint°, chloral, "morphia, and other narcotics and seda- tives. Peps are entirely free from these drugs, and are safe alike for young and old. The Peps method of compressing a breathe- able medicine into solid tablet form is ,the latest triumph of modern science. It realis°, the aim of •many great specialists in throat an lung troubles. Peps are praised everywhere by people wIlf! have proved their wonderful power in cases et chronic cough, colds, bronchitis, asthma, influ: enza, sore throat, old-age cough, bronchial asthma, difficult breathing, tightness of til; chest, laryngitis, chest weakness, factory an(' workshop coughs, children's coughs, wheeziness* and other. throat and chest ailments.</p>
        <p>F</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <p>WONDERFUL HEALING METHODS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Testimony From The Trenches.</head>
        <p>Testimony From The Trenches.</p>
        <p>SAVING BOTH SOLDIER AND WORKER. It has needed the War to make known the facilities now in existence for the rapid and clean healing of wounds. While in the Boer War there were thousands more deaths from disease than from bullets, it has been a significant fact that in France dur- ing the present war this class of mortality has been wonderfully low: This is due largely to our greater knowledge .51 antiseptic healing. • The men in the trenches have also borne testimony over and over again to the priceless aid they have received from the handy box of Zam-Buk with which many com- panies are now equipped. Cuts from barbed wire, superficial wounds from flying shell frag- ments and innumerable other injuries have been treated promptly with Zam-Buk, and so success- fully that ever-feared blood-poison has been warded off. The men have continued with their trench warfare wearing their Zam-Buk bandages, and thus safeguarded, have avoided the unpleasant necessity of "reporting sick." Many Officers, Non-Coms. and men, including Corporal Upton, V.C., Private Robon, V.C., Private Acton, V.C., several D.S.O.'s, and scores of Distinguished Conduct Medallists, have stated that to Zam-Buk alone belongs the credit of healing their wounds and keeping them in the fighting line. War conditions impose the severest test on the efficiency of healing preparations, and the unvarying success of Zam-Buk in France, Servia, Italy, and in the Dardanelles, proves that this rare balm must possess an extraordinarily high value.</p>
        <p>The exceptional value of Zam-Buk can be very simply explained. It is a highly concen- trated balm, purely vegetable in origin, and unique in character, healing swiftly even obstinate wounds, banishing sores, and literally growing new healthy skin. Zam-Buk Is perfectly free from lard and all such animal fats, and equally free from injurious minerals like, com- pounds of lead, zinc and quicksilver. It sets out to heal injuries and cure skin diseases- in a new and better way than was ever done by old methods. The fact that Zam-Buk is a house- hold name throughout the World is proof of its wonderful success.</p>
        <p>No other preparation possesses the same splendid healing, soothing, and antiseptic pro- perties. Zam-Buk 'has-been aptly called "A Surgery in a Two-inch Box." It is certainly indispensable in every home, for every day brings its crop of unexpected mishaps. Never be without you r box of Zara-Buk. Sold only in sealed C re,•ll packages, of all chemists and stores.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>VETERINARY ZAM-ELJK</head>
        <p>A VETERINARY ZAM-ELJK</p>
        <p>There is now to be 'obtained in a special fled package a form of Zam-Buk adapted for use upon the common cuts and sores of horses, cattlb, sheep, dogs and pets. It embodies the same powerful healing properties of the original or household Zam-Buk. A tin proves an invaluable Stable Companion and is the farmer's ideal first-aid. Immediate resort to Veterinary Zam-Buk (sold at Mk and 2/9 by chemists, saddlers and medicine dealers) saves suffering And reduces the regular " Vet " bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 23, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>a/m.lw : JO, ISTR</p>
        <p>Welcome Field Card.—A field card has reached Mrs. Percival from her brother-in-law, Pte. Sam Percival, Newfields, Lymm—who is serving with the Cheshires—to say that he is well, and that he has received letters which have been sent to him.</p>
        <p>National Registration.—The work in connec- tion with the Blue Forms of National Regis- tration is being carried on the whole of this week at the Assembly Room in the Conserva- tive Club, in charge of Mr. F. E. Evans, chemist, The Cross, Lymm. A complete can- vass of the village took place on Wednesday, Thursday and Friday last, and it is satisfac- tory to note that excellent results have been obtained. •</p>
        <p>IYMM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>CITIZEN SUNDAI.</head>
        <p>CITIZEN SUNDAI.</p>
        <p>Annual Church Parade.</p>
        <p>The ninth annual church parade promoted by the committee of the Tractesmen's Associa- tion took place on Sunday afternoon at the Parish Church. There was a very large attend- ance. At the head of the procession was the Lymm Subscription Band, under the direction of Mr. J. "Neigle. Among those at the service were the Hon. John E. Cross, Vice-Chancellor Dudley Stewart-Smith, and Messrs. A. Watkin, John Coxon, A. W. Callender, W. Peter Rylands, S. W. Gould, T. J. Ridgway, magis- trates; Mr. H. L. Harris, clerk to the magis- trates; Messrs. R. H. Gotobed (chairman), C. E. Cowper, F. Lockyer, S. Critchley, J. H. Holt, F. Ingham, W. G. Groves, T. H. War- burton and G. H. Parker, members' of the Urban District Council; W. Mullard; clerk; H. T. Mercer, relieving officer; Thomas F. W. Dixon, guardian; the Lymmi National Guard, under the direction of Mr. George R. William- son and Sergeant S. Aloston; the Oughtrington Platoon; under Captain W. B. S. Hawkins, R.E., and Sergeant T. H. Peters, the Rev. G. S. Tudor-Evans, rector of Oughtrington; the Lymm, High Legh and Heatley Troops of the Boy Scouts, under Scoutmasters C. W. W. Surridge and T. Hatton; members of the Odd- fellows' Society (M.U.), Bros. Herbert White- legg (secretary) and Alin Whitlow (treasurer); Foresters, Bros. William Holt (secretary) and Henry Moston.</p>
        <p>The congregation also included Corporal W. Hirst and Lance-Corporals Fred Kay and Reg. Hillier, wounded Lymm men home from the Front. The Rev. G. B. Thurston (rector) and the Rev. Horace Gray were present, in addition to the special preacher for the day. The arrangements at the church were made by Messrs. F. E. Evans. and W. M. Beckett (wardens) and Henry Parsons and J. T. Milne (sidesmen), and for the procession by Messrs. David Daniels (president of the Tradesmen's Association), W: E. Hillier (hon. secretary) and Alfred Richardson (hon. treasurer). Nation's Duty to God.</p>
        <p>The sermon by the Rev. J. P. Cort, of Sale, was preached from the words: "A citizen of no mean city." St. Paul, said the reverend gentleman, always remembered that he was a citizen of Imperial Rothe and was 'impressed by a great ideal of citizenship. We, as Chris- tians, Must bring our individual lives under government which was from God, and "render, therefore, unto Ctesar the things that are Ctesar's, and unto God the things that are God's." We were citizens, he said, of no mean State, but of an Empire the greatest the world had ever known. We were bound and united in a way we had never been before, the means of which were to carry us to a success- ful issue through the present war. It was our first duty to honour and reverence God, the Ruler of nations as well as individuals. It was for the follower of Christ to prove to the world that he was "a citizen of no mean city," and an active worker in every good and righteous cause for his fellow-men. Let them go to church resolved to carry out God's wishes, which would be for the betterment of the human race. .</p>
        <p>The offertory was in aid of the Nurses' Fund, the Sick and Poor Fund, and the Convalescent Home Fund, and realised £8 7s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>"OUR DAY" CONCERT. Large Audience Enjoys Splendid Programme.</head>
        <p>"OUR DAY" CONCERT. Large Audience Enjoys Splendid Programme.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED WARRIORS WELCOMED.</p>
        <p>A concert, promoted by the Working Men's Committee of the British Red Cross Society (Lymm Detachment), was held at the Drill Hall, Lymm, on Wednesday evening, in aid of the "Our Day" effort. The hall was crowded, and the concert proved to be of a standard worthy ofH the cause. The artistes who gave their services for the evening included iiNss .Ada McHugh (Manchester), Madame Sara Silcock, -Mr. William Booth, Messrs. J. Blackburn and F Rogan (cornet soloists), the Brooklyn Quartet (Madame Sara Silcock, Madame Eckersley, Mr. John Sephton and Mr. Dutton), Crosfield's Prize Band (conducted by Mr. W. Halliwell), and the ! Warrington Male Voice Cioir (conducted by 1 Mr. J. H. Brazendale). The programme consisted of Reins which gave both musicians and soloists plenty of oppor- tunity for showing their ability. One item, which was not on the programme, proved to be exceedingly popular, and it took the form of a number of popular selections by Crosfield's Band. Both old and new selections were in- cluded, and at the end of the piece a number of selections had to be repeated in response to the incessant applause of the audience. Other selections by the band included "Imperial Echoes," a spirited march by Smith; Meyer- beer's "L' Etoile du Nord," the soloist being Mr. F. Rogan; Flotow's overture to "Martha," and the "Beauties of England," by Newton. Mr. J. Blackburn gave an admirable rendition on the cornet of the solo "Skylark." The items by the Brooklyn Quartet were sung in a manner that audiences have been led to expect from such talented artistes. Their first item was "In this hour of softened, splendour" (Pinsuti), and later in the evening they sang •" A regular Royal Queen," from Sullivan's "Gondoliers. In addition to these, Madame Eckersley and Mr. John Sephton rendered the duets "I heard a voice" (Stephen Glover) and "Watchman, what of the night?" (Sargeant). Madame Sara Silcock gave a delightful rendition kif "Angus Macdonald' (Joseph F. Roeckel), t:.-!oring a great success. As an encore she sarg "An my garden." Later she gave "Down in the forest" (Landon Ronald) and- " A birthday" (Frederick H. Cowen). Mr. Wm- Booth gave but one solo, but scored a distinct triumph, his splendid Voice being heard to great advantage. His selection was Wallace's "Yes, let me like a soldier fall," and it was heartily ;Appla uded and, in response to a vociferous recall, he gave " Two eyes of grey." The other soloist, Miss Ada Mr•llng,h, was every whit as "successful as her fellow artistes, her songs being "When you come home" and "All joy be thine" (Wilfrel Sanderson). The Warrington Male Voice Cheir sang a number of items in splendid fashion. These included "On the ramparts" (A. Saintis), "The destruc- tion of Gaza" (Laurento de Rule), "Zeus, Lord of Heaven" (Granville Bantock), "When even- ing's twilight" (J. L. Hatton), a descriptive chorus, "Crossing the plain" (T. Maldwyn l'rice), "Far beyond all mortal ken" (W. G. Rothery), and "The long day closes" (Sulli- van).</p>
        <p>The concert was chiefly organised by Mr. A. H. Hudson, supported by the following com- mittee: Messrs. K. N. Tweedale (chairman), H. E. Odber (vice-chairman), Owen Davis, G. Rixon, J. Wilson, J. Walker, jun., 11. Thomason, T. Redditt, A. Sutton, C. Leather, IL Clare, A. Appleton, W. Seymour, D. Bradblirn, A. Williams, A. Halliday, E. Simpson and H. Hesketh.</p>
        <p>Four little girls—Miss Odber, Miss Seymour, Miss Rion and Miss Hudson—sold Red Cross Flags in the hall. The plants for the . stage decoration were lent by Mr. T. Horton, of West Leigh. • Included in the large audience were 21 wounded soldiers from the Lymm Red Cross Hospital, and these were given a hearty_3vekome on their entry:lt'll-umber of Belgian ladies were 41 present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>CORPORAL B. ELLIOTT.</head>
        <p>CORPORAL B. ELLIOTT.</p>
        <p>An Indescribable_ Sight During The</p>
        <p>Great Advance.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Benjamin Elliott, of Canterbury Cottage, Lymm, have received news that their son, Corporal B. Elliott, Scots Guards, was wounded in action on the 16th instant. In a letter to his sister, dated two days before he received his injuries, he states :</p>
        <p>_ "I am in the best of health and am resting in some shattered houses that have been smashed to atoms. We are again going into action, and as I write only a little distance away 'Jack Johnson's," Whiz-bangs ' and other projectiles are bursting, which cause the remnants of the houses to shake alarmingly. However, we don't mind them now as we are pretty used to it. We are as happy as can be, some are singing, whilst others are eating bully beef and biscuits. I shall never forget the day we went through the village of , it was a sight indescribable. lam proud of my regi- ment. Well, sister, there goes the 'fall in,' so we are in for another_ picnic."</p>
        <p>Corporal Elliott is '3-2 years of age and single, and left a good position to join H.M. Forces. He</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>"BEATEN ALL ALONG THE LINE."</head>
        <p>"BEATEN ALL ALONG THE LINE."</p>
        <p>Wounded Man's View Of Germany's Position.</p>
        <p>Private James Fraderick Percival, ef the Chesliii.e Regiment—who, as we preytously re- ported,. has- been wounded in France in the right foreEnger—hits written two letters to his wifl and children at Canal` Side, Grappenhall. The first letter; dated October 13th, and written from I. 4 Stationary Hospital; France, stated: "My finger' is getting all right again, and I am in the best' of' health, after a fortnight's rest— for I was' getting run down for want of s'leep and rest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>"Going On Fine Now."</head>
        <p>"Going On Fine Now."</p>
        <p>"I hope you are not upsetting yourself about me, as I am going on fine now, and will soon be back with the regiment." The second letter, dated October 15th, and written from the same hOspital, ran: "I am glad to tell you that I have not lost my finger, and it is getting all right again. .. . I was surprised to hear about ' Smack ' Booth being wounded. You can tell . . . . nat lam keeping my heart up, and hoping to be at home again soon, for the Germans are beaten all along the line."</p>
        <p>LIEUT. D. STEWART-SMITH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Viee-Chancellor's Wounded Son Home On Leave.</head>
        <p>Viee-Chancellor's Wounded Son Home On Leave.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Dudley. Stewart-Smith, of the Black Watch, and eldir son of Vise-Chancellor Stewart-Smith, K.C., of Lymm Hall, who par- ticipated in the great British advance, is now home on furlough for a• few days. It will be remembered that the gallant officer was wounded in the head, arm and hand,. as well as being gassed. He is progressing 'satisfac- torily, the only ill-effects, being that his eyes are affected in consequence of the fumes of poisonous gapes. Lieutenant Stewart-Smith was first educated at Winchester and now be- longs to University College, Oxford, as well as the Middle Temple. He held a commission in the Public Schools' and University Batta- lion, but subsequently transferred to the Black Watch in January, being drafted to France in May. Lieutenant D. Stewart-Smith has witnessed many stirring engagements and has played an active part for his King and country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>1:111.11 CORPORAL PROMOTED.</head>
        <p>1:111.11 CORPORAL PROMOTED.</p>
        <p>Mrs. G. Davies; whn.resides at Booth's Hill, Lymm, has this wvele- receiVed. news that her nephew, Corporal 4: H. 3fastbn, of the Cheshire Regiment, has been promoted to sergeant In a letter dated October ~18th he states:—</p>
        <p>"I am keeping very well just at present: We are 'having lovely weather, but it is very -cold at night when we go into till trenches, espe- cially in the early morn. I am pleased to inform you that I have been promoted to sergeant, which shows I am getting on fine."</p>
        <p>Or. De Jong and His Sons.—Dr. Edward Meyer De Jong recently left his work at Lymm to journey to Weeton Camp to act as medical officer in charge. Lieutenant Kenneth De Jong, who last winter joined His Majesty's Forces as a private, is at present acting as adjutant to his battalion and is "Somewhere in France." Dr. Oscar De Jong is now on his way to Malta on Army work. Private " Teddy " De Jong, the youngest son, who went out to the Dardanelles with the Austra- lians, is home on sick-leave for a short time.</p>
        <p>Motor Cyclist Fined.—At the Petty Ses- sions on Wednesday, Alfred Lilley, of 8, Victoria-road, New Brighton, pleaded guilty to riding a motor cycle without a rear-light, and not guilty to a charge of refusing to stop when requested by a police-officer.—Evidence substantiating the charge was given by Police- Sergeant Davies, P.C. Davis and P.C. Gaul.— The defendant maintained that he did not hear P.C. Davis blow his whistle as a signal that he should stop.—Defendant was fined 7s. 6d. on the Arst charge And given the benefit of the doubt on the second.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing October 25th : Wednesday, 27th October.—Parade. Fall in at Headquarters at 7.40 p.m. prompt. N.B.— Those members who have now been supplied with uniforms will parade in same on this occasion,</p>
        <p>Saturday, 30th October.--Company Drill. Fall in at Headquarters at 3.25 p.m. sharp. A third section has just been formed, and in- tending members should make early application for enrolment any time the Headquarters are open for drill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>Harvest Festival.—The harvest thanksgiving services were held in Winwick Church on Sun- day. A large congregation assembled in the evening including a number of the istaff from the Lord Derby War Hospital. The preacher at the morning service was the Rev. Mr. Thomas from Chester, and, in the evening the Rev. J. T. Owen. The services were conducted by the Rev. Canon Penrhyn. Special music was rendered by the choir, including the anthem "Praise the Lord, 0 Jerusalem." the solo being taken by Mr. W. Sweetlove. The church was very tastefully decorated with flowers, fruit and vegetables. The collections, amounting to about el 3. were devoted to providing comforts for the Navy,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <p>CORPORAL B. ELLIOTT. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>enlisted in November of last year, and was in training at the commencement of the war at Wellington Barracks, London. He was drafted to the Front on January 15th. He attended the Lymm Wesleyan Church, and is widely known and respected by many friends in the village. Corporal Elliott comes off a fine patriotic military family, for at present there are two brothers serving with the Colours, viz., Bom- bardier William Elliott, R.F.A., and Sergeant Otho Elliott; drill sergeant to the Dorsetshires. Two other brothers are on munition work. Bombardier Elliott saw service in the South African campaign, and was fortunate to come out of many engagements without a scratch. He was with Sir George White at Ladysmith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>DRIVER GEORGE LEIGH.</head>
        <p>DRIVER GEORGE LEIGH.</p>
        <p>Lymm Congregationalist Suffering</p>
        <p>From Dysentery At Lewisham.</p>
        <p>News has been received that Driver George Leigh, of 5, West Hyde, Lymm, is at present lying in the Military Hospital, Lewisham, suffering from dysentery. He• joined H.M. Forces in September of last year and was drafted out to Alexandria, Egypt, in March. A member of the A.S.C., he is widely known and esteemed in Lymm and the surrounding villages. He was 'educated at the Lymm Paro- chial School and attended the Congregational Church, Lymm. Taking a great interest in all branches of sport, particularly football, he was a member of the Lymm Association Football Club. Driver Leigh is 27 years of age and the sole support of an invalid mother. Writing to his sister a day or two ago, he states:— " At present I am not feeling very well, the change of climate affecting me, which hinders</p>
        <p>Regiment, who has been wounded in both legs by shrapnel, in the present campaign.</p>
        <p>Worth Ten Germans.</p>
        <p>Private James Booth, writing to a personal friend—Councillor Frederick Lockyer, of the Star Inn, Cross Brow, Statham—states:— " I shall never forget the charge as long as I live; the Germans kept coming on just like leaves on a tree. It was .cruel having to crawl over the many dead and wounded, but our orders were that we had to take the redoubt at all costs. Away we went. Talk about Hell, it wasn't in it! We got within 50 yards of them and in a twinkling I got hit. I don't remember much else, only that I crawled for a mile and a half to the dressing station and lying on a stretcher and them giving me a drink. • My trousers were all torn and I looked an awful sight. However, I soon hope to be right again and worth ten German soldiers in spite of my injuries."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
