      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>PRIVATE' H. WEBSTER.</p>
        <p>Missing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>ton Heath, is reported missing, and no tidings of his safety have been received since Septem- ber 23rd.</p>
        <p>In his last letter, dated as above, writing to his parents, he states: ",We are going into the trenches to-night, and it won't be for long. The weather over here is pretty good just now; which is fortunate, for this country is awful. Some- times we are up to the ankles in mud. I am at present in the best of health, and hope to so continue."</p>
        <p>Private Webster joined the Warrington Terri- torials in September of last ycr.r, and eventually asked for a transfer to the Scottish Borderers, which was granted a few weeks before the big charge. He was engaged as a bomb-thrower, and previous to enlisting was employed by Mr. John Taylor, greengrocer, Walton-road, Stock- ton Heath. He completed his 23rd birthday at the beginning of the present month. A member of the Stockton Heath R.F.C., he showed con- siderable ability in this particular class of sport. He attended St. Thomas' Church, and was widely known and respec7.ed by a host of admirers in the village. The first intimation of his being missing was received from Lance- Corporal Albert Harper, a particular friend and member of the same battalion. In a letter home he states that Harry did not answer the roll call and no trace of him has yet been found. Further news is anxiously awaited by his parents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>DROWNED.</head>
        <p>DROWNED.</p>
        <p>DRUMMER H. RICHARDSON</p>
        <p>X‘alton Gardener Went Down With The Royal Ed,warjj. e) •</p>
        <p>DRUMMER HAROLD RICHARDSON.</p>
        <p>Drowned</p>
      </div>
