      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>J. S. GARLAND,</head>
        <p>J. S. GARLAND,</p>
        <p>Forces in the Scottish Borderers, on September Ist, 1914, and was drafted to the Mediterranean on August Ist of this year. Previous to his enlisting he was employed in the capacity if gardener at Sir Gilbert Greenall's Walton estate, where he was much respected and esteemed. When his duties allowed him he at- tended St.. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, and was passionately fond of music and local musical festivals. Writing to his sister in his last letter received, dated August 12th, he states: "I am in the best of health. We must be nearing the end of our journey, for we have been sailing 10, days.. At present I don't know exactly where we are, but each day brings us :nearer to our destination to fight for those at home. I suppose Harry (his brother) is still motor driving. I wish I could drive one. Never :mind, I expect wp shall have great fun when we have got over this journey, SO dlieer up." A host of friends mourn his loss, and his parents have received many tributes of sym- pathy,. for which thanks are expressed.</p>
        <p>51, 53 &amp; 55, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A Contentious Matter.</p>
        <p>it was asked if lights could not be provided at certain specified places? - They all felt, and could not help sympathising 'with people who Made those appeals, for there was a certain amount of justice in them. But still, the power was practically taken out of the hands of the local Lighting Committee and, at the present time, they were working under military in- structions. That was the difficulty and would remain the difficulty whilst the existing Order was in force. With regard to the hoardings, about which so much had been heard, tliey would, no doubt, have noticed that these things had been removed, and the road had been placed at the entire disposal of the public without obstruction. The water-carriage sewerage ques- tion, which had been before them previously, could not be proceeded with as the Council could not borrow money for such a purpose.</p>
        <p>The other matter they had to dispose of at that meeting was to consider the following resolution:—" That the minute of the Parish Meeting held on 4th March, 1907, to the effect that Parish Meetings be held half-yearly, be rescinded." 'The Parish Councils' Act called for one meeting per year, and it was illegal to hold two. It was quite possible if anyone took notice of it that the Council might be sur- charged for the cost when more than one meet- ing was held. In the past, the meetings had been attended by so few that the Council thought it was not necessary to hold them more than once a year, but if there was a feeling that more meetings should be held, there were plenty of opportunities for calling them. Six ratepayers could make a request that a meeting should be held. The chairman could then pall a meeting, and so could two members of the Council. 'As past meetings had not been well attended, he took it that the ratepayers were perfectly satisfied with the way in which the affairs of the parish had been conducted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>KILLED.</head>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE. B. J. SAVAGE.</p>
        <p>Ir•Ounti. Soldier). Who Proved Ills A'Vorth-.•</p>
        <p>Mr..alid Mrs. T. .H. Savage, of 12, Whitefield- road, Stockton Heath, received information on</p>
        <p>Thursday evening that their son, Private 3enjamin J. Savage, of the Sherwood Foresters l'erritorials) had been killed in action. Mr. knd Mrs. Savage came to reside at Stockton</p>
        <p>Heath last August, and their son is a compara- tive stranger to the district. •He was 22 years of age at the time' of his deatE4- and had served in the Territorial Force for a number of years. He went to France on February 24th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>" He Died a Noble Death."</head>
        <p>" He Died a Noble Death."</p>
        <p>En a letter to Mrs. Savage, a comrade of Private Savage writes:—" It is my painftd duty :o have to write this letter to yorct: This morn- ng I had to go out and bury your son. my )al and I gave him a decent burial. The action n which he died will never be forgotten by :hose who were in it. He died a noble death, 'orcing the German trenches. "A Son To Be Well Prouir Of."</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Taylor, of the Sherwood Fores- ters, writes :—" You will see in the West Derby- shire local papers within the next few days, a letter from the Divisional Commander, saying that the North Midland Divisiirn has been.</p>
        <p>taking a prominent part in the recent fighting. Ile success which crowned its effOrts cost the lives of some of our best men, and regret to have to tell you that your son MI on this occasion—the 13th of th'e• month:.—.... Privates Oldfield and Taylor say they were standing in the trench at: one time during the operations, with Ben just round the corner. A heavy shell exploded near to them aid buried the 'tvio Taylor having to be sent to hospital. Ordfield said that he never saw .Ben again. It was W. Harrison, of Tidewell, who broke the news to</p>
        <p>us, he found him near the trench 'first-aid post,' where he had been carried by the stretcher-bearer. He was a son to fie well proud of—a most reliable man, who showed no fears, even if he felt them. " A Splendid Little Fellow."</p>
        <p>"lie never pleaded inability to do his work on account of his size, but rather did he take on tasks which would have-lieen set other men. in order that he might prove hi§ worth. I need say no more. You must well-know what a splendid little fellow he was. There is perhaps one occasion which stand's out in my memory more than any other, though I was not actually there myself it has often been referred tu by other officers of the compkny. It was the night that a mine went up ourtrench and which made things very difficult for a time How- ever he was completely master of himself, did tot lose his head, and was of ihPstimable value to his officers But this i§ onry one occasion. There were several others when he did equally sell. It was at putting out barhed wire—an cm:up:Won requiring not a little courage—that h.. .ePmed tc come into his own. You have my most uincere sympathies in your bereavemeht."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>FOR THE RED CROSS.</head>
        <p>FOR THE RED CROSS.</p>
        <p>Big Crowd At First-Rate " Our</p>
        <p>Day " Concert.</p>
        <p>IA concert in support of the "Our Day" move- ment was held in the Church Room, Stockton Heath, on Tuesday afternoon, and was exceed- 'iv well attended. Among the large audience : re" .Q.?? Timber of wounded- soldiers from several 4 co o.„, c,(Zross hospital's in the neighbour- hotid.. 'filAtayor and Milyoress of Warrington (Alderman am' Mrs. Peacock) were also present. Professional ill.b,tes: Provide Fine Programme.</p>
        <p>The items of t.e programme were provided by the Liverpool Repertory Commonwealth Co., assitted by the weltrknown local tenor, Mr. Jack Rabbitt, &amp;c. Songs were rendered by Mrs. Budd and Mr. Rabbitt. Mr. Harvey Adams, in his monologue "A fishy fishing story," was a dis- tinct success, as atso was Miss Ursula Hughes with her dances. Mrs. Robert Garnett dramatic- ally recited "The Hell of Soissons," and was heartily applauded for her efforts. Th 3 recita- tions "Butterflies," "I remember meeting ycu" (Herman Loho and "Laugh, and the, world laughs with you" (Ella Wheeler Wilcox), were well delivered by Miss Madge Mclntosh. The second half of the programme opened with a dramatic sketch entitled "Between -the Soup and' the Savoury "—by Gertrude E. Jenning3 - the dramatic person ce being: Maria (the f:ook), Miss Nina Henderson; Ada (the parlourmai.l), Miss Doris Lloyd; and Emily (the kitchen- maid), Miss Edith Smith. An 'excellent orchestra played a nuttiber of selections. The iirogramme concluded with the National Anthems of the Allies. Miss Clarice Newall was the accompanist. Warrington's Mayor Thanks Suburbanites.</p>
        <p>The Mayor of Warrington said that he had been asked, on behalf of the cominittee, to thank all the artistes who had given their time and talent for the entertainment of those present. That day was the anniversary of the opening of Raddon Court Red Cross Hospital, and every one felt grateful to that institution, Thelwall Heys, and The Oaklands, for their efforts to help tho wounded during this great war. Referring to his invitation to help the movement at Stockton Heath, his Worship said that whilst ndt Mayor of Stockton Heath, Stockton Heath might some day come to terms with Warrzngton and reap some advantage—the same as one always did when one ' made terms. lie compli- mented the committee on having or- ganised such a splendid. entertainment. _</p>
        <p>After the entertainment came tea in the Hall Cafe to the wounded soldiers and other visitors. The entertainment was arranged by a repre- sentative bical committee, presided over by Mrs. Segar Owen, and ably assisted by Mr. S. Wall- head.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>PARISH MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>PARISH MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Shall They Be Held Once Or Twice Year?</p>
        <p>AN ABRUPT ENDING.</p>
        <p>Though it was of comparatively short dura- tion, several " lively " passages marked the progress of the Parish Meeting, which was held at the Church Room, Stockton Heath, on Tues- day evening. Mr. Wm. Mt.i.ntyre was in the chair. Mr. E. Bradsha'w (clerk) and several Councillors were present, and there was a fairly good attendance of parishioners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>STREET LIGHTS.</head>
        <p>THE STREET LIGHTS.</p>
        <p>In opening the meeting, the Chairman said that of the various items which the Parish Council had had under discussion, the first in importance, perhaps, was the lighting of the place. For ,sonle time past complaints had been received from various residents in the village C021C2:11i27, the bad lighting of the streets and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WHERE THE LIGHTING ORDER COMES FRGIW.</head>
        <p>WHERE THE LIGHTING ORDER COMES FRGIW.</p>
        <p>Referring to th lighting of the district, Mr. Chas. Dukes asked if the Stockton Heath people were under Knutsford or were they under War- rington, for he understood that the instructions as to reduced lighting witlt regard to Warring- ton had been withdrawn.</p>
        <p>• The Chairman: We are under the Western Command. Mr. Dukes added that if they were out for economy, let them understand what they were doing, but if it was a military requirement that was another matter, and- he was in perfect agreement.</p>
        <p>The Chairman replied thnt the Order came from Chester.</p>
        <p>A ratepayer said that some districts appeared to be better lighted than others, and ',gave in- stances in support of his contention. He sug- gested a rearrangement of 'the lights at Egerton- street and Brackley-street. The Chairman said 'that that would be taken as a recommendation to the Lighting Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>Another ratepayer asked what was the cost of the meetings. The Chairman: About 30s. each meeting. Less Gas—Smaller Rates? . . • A question was asked as to whether in view of the reduction in lighting, there would be a reduction in the rates? (Laughter.) The Chairman: I cannot hold out hopes of a reduction in the rates. A ratepayer: And gas not being used! ' Another ratepayer: And evelything going Up! Referring to the suggestion to hold only one meeting a year, Mr. Dukes said that lately there had been little criticisM offered at the meetings. A ratepayer contended that at the last meet- ing the Church Room was closed within a quarter of an hour after the time the meeting was due to commence, and: consequently many people went away. The Chairman: The time of starting is o'clock. We can't sit here all night looking at each other. It was moved from the back of the room that the meetings be held tiViee a year, but the Chairman refused to accept this propositidn, saying that the mover had no power to put forward such -a motion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes pointed out that the Council wanted to brin,g the matter before the iketing as a test. If more than one meeting was desired, ways and means wOuld be found to call a meet- ing. The Council thought it a waste of time and energy to call a meeting when only four or Aye ratepayers turned up.</p>
        <p>• POORLY ATTENDED MEETINGS. The Chairman's proposition was supported by a ratepayer, who said that, taking into con- sideration the fact that the meetings had lately been badly attended and that there were ex- ceptional circumstances, he thought it would be advisable to discontinue the half-ydarly meeting, and only hold them when necessary. Another attempt was made to move an amendment that the meetings should be held twice a year, but the Chairman again ruled it - out of order. After further discussion the Chairman 'de- clared the motion carried. Several ratepayers protested that the motion had not been carried. The Chairman said that the meeting could not settle the matter. If the ratepayers made a request for a half-yearly meeting of the Parish Councillors would be very pleased to come and listen to what the ratepayers had to say. A ratepayer: I don't think we have had a proper hearing at all! Another ratepayer maintained that the motion had never been put, and another said that the ratepaygrs were being ignored. The Chairman thereupon left the chair and the meeting came to an end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
