      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY OCTOBER 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>UNTO THIS LAST!</p>
        <p>Between now and the end of November this nation is to show the mettle of which it is made. So far, the voluntary system, which is in, keeping with all our principles of freedom and ideals of the liberty, the glory of our national life, as well as the ultimate secret of our long and continuous success, has stood the test in marvellous fashion. Unprece- dented demands have been made upon our manhood, and unprecedented has been the response. Millions have flocked to the stan- dard voluntarily and in the true spirit of an unfettered democracy that sees the dangers lying ahead, and is willing to take a hand in saving itself and the institutions that exist with it side by side. But, handsome as the. response has been, more men are needed. So desperate has the enemy become that he is seeking to involve the whole world, and is so putting a greater and greater strain upon , our resources. That strain must be met. The Hun is. effectually held in the West and in the East, and if the Balkans diversion calls for further effort on the part of the Allies there must be a correspondingly greater call also upon the material at the disposal of the enemy. .This German dashing about does but prolong the regony. The result will be he same for an enemy that is surely, and not very slowly, being waited to breaking point. Early in the war Lord Kitchener told us that the enemy would deteriorate as we improved. That message to the nation was worth many divisions in the field. It has come literally true. The enemy is sorely pressed, and we want men now to press him finally to the earth. Lord Derpy has been appointed Recruiting-Officer-in-Chief to make a final appeal to the men in order to make assurance doubly- sure. .If the appeal is not successful, the appeal will bectme a demand. We feel sure, however, that the " appeal " will hold the field, and the " demand " will be un- necessary. The German military, system has bred .such a many-headed monster that Eng- lishmen may well dread the idea of dabbling with it even in the guise of compulsory national service. But it is absolutely neces-. sary that the Men shall be forthcoming, and it is the duty of every district to supply its full quota in accordance with its means. We have millions of men good and true to 'come forward, who, now they know the need, will not hesitate. We believe that Warrington and the district of which it is the centre will, as ever, do its share, and that the town will be able to say hereafter not only that it took a great hand in thrashing the German, but that it played its part in preventing the intro- duction of the thin end of the wedge of Ger- man militarism into this land of ours—and so in preserving it " the holy, the happy, and the gloriously free I"-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WOMEN TEACHERS WANTED.</head>
        <p>WOMEN TEACHERS WANTED.</p>
        <p>The Education Committee make an appeal to those married- women teachers, or others who for any reason have retired from the pro- fession, to return to duty in order to fill the places of the male assistants who have en- listed, or who will answer Lord Derby's latest call to arms. Whatever happens, the educa- tion of the rising generation must not be allowed to suffer. When we have beaten the German in the field we shall have to beat him in the workshop, and only a sound educa- tional foundation will avail us to that end. We feel sure that our womenfolk will come forward in force to support the fighting line. They will do so all the more readily in this case, because they know that, being experi- enced in the practical affairs of life, they will, though temporarily out of practice, be able to , give really valuable expert service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>"OVER AGE" BATTALION.</head>
        <p>"OVER AGE" BATTALION.</p>
        <p>Another advertisement which should call for respectful consideration is that which an- nounces a public meeting in the Town Hall next Wednesday evening to consider the ques- tion of the formation of a local battalion of the• Lancashire Regiment. The regiment, which has been officially recognised by the War Office, affords an opportunity to all men over military age, or otherwise unable to enlist, to serve their country. Mr. W. Peter Rylands has undertaken the duties of com- mandant of the Battalion, which is a good augury for the good response we expect to result from the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MUNITIONS AND DRINK RESTRICTIONS.</head>
        <p>MUNITIONS AND DRINK RESTRICTIONS.</p>
        <p>Should Action Be Taken In Warrington ?</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE DECISION.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am glad to see that the County Jus- tices at linutsford Quarter Sessions decided practically unanimously last Monday, to peti- tion the Central Munitions Board to apply their powers ,of •reetriction of the hours for .sale •of liquor to the whole- of the administrative county.. Mr. Sidebotham gave excellent reasons in sup- port of his resolution, and was met with very feeble opposition from Colonel Alan Sykes, M.P., who has developed a carious affection for "local option "—.-alleging that the action .of Quarter Sessions, if the resolution was carried, 'would be to override the powers of the Petty Sessions justices. Why cannot the Warrington justices follow suit? There are a very large number of works in Warrington engaged on "munitions." The vast majority of the workmen are ideal in their behaviour and in their sense of responsibility, but there is-a minority—a small minority—who require strong measures to keep them true to their obligations and duties. It is, a pitiful sight to notice the amount of obviously excessive drinking which is going on in our midst-rthe greatest offenders are .undoubtedly elderly men, and not a few of them wear the uniform. To 'their infinite credit let it be said that our young soldiers,, in their general conduct, and especially in regard to intoxicating drink, are an example to their older comrades. • In this connection is it not astonishing to notice - the number of appeals against their assessments 'which have been lodged by liquor traffickers quite recently? While there may be an isolated case or two, does any man believe for a moment that there is any less bulk con- sumption of intoxicants now than was the case before the war? There may be less consump- tion on some licensed premises, but it is notorious that the "jug and bottle" trade has greatly increased. How much Jess beer is being brewed now than was delivered a year ago? Are the brewers in our locality less busy—is it not a fact that, without exception, all the local , breweries are on full output? If this is the ease, where does the beer go to if not to the licence-holders, and through them to the public? We are living in perilous times, nn.i it appears that even yet in spite of a year of war, with 'all its sad experiences, we are a long way from I that adequate personal and individual realisa- tion of duty and true citizenship which is:neces- sary for our complete success and without \Aid" all our efforts may prove unavailing. Surely our Chief Magistrate is prepared to give a "lead "on this important question.—Yours. &amp;c.. CITIZEN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 23, 1915. SIR PETER WALKER DEAD.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 23, 1915. SIR PETER WALKER DEAD.</p>
        <p>Head Of Great Brewery Firm Succumbs To Long Illness.</p>
        <p>Sir Peter Carlaw Walker, Bart., of Osmaston Manor, near Ashbourne, Derbyshire, and head of the great brewery firm of Peter Walker and Son, Warrington and Burton, Ltd., died, after a long 'illness, on Monday, at his beautiful Derbyshire home, at the age of 61. In his younger days Sir Peter was a fioted</p>
        <p>hunter of big game, in pursuit of which he visited America, India, and the Pacific Islands. His varied experience included the contracting of yellow fever, from which he nearly died; the capture of a wapiti, a rare species of deer, which he succeeded in acclimatising and per- petuating at Osmaston; the destruction of an enormous elephant, which the natives rejoiced to see put out of the way; and the capture or a tarpon, which weighed 1951 b. Among his other recreations were yachtiiig, hunting, and the Turf:, In 1901 he was elected a member of the National Hunt Committee. He never owned any horses of note, but pos- sessed some useful jumpers. Like his brothers, Colonel Hall Walker, M.P. for the Widnes Division, and Colonel John Reid Walker, he figured in racing, particularly steeplechasing. He won many races in this department of sport, the chief events including the Lancashire</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NOW OR NEVER.</head>
        <p>NOW OR NEVER.</p>
        <p>GREAT FINAL RECRUITING EFFORT</p>
        <p>TO SAVE VOLUNTARYISIVI.</p>
        <p>All Eligible Men To Be Canvassed.</p>
        <p>During the next six weeks the Voluntary Military system of this country will be put upon its final trial. For over a year appeals have been made for recruits, and those recruits, have, up to recently, come forward in satisfac- tory volume. So patriotic have been the men of the 'land' that niillions have 'joined the colours where hundreds of thousands were asked for; but the war has made unexpected developments, and there is need for men and more men to complete the work of ridding civilisation of the blight which has been wafted over it from Prussia. Thanks to the organisa- tion and the enthusiastic patriotism of the workers, taken as a whole, the munitions and equipment problem has been solved. We hive put forces in the field with the necessary supplies that are competent to wear down the enemy. What is now wanted is an irresistible supply of reserves to complete the job thoroughly by following up our successes by conclusive knock-out brows. For nothing but a real knock-out will suffice in the present .case.</p>
        <p>It is absolutely necessary that every eligible man should be at the service of the State; and Lord Derby, into whose hands the task of recruiting has been placed; has evolved a plan in "a final appeal to the people to recruit for the people." He hints that if failure attends this appeal other methods will have to be adopted to bring men to the colours. Up to now the military authorities, with certain auxiliary assistance, have been respon- sible for recruiting. It is not intended to inter- fere with the work of any existing organisation; but to co-ordinate the work of all and,so save</p>
        <p>the overlapping that has taken place and, by a systematic canvass of the men whom the National RegisWr has shown to be ,available, also save the potential . soldier from the annoyance of being canvassed by several differ- ent parties. Another es3ential feature of Lord Derby's scheme is to make civilians responsible for bringing the raw,matOrial in the shape of a recruit to the military authorities for them to enlist, clothe, equip, and train.</p>
        <p>Deputy-Mayor (Dr. Joseph), Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., C.V.0., Sir Arthur H. Crosfield, Bart., Mr. W. Peter Rylands, Mr. C. E. Parker, Mr. J. Fulton- Smith, Alderman Tinnion, Councillor Wilkin- son; Councillor Locker, Mr. George H. Smith. Mr. E. Bradshaw, and the Town Clerk' (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), .together with the two. political agents (Messrs. E. A. Sanders and H. Hayes) as joint secretaries. The Labour party will also be represented on the Committee. • -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LORD DERBY'S SCHEME.</head>
        <p>LORD DERBY'S SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The following are the main points of the scheme outlined by Lord Derby, which he described as "the last effort on behalf of voluntary service" : Every existing recruiting body will be made use of in the scheme, which will be carried out by the aid of the National Register. Everybody who recognises that the State has a right to call on his services should enlist, and those found to be fit will be attested ' there and then. Those who wish to join the Colours at once will be allowed to do so; the rest will go on with their usual vocations and will be sub- ject to be called up only when required. Men who have enlisted will be divided into 46 groups, the unmarried men in the first ffl groups according to age, and the married also in 23 groups according to age, and beginning when the unmarried groups are exhausted. These men will be called out in successive groups as required, a fortnight's notice being given to each before he joins. Appeals may be made to be put in a later group; men thus allowed on leave will draw no military pay. If every man liable to be called on will join, it is hoped that it will be many months before the older married men are called on at all; it inay even be that the war will be decided before it is necessary. Pay and allowances at the rate of 3s. a day will be granted to men who are prepared to join at once but are allowed to go to their homes after being attested. They will be subject to 24 hours' notice before being called up. "Starred" men who enlist will be attested, to be called on if wanted, but will be sent back at once to their employment. Un- starred men found to be indispensable in their employments may also be sent back. It may be found that even in "starred" trades there are portions in which men can be liberated from their employment. All men will be enlisted for general service, and no promise can be made to allot them to any particular branch., Preference for the Army Service Corps and the Royal Army Medical Corps will be given to mar- ried men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>CLASSIFICATION.</head>
        <p>THE CLASSIFICATION.</p>
        <p>Men who have enlisted will be divided into 46 groups,. the unmarried men in the first 23 groups according to age, and the married also in 23 groups, according to age. Each group of 23 will be made up as follows: Class. Age. Class Age. 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 . ..?7 21 10 11 12</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>HELPERS WANTED.</head>
        <p>HELPERS WANTED.</p>
        <p>Canvassers, Workers And Cars Urgently Needed.</p>
        <p>The local committee under the scheme has been appointed and met on Wednesday to set the work in motion. The Corporation has placed the central premises, 3, Horsemarket- street, at their disposal, arid operations will begin in earnest on Monday. Lord Derby makes use of all existing recruit- ing committees, and whether they bear the name of Parliamentary Recruiting Committee or not, asks them to undertake .in their, .varions loCilities the duties-,which, in areas where a Parliamentary Recruiting Committee exists, would be undertaken by that body. The existing committees are asked to avail them- selves of the services of the political agents of all parties. In some cases these gentlemen are already honorary, secretaries to the recruiting committees, in 'others his Lordship hopes they will be ct;7-k.pted on to. the committees.: _ _ • _ _</p>
        <p>To these -committees will be entrusted the work of canvassing, and it will be readily seen how useful the political agents will be in this respect. In Warrington Messrs. Herbert Hayes and Ernest Sanders have entered, upon their labours with enthusiasm and -.confidence. They appeal for volunteers to carry out the canvass, locally, the organisation of 'which they will supervise. We understand that in _Warrington, when the munition workers and other ineligibles have been deducted, there are. still 5,000 of military age available, and these will be canvassed. In the work of approaching these men lies the chance of every man, not of military age, and of every woman of doing something for the common cause. Those who usually canvass at election times and are not eligible for the ranks will be, or ought to be, among the first to volunteer as canvassers. It is hoped that well- read men and men of influence in the town will come forward in large numbers to assist not only with the actual work of canvassing, but by giving instrilction and advice. The loan of motors to assist ,in canvassing and in bringing up recruits, as voters are brought up to the poll, will be greatly. appre- ciated.</p>
        <p>It is hoped that ministers of religion, the heads of Sunday schools, the managers of all places of amusement, and others who have like facilities, will, during the next six weeks, make appeals to their congregations, scholars and audiences to do all they can to provide the necessary recruits to save our voluntary system.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>LOCAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE LOCAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Mayor of Warrington received a letter from Lord Derby inviting his co-operation in carrying the scheme into effect, and the follow- ing committee, which met on Wednesday at the Town Hall to make preliminary arranges meats, was appointed The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PROCEDURE.</head>
        <p>THE PROCEDURE.</p>
        <p>Two sets of cards—blue and white—duly filled in, will be supplied to the committee.</p>
        <p>The white cards are for use in the offisce as a register of results and must be returned to the Area Commander when the work iA completed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>XVILL YOU RESPOND TO THE CALL?</head>
        <p>XVILL YOU RESPOND TO THE CALL?</p>
        <p>The blue cards are for canvassing. 'Upon the back of each card will be written the name and address of the canvasser to whom it iS entrusted. A letter from the Director-General of Recruiting will be posted to every man whose name is upon the .unstarred blue cards in order that the man may receive it before he is canvassed.</p>
        <p>This letter, .-which has already begun to arrive at the homes of men in Warrington, says : . _</p>
        <p>At my request the Parliamentary Recruit- ing Committee, representing all political parties working in conjunction with the Joint Labour Recruiting Committee, are</p>
        <p>organising a great recruiting campaign to induce men who can be spared to come forward voluntarily for service in the Army. If this effort does not succeed the country will know that everything possible will have been done to make the voluntary system i) success and Will have to decide by what method sufficient recruits can be obtained to maintain our armies in the field at the required strength. , Mr. Asquith has pledged this country to support Our Allies to the' fullest extent of our power. It was a pledge given on behalf of the "lotion and endorsed by all parties. Every man of military age and fitness must equally bear his share in redeeming it. May I, as Director-General of Recruiting, beg you to consider your own position? Ask yourself whether, in a country fighting as ours for its very existence, you are doing all you can for its safety, and whether the reason you have hitherto held valid as one, for not enlisting holds good at the present crisis. Lord Kitchener wants every man he can get. Will you not be one of those who respond to their country's call? DERBY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>LABOUR SUPPORT.</head>
        <p>LABOUR SUPPORT.</p>
        <p>How The Voluntary Principle May Be</p>
        <p>Vindicated.</p>
        <p>The Joint Labour Recruiting Committee have issued an appeal, to free men, signed on behalf of the three National Committees as follows: H. Gosling and C. W. Bowerman (Trades Union Congress Parliamentary Committee), J. O'Grady and W. A. Appleton (General. Federation of Trade Unions), W. S. Sanders and G. J. Wardle (Labour party Executive). "Under the voluntary system we' have raised great armies to oppose the aggressor:, and we can point proudly to their achievements. "We are satisfied, however, that if the volun- tary principle is to be vindicated, at least 30,000 recruits per week must be raised to maintain the efficiency of our Armies, and secure such a /vie- tory as will free the world from the fear of that military tyranny which Germany would impose upon it.</p>
        <p>"There are in the country at the present moment tens of thousands of men of military age and fitness who have not yet joined the colours, and for whom equipment and munitions are ready. "We appeal to them to take up at once their share of the burden, not only because by so doing they will be defending their own inter- ests, but also because their action will preserve the vital interests of the nation."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WHAT ABOUT YOU?</head>
        <p>WHAT ABOUT YOU?</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Bradbury Asks Yo To Fill Up The Banks.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal John Wm. Bradbury, of the machine-gun section of the 2nd South Lanca- shires, who has been at the Front over twelve months, in a battalion that has been in the thick of the fray from the beginning of the war, is the writer of the following lines. He is the son of Mr. John William Bradbury, 28, West-street, an ironworker at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. Mr. Bradbury has another son in the Royal Horse Artillery.</p>
        <p>WHAT ABOUT YOU? In a little churchyard back o' the line</p>
        <p>Yet one more to rest we have laid, And soon another widow will pin For one who the price has pail:</p>
        <p>The price of freedom for those and you His duty to them and you; His duty to King and Cod above Has been done them—What About You?</p>
        <p>A'int you coming to fill up the space He has left in the long, long, line: To do your bit and take his place In the struggle to reach the Rhine ?</p>
        <p>Or, do you say, "If the country need Me, let it call," thou Cain, Its call as every hour speed With the new-spilled blood Of its slain?</p>
        <p>Ask yourself, and clinch your fist Ask, and go get your gun: Would you like to see your sister kissed • By a dirty, drunken Hun?</p>
        <p>Would you like to-morrow to see your home Burning 'mid many more? Would you like to see your mother roam Homeless, and heartsore?</p>
        <p>It's happeneZ already, too often alas! And if all were the same as you T'wbuld have happened in England—but let that pass— Say, What About You? •</p>
        <p>Photograph by Birtles. THE LATE SIR PETER.</p>
        <p>Steeplechase, the Middlesex Hurdle at Kempton Park, the Juvenile Steeplechase National Hunt at Warwick, and the Great Staffordshire Steeple- chase. He still kept a number of jumpers in training, of which, perhaps, the best known is Lord Ninian. For many years he hunted with the Meynell and with the Cheshire Hounds, Lady Walker also being devoted to this sport. For several seasons Sir Peter was master of the Dove Valley. Harriers. He was at one time a captain in the Duke of Lancaster's Yeomanry and afterwards lieuten- ant-colonel commanding and hon. colonel of the Derbyshire Yeomanry. In 1896-7 he was High Sheriff of Lancashire, and he was a deputy- lieutenant for Derbyshire and Lancashire. Sir Peter was born in 1854, the eldest son of Sir Andrew Barclay Walker, first baronet, by his marriage with the eldest daughter of John Reid, of Limekilns, Fifeshire, and succeeded his father both to the title and the chairmanship of the company in 1893. Re married in 1899 Ethel Blanche, the daughter of Mr. 11. C. Okeover, of Okeover, Ashbourne, a granddaughter of the third Lord Waterpark and a younger sister of Sir Andrew Walker's second wife, and leaves a son and a daughter. He is succeeded by his son, lan Peter Andrew Monro, who was born in 1902, and will be thirteen next month. GIFTS TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Sir Peter served his apprenticeship in the King-street Brewery, Warrington, and he and his family have always taken a deep interest in the town. Besides giving the large fountain in front of the Town Hall at the Sankey-street entrance to the Park, grounds, they have placed at the disposal of the Town Council two separate sums of £l,OOO each, to be used for "general purposes" as occasion might require. The first £l,OOO was given by Sir Peter's grand- father, and the late Baronet, hearing from the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) that that .sum had been exhausted,, wrote on behalf of his family asking the town's ,acceptance of the second, £l,OOO "for the sake of old and present associations." The inscription on the fountain is fully explanatory of the gift : " Presented to the town by Sir Peter Carlaw Walker, Bart., and other members of the Walker family in memory of, their grandfather, the late Mr. Peter Walker, who was for many years connected with the Borough, and for some time member of the Town Council. 1899. John Ed. Wright, Mayor." A SHREWD SCOTSMAN'S DESCENDANT.</p>
        <p>"In the last century but one," says "Men of the Day," "there lived in Ayr a shrewd Scots- man, one Peter Walker, who migrated south- ward to found a prosperous business in Warring- ton.. He had the satisfaction of living to see his eldest son, Andrew Barclay Walker, rise to great wealth in the world through the excel- lence of his brewing. Andrew Barclay was a man of wide generosity. Liverpool owes its magnificent Art Gallery and many other generous gifts to his munificence. Mayor, High Sheriff; Knight, and Baronet formed the ascend- ing scale of his honours.. Also Providence blessed him with a numerous progeny, the eldest of Whom was Peter Carlaw, who was educated at home, proceeding neither to public school nor -university. Big game have fallen to his rifle in , Norway, Ceylon, Assam, Colorado, Wyoming, and British Columbia. Six pleasant months he ' spent in the South Seas, sailing round the fairy islands devoted to missionaries and cannibals in a thirty-ton coasting steamer. On, his journey home he sought and slew tarpon in Florida, before tarpon fishing had become a recognised sport to be duly•photographed and paragraphed. His steeplechasers have won him credit on the Turf; and at one time he Was a keen rider to hounds and a player of polo." The funeral took place yesterday at Osmasion. To Sir Andrew Liverpool owed the Walker Art, Gallery and other generous gifts, and his son was a liberal supporter of the Liverpool Seamen's Orphanage. MAYOR'S TELEGRAM OF CONDOLENCE. At Tuesday evening's meeting of the Town Council the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) stated that he had sent the following telegram to Lady Walker: "May I. on behalf of the borough with which Sir Peter Walker was so long and happily connected, offer sincere sym- pathy in your bereavement." His Worship added that it needed no words of his.to ,explain to the Council that the late Sir Peter belonged to a family which-was well connected with War- rington—e Samily which .Warringion had reason to be proud of. Their generous benefactions to the town in various directions would cause the Walker family to stand out prominently from amongst the public-spirited men of Warrington.</p>
        <p>_ The Council passed a vote of condolence with Lady Walker, the raenal)ers standing in silence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WAR AUCTION.</head>
        <p>WAR AUCTION.</p>
        <p>Forthcoming Sale To Help Local Funds.</p>
        <p>In aid of the local War Funds a sale by auction is to be held in the Parr Hall at 2.30 on Thursday afternoon next.' The proceeds of tha sale will be devoted to the local Red Cross Funds to help the excellent work being done at Raddon Court, Thelwall, and The Oaklands; to supplement the Prisoners of War Fund, which sends out a ss. parcel fortnightly to over 200 prisoners of war interned in Germany; and also to provide gifts for the wounded soldiers in , Warrington. Writing us, on behalf of the tommittee tresponsible for organising the sale, the Rev. Irvine Lister, the hon. sec., says By means of circulars and posters an appeal has been made to the public of Warrington and district to send suitable articles to be sold. and a little personal canvassing has been done. The 'response has been fairly liberal, and many use- ful and beautiful articles will be offered , for sale. In order to make the affair an unqualified success, however, it is still necessary that many other contributions should be made. Anything saleable 'which anyone can spare may be sent to the Parr Hall, and will there be thankfully *teceived. Contributions .vill be receivei up to the very moment the sale commences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>WAR " COMFORT-MAKERS."</head>
        <p>WAR " COMFORT-MAKERS."</p>
        <p>Present Efforts To Be Co-ordinated. An interesting meeting was held at the Town Hall on Thursday afternoon, when representa- tives of various .voluntary organisations in the -borough who are actively engaged in supplying "comforts" for the troops met under the chairmanship of the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock).</p>
        <p>The meeting had been convened at the request of the War Office. The •suggestion was made that there should be one central authority for the borough. This would be recognised by the War Office, and the other trganisations would be affiliated to it. The War Office would then state their requirements for the troops and would undertake the free delivery and distribution of the "comforts."</p>
        <p>A very interesting discussion took place, and before anything further was done the. Mayor undertook to bring the points raised to the notice •of the War Once with a view to a further meeting being held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Constable Commended.</p>
        <p>The Watch COmmittee reported to the Town Council on Tuesday evening that at their last meeting it was resolved that P.C. Bennett be commended for his prompt and plucky con- duct in stopping a runaway horse at Bridge Foot on the 6th October.</p>
        <p>Married _Since " Joining_Up."</p>
        <p>Since his enlistment in MAL</p>
        <p>'For;es, P.O</p>
        <p>Taylor has entered the bonds of matrimony. With the consent of the Town Council, the Watch Committee have decided to place him upon the same conditions, as regards allow- ance, as married members of the Police Force who nave been allowed to enlist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>" Vacant Lands."</head>
        <p>" Vacant Lands."</p>
        <p>Among the minutes approved at Tuesday evening's meeting of the Town Council was the following, submitted by the Finance and Estate Committee: "A letter was read from Councillor Pemberton with regard to the ad- vertising of vacant lands, and it was decided to take no steps in the matter at present."•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Town Planning Scheme.</head>
        <p>Town Planning Scheme.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement Committee have re- ported to the Town Council that an interview which a deputation had had with the Town Planning Officer of the Local Government Board on the Town Planning Scheme had re- =,ulted in such information and instructions being received as had removed many doubts Ind difficulties and would greatly assist the committee in the settlement of the scheme.</p>
        <p>When</p>
        <p>Councillor Locker asked Alderman Bolton, chairman of. the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, at Tuesday's meeting of the Coun- i;il, when he hoped to finish St. Elphin's adding that he supposed it would be opened some lay !—The Alderman replied, amid some laugh- ler, that he thought it was one of those things ,ihich would have to Wait until the war finishes.</p>
        <p>No Tram Fares From Discharged Soldiers.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Electricity and Tramways i.:!ommittee, submitted to the Town Council on Tuesday evening, contained a resolution •to the -ffect that the manager be authorised to issue to discharged soldiers, whilst unemployed, free :nonthly .passes on the trams.—Councillor Wil- ,Oii expressed his pleasure at the concession, and at the fact that the 'committee had dealt with Ms suggestion in such a prompt manner.</p>
        <p>Trees for Lovely-lane. At the request of the Street Improvement ,ommittee, the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee—so they reported to the Town Coun- cil on Tuesday evening—have decided to plant a number of trees along Lovely-lane.—Councillor Broadhurst wanted to know why the committee had not granted the repeated requests that trees should be planted round he recreation ground off Liverpool-road? It was a simple matter, but it would make a beauty spot.- - Alderman Bolton laconically replied "If any committee recommends that trees be planted, we do it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Next Time</head>
        <p>Next Time</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Water Committee having been duly proposed and seconded at Tuesday night's meeting of the Town Council, the Mayor, there being no dissentient voice, duly declared them carried. No sooner had he done so, how- ever, than Councillor Broadhurst complained that his Worship "did not wait one single second" to see whether anybody wanted to com- ment on the minutes, "There is," the Coun- cillor added, "a very important matter on the minutes to which I wanted to refer." "Then you'll have to take it at the next meeting now !" said his Worship, passing on to the next busi- ness.</p>
        <p>Gregg Shorthand. The first of a series of lectures in connection with the Gregg system of shorthand was given by Mr. Thomas Lowe, before the members of the Gregg Shorthand Class, at the Winmarleigh Cafe, on Tuesday evening. The lecturer said that the author of the Gregg system of shorthand was a man who had the interest and welfare-of the student at heart, and certainly his ystem was one that would commend itself to all progressive students. Regarding the future of Gregg, he (the speaker) was quite pre- pared to wait the time, which would not be long, when Gregg would receive its just place in the commercial life of this country. After a brief survey of the success achieved by Gregg in America, the first lesson in the system was fully explained with suitable blackboard illus- trations. The interest taken in the first lecture speaks well for the future success of the Gregg system in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Miss Marie Houghton. On Monday evening Miss Marie Houghton sang in Hereford Cathedral at aa organ recital given by Dr. Sinclair, ill aid of the Hereford- shire Territorial C,oniforts Fund. In the course of its criticism of the recital, the "Hereford Mercury" says: The soloist was Miss Marie Houghton, who was one of the principals at the last Hereford Festival, and has been a pupil of Madame Albani. She has a singularly clear voice, of rich quality, and in such a place as the Cathedral it was heard to perfection. On the whole she gave nothing better on Monday than her first selection, the well-known but somewhat difficuli. air from the "Messiah." "I know that my Redeemer liveth." It has not often,, if ever, been sung better in Hereford. \She also sang very well the air, "I will sing of Thy great mercies " from "St. Paul" (Mendel- ssohn), and finally she gave "Nearer, my God, to Thee," to Lewis Carey's setting, very beauti- fully.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Helping Manufacturers.</head>
        <p>Helping Manufacturers.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Town Council, held on Tuesday evening, Alderman Bennett called attention to the following item appearing in the minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Com- mittee: "The Borough Surveyor reported upon the proposed laying of mains from the river to Messrs. Rylands's works, Church-street, and the further consideration of the matter was referred to a sub-committee consisting of the Chairman, Deputy-Chairman and Borough Surveyor." If, said the Alderman, the proposal meant putting more untreated sewage into the river, he did not think the Council ought to approve of it.—Alder- man Tinnion thought it was not desirable to publicly discuss the minute. The proposal in- volved a re-arrangement of the sewers to suit Messrs. Rylands Bros. They had taken so much water out of the river, or they wanted to take so much out of it, and their desire was to return it again as near as possible to the spot from which they took it. The committee thought they were justified in doing all they could to help the manufacturers of the town to carry on their businesses more fittingly and more profit- ably. (Hear, hear.)—The minute was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Late Mrs. Travis.</head>
        <p>The Late Mrs. Travis.</p>
        <p>We record with regret the death of Mrs. Mary Travis, of the Volunteer Inn, Coekhedge, who passed away at her residence on Saturday last. Mrs'. Travis was , the widow of ' the late Mr. James Travis,. and they have held in succession the licence of the Volunteer Inn for the past eighteen years'. She was in her 56th year. Mrs. Travis has enjoyed the esteem and goodwill of her neighbours, -and their •respect was shown in a marked way on the occasion of the funeral, which took place on Tuesday. The coffin, which was of polished brown oak, was conveyed to St. Paul's Church, where the funeral service. was read by the Rev. E. Down- ham. ,The interment took place at the War- rington Cemetery, where the committal service was read by the Rev. E. Downham. Among the mourners pre- sent were: Mr. George Travis (son), Mr. John Ford (brother), Mr. N. Pearson (son-in-law), Mr. John .Ford and. Mr. Joseph • Ford (nephews), Messrs. Kirk, F. Barber, F. Newbold, R. 'Bur- rows, J. Moores and a number of customers of the Volunteer Inn.</p>
        <p>Wreaths and floral tributes were.received from " Edith," "Folly and Norman," "George and Maggie," "Nellie and Bert," Brother John and family, " Joe, Emily, Sally and Maggie ". (nep- hew and nieces), "t,Lucy and Frank," "Sister Bella," " Aunt, Jane," Mr. and Mrs. R. Burrows, Mrs. Whitaker and family, Mrs. Pearson, Mrs. Tavlin and son, Mrs. Nolan, Mr. and Mrs. Looker, Mrs. Atkinson and family, Mrs. Wain- wright and Mrs. Smart, "Two dear old friends," Mr. and Mrs. John Davies, Mr. and Mrs. Bridge and family, Mr. and Mrs. Owen, and "Some of the customers."</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. Mouitheld and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Borough Gymnasium.</head>
        <p>The Borough Gymnasium.</p>
        <p>It may be interesting, as evidencing the popularity of this institution among the ath- letically inclined, to note that during the fortnight ended October 11th the total number of attendances at the institution was 791.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Coal for the Schools.</head>
        <p>Coal for the Schools.</p>
        <p>On the recommendation of a sub-committee the Education Committee have agreed to accept the tender of Messrs. W. W. Westwell and Co. for the supply of coal to the schools during the next six months, subject to the tendered, coal being satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Old Railings At Bridge Foot.</head>
        <p>Old Railings At Bridge Foot.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council 'Alderman Bolton asked when the old railings at Bridge Foot, which were such an eyesore, were to be removed? He added, amid laughter, ethat they gave the place the appearance of a stone- yard.—Alderman Tinnion replied that when his committee got instructions to carry out the work it should be done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Temperance Lecturer for Schools?</head>
        <p>Temperance Lecturer for Schools?</p>
        <p>To the Warrington Education Committee at their monthly meeting on Monday evening, the School Management Sub-committee reported that at their recent meeting a letter was received from the Church of England Tem- perance Society, with reference to temperaUce teaching in elementary schools and to the pos- sible appointment by them of a day school lecturer. The Director was instructed to refer the matter to the school managers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Trades Council and Newsboys.</head>
        <p>Trades Council and Newsboys.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, held on Tuesday week, a letter was read from Mr. J. Moore Murray, the Director of Education, stating—in reply to a query from the Council—that with regard to the employment of newsboys in the early marning he was given to understand that such action was to be taken as would prevent any violation of the bylaws. The delegates pre- sent expressed themselves as satisfied with the Director's communication.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Brief Meeting.</head>
        <p>A Brief Meeting.</p>
        <p>There were present at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee, held at the Town Hall on Monday evening, Coun. H. Roberts tchairman), Mr. A. J. Jolley (vice- chairman), Canon Stevenson, the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes, Aldermen Smethurst and Bennett, Councillors Locker, Wilkinson and Archer, and Messrs. C. Unsworth, R. T. Fairclough, J. tpson, C. Plinston, J. Jarman and George Walker; with the Director of Education (Mr. J. Woore Murray). The meeting lasted a bar* twenty minutes and is therefore among the shortest on record.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Elmwood Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>Elmwood Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>The takings at the Elmwood Avenue Congre- gational Church sale of work on the final day— Friday of last week—amounted to £44. With several amounts received later, and including the takings on the two previous days, the grand total of £336 was reached. Before the sale there remained a debt on the church buildings of £4OO, and Mr. Ziba Armitage very kindly promised that whatever was raised up to half that amount he would double. Consequently the debt will now•be cleared, and a considerable balance left in hand.</p>
        <p>Convalescent Home for the Borough.</p>
        <p>The Finance and Estate Committee reported to the Town Council on Tuesday evening that the Town Clerk had informed them that the Registrar had extended the time for the exer- cise of the option by the Corporation of taking over Seabank Nook until the 10th of November next. It was resolved by the committee that the Council be recommended to exercise the option under the will of the late Mr. Joseph Kitchingman, deceased, in favour of taking over Seabank Nook as a convalescent home.— Subsequently the committee's resolution came before the Council, in General Purposes Com- mittee assembled, and it was resolved that the recommendation be approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Sir Henry Hibbert's Loss.</head>
        <p>Sir Henry Hibbert's Loss.</p>
        <p>Sir Henry Hibbert, M.P., whose only OM Captain Cyril Hibbert, has been reported missing, rose at Monday's meeting of the Lanca- shire County Education Committee to make a personal statement, but broke down. The Director of Education (Dr. Snape) explained that owing to his recent loss and heavy en- gagements Sir Henry had spoken of the possi- bility of his retirement from the chairmanship. The committee, unwilling to lose the services of Sir Henry, allowed him three months' leavo of absence, with complete freedom from com- mittee work.—lt will be remembered that some three years ago Sir Henry visited Warrington and distributed the prizes at the Grammar School Speech Day held in Parr Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>War and School Building.</head>
        <p>War and School Building.</p>
        <p>The Sites and Buildings Sub-committee -re- ported to the Education Committee on Monday evening that they had had submitted to them a further letter from Messrs. J. Dolan and Son„ the contractors for the erection of the Oakwood avenue Council School, giving details as to the increased cost of materials, labour and carting ---owing to the war—and requesting their con- §ideration thereof. It was resolved that as from October Ist—providing the necessary time sheets and other papers are produced when and as desired—the contractors be paid, without pre- judice, in respect of the increased cast of (a) pabour: the difference between the standard rates of wages . paid for all labour employed upon this contract immediately prior and subsequent to the Ist October, 19151 (b) Carting: a sum equal to 2s. per full day for each cart employed wholly upon this contract; and (c) Materials: the sum of £5O, being equivalent to half the increased cost of plumbing materials, together with a sum equal to 50 per cent. of the increased cost per ton upon all cement purchased after the Ist December, 1915.</p>
        <p>Late Principal of Secondary School.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee held on Monday evening further reference was made to the loss which the cause of education in the town has sus- tained by the death of Mr. E. S. A. Robson, the late principal of the Secondary School and Technical Institute, whose death occurred at the beginning of August after a long and pain- ful illness.—Two letters referring to Mr. Rob- son's death were read. The first was from the widow—Mrs. K. L. Robson—who, writing from St. Cyres, Menston-in-Wharfedale, Yorkshire. said :—" I hardly know how to express my thanks to the Education Committee for their message of sincere sympathy. . . . Their words of appreciation of my husband's work in the school will always be greatly treasured by me and also by my two boys when they are older." The, second letter was from Mr. John Robson, of Selbourne Lodge, Sale, the father of ,the deceased headmaster, who also wrote thanking the cbmmittee for their expres- sion of 'sympathy with the family. The writer added: "I also wish to record our deep obli- gation to the committee a for their kindness shown to my son during his long and trying illness."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Silver-street School Prestige.</head>
        <p>Silver-street School Prestige.</p>
        <p>In view of the transfer of the Sifv:er-street School to the Local Education Authority as from the Ist September, 1915, it has been re- solved by the Education Committee that the services of the members of the staff of the school be continued upon the usual conditions for teachers employed in Council Schools. The teachers %affected are :—Mixed Department : Messrs. John Upson, Ernest Hawkesworth, Charles Wilcockson, Stanley C. Jones, Thomas J. Dyke, and Alfred R. Cragg; and the Misses Elizabeth A. Parker, Jessie J. Ward, Lilian Atkinson, Janet A. Dewar, Winifred Upson, and Dorothy Upson. Infants' Department: The Misses Annie Booth, Maggie Griffiths, Mary E. Trafford, Bessie P. England and Eva F. Woollacott. Alderman Bennett made brief allusion to the, transference of the school, re- marking that it would be now coming under the complete control of the Education Com- mittee, and that as it had in the past, under its present Headmaster, Mr. J. Upson. borne a name which stood high, and was held ill honour throughout local educational circles, so he hoped that, under the same able Head- master, but under the new regime as to mall: fl agement, it would continue to flourish a would gain even new laurels and Preotige tb,tl days that are to come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>foraljniettivnre</head>
        <p>foraljniettivnre</p>
        <p>Continued from Page 4.</p>
        <p>That War !</p>
        <p>Owing to the war the contractors for the supply of police helmets to the Borough Force have intimated that the manufacturers now demand an extra ss. per dozen. The Council have decided to pay the increased cost. "The Will To Love."</p>
        <p>At the Friends' Meeting House, Buttermarket- street, on Sunday evening, Mrs. C. Watts, or Manchester, will give an address, to which all are invited to listen, on "The Will to Power and the Will to Love.'"</p>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices. Wednesday.</p>
        <p>• Wheat was firmer on the market to-day, prices ranging from 6s. 6d. to 6s. 10d. per bushel of 701 b., good samples are being shown. Oats are firmer, but poor samples only are offered, but good prices were given, viz., 3s. Bd. to 3s. 10.1 d. being offered. Straw is better sold, good oat straw being worth 405.; wheat straw 38s. Flour is firmer, price per 2801 b. 395. Bran and all offals are advancing. Eggs 5 for is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Swimming Success.</head>
        <p>Swimming Success.</p>
        <p>At a swimming gala held at the Garston Baths on Tuesday evening, the championship gold medal and O'Hagen silver rose-bowl were won by ,Miss Phyllis Hodgkinson, daughter of Mr. Peter Hodgkinson, Wash-lane, Latehford, and late of Moorside Farm, Appleton, near Widnes. Miss Hodgkinson, who is sixteen years of age, is niece of Miss Lamb, of Garston, and also great granddaughter of the late Ur. Thomas Lamb, of Garston</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Cards For Comforts.</head>
        <p>Cards For Comforts.</p>
        <p>At the John Crosfield Memorial Hall on Thursday evening next, a whist drive and social will be held in order. .to swell the funds of the Liberal Women's Sewing Class, which has been, and is still being, so useful in the matter of snaking comforts for the boys at the Front. Tickets are obtainable from the steward of the dub, or from Mrs. Osborne, hon. sec. of the Women's Liberal Association, 22, Bold-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Latchford Baptists' ,Autumnal Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Latchford Baptists' ,Autumnal Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The autumnal anniversary services, in con- nection with the Latchford Baptist Church, were</p>
        <p>held on Sunday. The special preacher at the • morning and evening services was the Rev. 4ackson Chedburn, Of Ilkley. In the afternoon a musical cantata entiled "The Day of Rest" (Witty) was exceedingly well rendered by the choir. There were good congregations through- out the day, and the collections, taken on behalf of the church funds, amounted to in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Sankey's New Bride.</head>
        <p>Sankey's New Bride.</p>
        <p>The old wooden swing bridge which for many years has served those who have had occasion to cross. the St. Helens and Sankey Canal at Sankey Bridges has given place to a fine steel ' structure built cn the bascule principle, the roadway being 'raised ,by hydraulic power in order that boats may pass. The new bridge has been in course of construc- tion for some considerable time. and it Was first opened to' the public on Tuesday afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Four Weeks' Funerals.</head>
        <p>Four Weeks' Funerals.</p>
        <p>The report of the Registrar for the four weeks ended October 11th, which .was presented to the Cemetery Committee, and, by them, to the Town Council, showed that during the period named there were 57 funerals of adults at the Cemetery, 33 of minors, and six of still-. born or prematurely-born children, the figures for the corresponding period of last year being 49, 54 and 1.7 respectively. The receipts for the four weeks named were ..t129 12s. 9d., as compared with Y-99 Os. Bd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Spiritualist Services.</head>
        <p>Spiritualist Services.</p>
        <p>The service; held on Sunday in the Druids' Mall were largely attended, the evening meeting especially so, many being unable to obtain admission. The special attraction was the visit of Mr. Tom Tyrrell (of Blackburn), the noted psychic, whose demonstrations on previous occasions have created so much wonder. On Sunday brief addresses were given by the Presi- dent (Mr. Stout), and Mr. ,Tyrrell, after a few introductory remarks, gave a large number of clairvoyant descriptions, together with full names and in some cases earthly addresses, occupations, cause ,of death, &amp;c. In almost every instance recognition was acknowledged. Mrs. Fox, a local medium, gave good clairvoy- ance at the after-meeting, and also conducted the usual meetings on Monday..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Friendly Societies and IVlaoral Churchgoing.</head>
        <p>Friendly Societies and IVlaoral Churchgoing.</p>
        <p>A meeting of representatives of the Friendly Soeieties of the town was held in the Town Hall on Monday to make arrangements and ballot for positions in the Mayor's procession to the Parish Church on November- 14th. The ballot resulted as follows :—R.A.0.8. with Band, 1; M.U. 1.0.0. F., 2; National Independent Order of Oddfellows, 3; A.O. Shepherds, 4; Free Gardeners, 5; A.O. Foresters, 6; A.O. Druids, 7; Catholic Friendly Societies, Irish National Foresters and Ancient Order of Ilibernians, 8; Grand United Order of Oddfellows. 9; Hearts of Oak and Rational Association, 10. On the proposition of the Mayor (Alderman Peacock, P.P.G.M.), Bro. T. P. Bradshaw (M.U. •1.0.0. F. was appointed chief marshal. Shorthand Successes. The following students a Wood's College have been successful in securing their certi- ficates from the National Shorthand 4ssocia- tion Examining Board :—Theory Janie Dodd, Edith. Saunders, Nellie Povey and Margaret 'Thompson. Speed: James Milner and Lilian Quarriel (100 words); Fanny Martin (distinc- tion), Ethel Wallington (distinction), Annie Chantler and Helen Wallington (90 words); Ethel BertleS (distinction), Marion Lowe (dis- tinction), Florence Malkin (distinction), Marion Simpson, May Heath, Fred Shelley, Gertrude Bradely and Dora Epps (80 words); Catherine Little (70 words); Rose Byrne (distinction), Eva Musket (distinction), Marjorie Barrington (distinction), Helen Littler (distinction), Wil- liam Hughes (distinction), Gladys Challoner (distinction), Dorothy Dendy and William Boughley (GO words); Ellen White (50 words). "Guarding Continents of Spiritual Ideals." •-• Continuing ,his course of special sermons on "The World War," the Rev. Percy Ine.son, at the Bold-street Wesleyan Church, on Sunday evening, took as his special subject" The Con- quering Hero," finding his text in the words "Guard that which is committed, to thee!': God, he said, wanted an 'fumy of conquering heroes, and the forefathers of the present generation ,had fought many a long and arduous battle in order to guard for the present generation that which God had committed to them. Many a weary fight had they waged that they might pass on to the present generation the treasures of freedom and truth. The charge God and their country gave them' that clay was: "'Guard that which is committed to thee!" and in considering it theyAniist remem- ber that to-day they, as a nation, were not merely guarding's° many-square miles of land: but were guarding continents of spiritual ideals. • Slot Gas Meters and Rentals. Councillor Pemberton called' attention at Tuesday's meeting of the Council to the decision of the 'Gas Committee to the effect that slot meters be not flied in houses of £2O per annum and upwards, and moved that the resolution, which lie described as arbitrary, be referred back. There were, he contended, many houses letting at Bs. a week and upwards that were occupied by people of the working class, to whom the deprivation of a slot meter would be a decided inconvenience. Why people of the working Class should not have slot meters when they were prepared to pay extra for them he failed to understand.—Councillor Wilson sec- onded.—Councillor Firth Lindley explained that the decision was solely due to the difficulty of finding the necessary labour during the period of the war. There had to be a curtail- ment somewhere, and the £2O house was fixed as the limit at whicli the line was to be drawn, because it was considered that people who were likely to live in 'houses at that, and higher, rentals, were likely to be able to do without slot meters, at least during the period of the war. The whole matter would be subject to revision when peace came again.—On receiving this ex- planation Councillor Pemberton withlrew his proposal- •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>injuries which consist of shrapnel wounds hi the wrist during the great British advance.</p>
        <p>Private Houghton, a typical specimen of the British soldier, interviewed by our representa- tive, stated :</p>
        <p>"Our officer gave instructions for the general advance, and right loyally did our men respond. It was hot work, I can tell you, but soon we were in the second line of German trenches, and then things began to hum. I was in the trenches half an hour, when suddenly I was bowled over. A shrapnel wound in the right wrist put me out of action' just as I was relish- ing the fun, but I'm getting all right and will soon be back again, with better luck; perhaps, next time."</p>
        <p>Private Houghton is a single man, aged 25 years, and was educated at the Hamilton-street Schools, and prior to enlisting at the outbreak of Far was employed as a wet-drawer at Messrs. nylands Bros., where he is well known and re- spected. He was sent over to France eight months ago. When a boy he attended the Brick-street Sun- day school. Another member of the family, his brother David, is at present in the Dardanelles,, serving with the R.F.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>We give details of men who were included in the long list of casualties published in "The Examiner" last week, Since then the follow- ing have been added:—</p>
        <p>KILLED. Private T. Warburton. Private John Dacey.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. Private John Bradbury. Private Albert Stout. Private J. Spensley. Private D. Munn. Totals :—Killed, 91; wounded, 424; missing, 11 —526.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PRIVATE A. STOUT.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE A. STOUT.</p>
        <p>Another Rylands's Employe Shot In</p>
        <p>The Face.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Stout, 30, Chorley-street, has been in- formed that her son—Private Albert Stout, of the Warrington Territorials—has been wounded in action. The offiCial communication,' received from the Record Office, Preston, and signed by Captain E. G. Finch, read as follows "I regret to have to inform you that a report has this day been received here to the effect that 22760 Private A.' Stout, of the 4th Battalion South Lancashire- Regiment, is ill at the 25th General' Hospital, Camiers, suffer- ing from a gun-shot wound in the face, and a slight wound in his ear. Any information received at this office as to his condition or progress will be at _once notified to you."</p>
        <p>Private Stout himself has since written home, and says he is progressing favourably. He only refers to the slight hurt which his ear has received, and does not in any way speak of the wound to his face. Private Stout, who is twenty-five years of age, was educated at Silver-street Day School. He was, before the outbreak- of war, employed by Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd., at which place he had worked for several years. He attended Buckley-street Sunday School, where he was well liked by all. Indeed, he was presented with a Bible as a reward for his good conduct and regular attendance. lie also belongs to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>PRIVATE J. SPENSLEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. SPENSLEY.</p>
        <p>"Never Mind ! We Have All Done Our Share ! "</p>
        <p>Private J. Spensley has written home to his wife, who resides at 139, Church-street, War- rington, stating that he is recovering Gplen- didly from his i.njuries received in the great _</p>
        <p>advance. He is at- present- in the Suffolk -Hos- pital, Ampton Hall, Bury St. Edmund's, and' in a letter dated October 17th states:—</p>
        <p>"My shoulder is- nearly better, but my ribs are still sore. I am doing a bit of waiting on the other fellows now, and now and again take a, walk around the beautiful grounds here. I am surprised Jim Prangleton has been • hit; there seems to have been a lot knocked over the -same night as myself. Never mind! We all have done our share."</p>
        <p>Private Spensley, whose age is 30 years, mined the battalion twelve months ago and was drafted to the Front in June last. Prior to his enlistment he was emphyed in the wet shop at 3fesrs. Rylands Bros. He was ..?du- eated at St. Thomas' School, Stockton Heath, and attended the Parish Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PRIVATE D. MUNN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE D. MUNN.</p>
        <p>Hurt By Trench Mortar When. Getting. Into, Dug-Out..</p>
        <p>Mr. Frederick Mungr„ oE 301, Fletcher-etrect,. .Warrington. has- received am .official, communi- cation from the War Office stating that his. son, Private Duncan Munn, was wounded on. Octo- ber 4th.</p>
        <p>Private Munn was just stepping into a dug- out when it was destroyed by a trench mortar. The occupants were buried alive, but none were seriously injured. Private Munn received wounds in the back and the right shoulder, but it is understood that these are not serious. He was taken to a small hospital fifteen miles behind the firing-line and from thence to Boulogne, and last week he Was sent to New- castle-on-Tyne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Footballer and Chorister.</head>
        <p>Footballer and Chorister.</p>
        <p>Private Munn is 20 years of age. For some months before the war he had been 'a Terri- torial. He was in the service of Messrs. John Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., as -an invoice clerk for about a year, having previously Leen a member, of the office staff of Messrs. W. D. Houghton and Co., Sankey Wire Mills. Private Munn has since boyhood been closely associated with St. James' Church, Latchfotd, and was at one time in the choir. He is a keen foot- baller and has played, for Crosfield's team. As a member of the Crosfield Operatic Society, he took part in the comparatively recent produc- tion of "The Old Guard."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>CORPORAL JOHN BUTLER.</head>
        <p>CORPORAL JOHN BUTLER.</p>
        <p>Unfortunate Territorial Wounded A Second Time.</p>
        <p>News has been received by Mrs. Sarah Butler, of 100, Lythgoes-lane, Warrington, that her son, Corporal John Butler, of the 4th South Lancs. Regiment, has again been wounded in action, as per a field card from headquarters. No further details are yet to hand. Corporal John Butler was wounded in the big charge of June 16th. He is the nephew of Private P. Warburton who was recently killed, and whose photo appears elsewhere in this issue. The unfortunate Corporal, prior to the war, was employed by Messrs. Rylands Bros., and was an athlete of note, being a member of the harriers team in connection with the firm, and winning many. prizes. Medals which were preseated to him flir athletic rrowess have all been lost in Hit• big charg:•. Educated at the National School and an at- tendarkt of the St. George's Mission, he is widely known and respected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <p>PRIVATE T. WARBURTON. Killed. %</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <p>Rylands Bros. He took a great interest in all branches of sport and was a harrier of consid- erable note in the works' team, winning many prizes by his prowess as a runner.. _ .</p>
        <p>- The first intimation of his death was first received by the late Private Warburton's father, who resides in Lythgoe's-lane, who re- ceived the ill-fated news from Lieutenant H. H. Clarke, machine gun officer. On September 28th he wrote as follows:</p>
        <p>"I am awfully sorry to say that this letter bears bad news for you. In the battle which started last Saturday your son, Private T. Warburton, was struck down by a large shell pitching on a dug-out in which he and two comrades were sheltering. He must have been killed instantly. A short time ago he joined me in the machine gun section and had been doing splendid work. I can assure you that all his fellow-gunners join with me in offering all our sympathies in your great loss, and they would like their sympathy to be extended to his mother. We have one consolation in ./ knowing that he died like a brave and gallant soldier. War takes a terrible toll and thous- ands have fallen in this big effort for the sake and safety_ of those at home. If we find any of his belongings we shall send them back to you. Please accept my sincere sympathy in your and Mrs. Warburton's great loss." „ Private Warburton, who was only 27 years of age, joined the Territorials soon after the outbreak of war and was drafted to France it June. He was educated at the National School and comes of a fine patriotic family. His brother Charles was killed in action whilst serving with the 3rd South Lancashire Regi- ment ih January last. Three of Mrs. Warbur- ton's brothers are with the Forces in France— Thomas Lawless, A.S.C.; Peter Lawless, King's Royal Rifles; and Frank Lawless, South; Lancs. Another brother-in-law—Private S. Brereton— is at present a prisoner of war in Germany; 'whilst the late Private Warburton's nephew is serving with the Warrington Territorials in the firing-line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PRIVATE JOHN DACEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN DACEY.</p>
        <p>Second Son Killed Within Five</p>
        <p>Months.</p>
        <p>The news that Private s'ohn Dace,y, of the Warrington Territorials, was killed in action during the advance on September 25th, has now been officially confirmed by a note from the War Office. Private Dacey was a married man, who lived with his wife and one child at 3, Manley-street, in the Lovely-lane district. He was 22 years of age and worked at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. At the outbreak of the war he joined the Royal Field Artillery, but subsequently transferred to the Warrington Territorials.</p>
        <p>Shot By Sniper.</p>
        <p>Private Henry Saton, of. the Territorials, writes to Mrs. Dacey:,-- " It is my deepest regret to inform you of the death of .your husband, Jack: he was one of my greatest pals. He was hit by a gunner sniper while going up to the trenches. My last words with him, half an hour before lie. was hit. were Cheer Oh.' He left me smiling. His pals will</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <p>.\\,_ Vii .. _ , :: ; .:: . It" --..,.....-...-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <p>CORPORAL J. G, LEIGH.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>T. and T. Vicars, of Earlestown. He has be- longed to the Territorials for about five years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PRIVATE JOHN WOODS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN WOODS.</p>
        <p>"Getting On Splendidly" In A Home Hospital.</p>
        <p>A few days ago Mrs. Woods, of 2, Warbur- ton-street, off Winwick-road, was informed that her husband, Private John Woods, of the machine gun section of the Warrington Terri- torials, had received a shrapnel-bullet wound in the right knee. Later she got the following letter from her husband, written under date October 6th, from the Edmonton Hospital, London :</p>
        <p>"I am getting on splendidly, and have been walking about. My knee, however, still troubles me a great deal. I am rather afraid that I shall never be able to walk properly again. It does seem strange to be here, after what we have gone through in Prance." Private Woods, who is twenty-five years of age, enlisted at the outbreak of war. He had been previously engployed at Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s works. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>PRIVATE JOHN BRADBURY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN BRADBURY.</p>
        <p>Bylands's Man _Says__" Lucky To Be</p>
        <p>Alive."</p>
        <p>Private John Bradbury, of the Warrington Territorials, and son of Mr. and Mrs. Brad- bury, 13, Birchall-etreet, has been wounded in action. The news was first conveyed through the medium of an official intimation received from the War Office, which stated that Private Bradbury had received a gun-shot wound in the face.</p>
        <p>He has since been brought to England, and is now in hospital at Yarrow. Writing home he says:—</p>
        <p>"I was hit in the cheek., The. bullet went through my mouth, and came out at the back of my neck, so I can say that I am a lucky man, to be alive. My head has been very bad this week. I , shall never forget. the sights I saw when I was wounded. . . . Please send me some stamps." Single and twenty years of age, Private Bradbury was educated at Hamilthn-itieet School. He was formerly- employed hy Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd.' and attended Buckley- street Sunday School. He took an interest in football.. He enlisted soon after the outbreak of war. His brother—Gunner Harry Brad- bury—is in training with the Royal Field Artillery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>PRIVATE J. ROBSON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. ROBSON.</p>
        <p>Hit By Two Bullets And Sent Borne On Leave.</p>
        <p>Whilst acting as a bomb thrower with the Warringkon Territorials on September 25th,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <p>PRIVATE JAMES THOMASON.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PRIVATE WILLIAM nouGirrox.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE WILLIAM nouGirrox.</p>
        <p>Put Out Just When lie Was Relishing</p>
        <p>The Fun.</p>
        <p>Private William Houghton, son of Mr. and Mrs. Josiah Houghton, of 54, School Brow, has arrived home on a ten days' furlough through</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>DRIVER JACKSON.</head>
        <p>DRIVER JACKSON.</p>
        <p>Private Joseph Robson received a rifle bullet wound in the left arm and a shrapnel bullet wound in the right thigh. He is now progress- ing satisfactorily, and has been home on ten days' leave. He was seen by an "Examiner" representative, but declined to talk of his ex- periences at the Front, or of the "Terriers," pointing out that it would be entirely against his orders to do so. Three Brothers in the Dardanelles.</p>
        <p>Private Robson, who is 30 years of age and married, has his home at 40, Amelia-street. He was educated in Manchester, and before the out- break of war was employed at the Cheshire Lines Company's Waggon Works. He rejoined the Territorials at the outbreak of war, having previougal served in them for four years. His brother has been out to the Dardanelles, and has come back wounded; two other brothers are still serving there, and a brother-in-law is with the Warrington Territorials.</p>
        <p>Territorials, who makes his - home with his sister—Mrs. Richardson, at 31, Chorley-street— has received shrapnel wounds in several parts of the body, and is now in hospital at Cam- bridge. Private Thomason, who is twenty years of age, attended, as a boy, the Silver-street School. He was formerly employed by the Longford Wire Works Company. He took an interest in sport, and had played football for a local junior club. He had been in the Territorials previous to re- enlisting at the outbreak of war, and is novi a member of the machine gun section.</p>
        <p>home at Its, Chorley-street, where his wife and family of young children are ter present residing. He had seen military service before the war broke out, having served in the Regulars for a number of years and being on the Reserve when the call came. - _</p>
        <p>--Writing to a friend—Mr. Wm. Aloock, of 99, Mersey-street, • Warrington—Driver Jackson says: "I have been mentioned in despatches and I have received my General's card already. He .presented it to me on Sunday last, and it was the proudest moment of my life. I am no better than any other man—l have only done my duty as a soldier should do. I suppose you will be wondering why I have come to be so highly recognised.,. . . I have performed some very difficult duties, and I took the General's eye the last time I was on parade. . . . I hope you won't think me a boaster or a brag in any way PP Before being recalled to the Colours, Private Jackson was employed at the Bewsey Wire Roper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</head>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>REPORTED KILLED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. BRADSHAW.</p>
        <p>mnirade Says Bullet Went Through</p>
        <p>Him.</p>
        <p>Private John Bradshaw, of the 7th Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment, is 'unoffi- cially reported killed. Private John E. Porter, a comrade, writing to Private Bradshaw's sister-in-law on October 11th, said that Private Bradshaw was killed while working in the trenches, a bullet passing through his shoulder and out at his back. Private Bradshaw, who was educated at St. Benedict's School and who lived with his brother, Mr. Robert Bradshaw, at 18, Laira- street, joined the Army 'several months before the outbreak of war. He was formerly em- ployed by Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd. He was invalided home from the Front with frost- bite about Christmas-time, and he had been back in France about three months when he met his death.</p>
        <p>Two Step-Brothers Killed.</p>
        <p>Two of Private Bradshaw's step-brothers have been killed while serving with the colours,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <p>\ a</p>
        <p>Reported Killed</p>
        <p>and another is in training for the Front Of the two step-brothers killed, one—John Hard- man, of the King's Royal Rifles—lost his life in the big advance; whilst the other—Harry Hardman—was killed through falling over a cliff while in training with the R.F.A. in the South of England.</p>
        <p>„ „ . „. : 111!.r. .• ' • '. .</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN BRADSHAW.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL H. DAVIS.</p>
        <p>Hit Two Miles Behind The Firing- Line.</p>
        <p>Corporal Harry Davis, son of Mr. and Mrs. T. F. Davis, 67, Longford-street, and a member of the Royal Engineers, received a shell wound in the back Qf the neck and another in the left thigh, caused by the bursting of' a stray shell while he was standing in his billet, two miles behind the firing line. He has, however, now almost recovered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Territorial Turned Engineer.</head>
        <p>Territorial Turned Engineer.</p>
        <p>A single man, twenty-two years of age, Cor- poral Davis joined the Army Service Corps (Territorial Section) about three years before the outbreak of war, but was transferred to the Royal Engineers about two months ago, and was drafted to the Front. He was educated at the Hamilton-street School, and later at the Tech- nical School. He was formerly employed at the laboratory at Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons' works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WITH MILITARY HONOURS.</head>
        <p>WITH MILITARY HONOURS.</p>
        <p>Impressive Scenes At Funeral Of Rifleman Heesom.</p>
        <p>Full military honours were accorded at the funeral on Saturday afternoon of Rifleman William Heesom, who, as we reported last week, died from wounds in hospital at Folke- stone. A firing party of the Warringtnn Terri- torials were present, and gave the customary volleys over the open grave, whilst the band of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment, in charge of Bandmaster R. E. Cahill, played a funeral march as the cortege moved along the thronged streets en route for, the Cemetery. At the grave- side the Last Post was sounded by the buglers, and the band played the hymn ." When our heads are bowed with woe." Many beautiful floral tributes were placed on the grave. The service was conducted by the Rev. H. B. Firth, vicar of St. James', Latchford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>LIFE FOR COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>LIFE FOR COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>Beamont School's Tribute To</p>
        <p>Brave " Old Boy."</p>
        <p>A touching ceremony was performed at the Beamont Boys' School, Warrington, on Wednes- day morning, in connection with the death of Rifleman Victor Albert Clarence Colter, for- merly of Battersby-lane, Warrington, whose tragic end whilst serving his country was recorded in last week's "Examiner." As we</p>
        <p>stated, Rifleman Colter was educated at Beamont School and, as he is the first " old " boy to fall in the war, it was znought only fitting that a memorial sßould be placed in the school. The memorial, which has been given by the girls of the senior school, takes the form of a picture depicting a fallen soldier at the foot of the Cross, from a painting by James Clark, which is now in possession of Her Majesty the Queen the inscriptions under the picture, in addition to one setting forth its purposes, are "Greater love bath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends," and "I am the Resurrection and the Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Sadness Tinged with Pride.</head>
        <p>Sadness Tinged with Pride.</p>
        <p>When the scholars had assembled, they were addressed by Mr. Win. Mason (head master), who said that the school received the news of Private Colte-r's death with intense, sadness, but the sadness was tinged with pride that Beataont School had already given a life in sacrifice for the good of the Empire. Private Colter was the first "old boy" from that sehool who had gone, and it was hoped that he would be the last. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Mason then asked Miss Taylor, the head mistress of the girls'. department, to unveil the picture. Before doing so, Miss Taylor said that it had been felt that they wanted to do some- thing in memory of Private Colter. Victor had laid down his life for his friends, and the girls wanted to show in some tangible form how deeply they felt hi S loss and appreciated his sacrifice. A number of girls then sang the hymn "On the Resurrection Morning," after which the pic- ture was unveiled. _</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett, who was present, remarked how deeply he had been touched by the brief ceremony. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</p>
        <p>Miss Marie Houghton And The Whitecross Hospital.</p>
        <p>We have pleasure in announcing that Miss Marie Houghton—assisted by Messrs. C. Lam- bert and J. Beddard—has kindly promised to arrange a concert each Saturday evening during the winter months for the benefit of the patients in the Whitecross Military Hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>NELSON DAY!</head>
        <p>NELSON DAY!</p>
        <p>Scholars Celebrate Anniversary Of Trafalgar.</p>
        <p>ALD. BENNETT'S PRIZES.</p>
        <p>Trafalgar Day (Thursday) was celebrated in the schools of Warrington this year by the dis- tribution of prizes at two elementary schools and at the Secondary School bylerman Ben- nett.</p>
        <p>For some years past it has been the custom to offer prizes for the best essays on patriotic subjects, and this year the subject for the elementary scholars was "The duty of a citizen. of to-day," the subject for thc• Secondary School student being "1815-1915." A number of essays were sent in, and the prizes, consisting of a number of books, were pregented to the following:— Secondary School: Kathleen Brown, 'SOS, Man- chester-road, Warrington, and Dudley P. Bay- ley, Greenbank-road, Latchford Without. Beamont Boys' School: Thomas Hazelhurst, 25, Cornwall-street, Warrington. Arpley-street School: Jessie Atherton, 147, Wellfield street, Warrington Of the elementary school essays sent in, that of Thomas Hazelhurst was the best, and accordingly the school to which he belongs will hold the Trafalgar Day Shield (also given by Alderman Bennett) fdr 12 months.</p>
        <p>AT ARPLEY-STREET SCHOOL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Alderman Bennett Revisits His Old</head>
        <p>Alderman Bennett Revisits His Old</p>
        <p>School.</p>
        <p>The prize to be first presented was that won at the Arpley-street School, where Jessie. Atherton received her books at the hands of the Alderman. _ _</p>
        <p>Welcoming the Alderman to the school, The Headmaster (Mr. Ed. Wallington) said</p>
        <p>that he was glad to see Mr. Bennett Present for two reasons. ..The first was that he Was to pre- sent to one of their scholars a prize for essay writing, and the second reason was that Mr. Bennett attended that school as a scholar many years ago, and in his public life had shown . the same character as when a boy. Alderman Bennett expressed h pleasure at being able to revisit his old school. Mr. Wallington, he said, had been working for the good of the town a great number of years, and what the town owed to him it was impossible to over-estimate. He (the speaker) instituted this because he did not think that the lesson of patriotism was taught sufficiently, but whilst they were living in the midst of a terrible war, the most awful war the world had ever seen, it was time they thought about their ce6ntry. The thought 'of Nelson should put encoTrage- ment in the hearts and souls of the boys of this country. Nelson was a great Man, who, above all, loved his country. He was one of the greatest and noblest of Englishmen that ever lived; he loved his country with a passionate devotion. His motto was "England expects every man to do his duty," and almost his last words were "Thank God I have done my duty." His (the speaker's) object wr.s to help every boy and girl to say "Thank God I have done my duty." Mantle Of Nelson Falls On Jellicoe.</p>
        <p>During his holiday, proceeded Alderman Bennett, he had the pleasure for the first time of visiting Nelson's birthplace at Burnham Thorpe. He had also seen the Victory this summer, and once more the Battle of Trafalgar rose before him, and once again the lessons of Nelson's life rose before him. This was the 110th anniversary of Waterloo, and in connec- tion with this battle they thought of Welling- ton, who was just as brave in his own way as Nelson was. To these two men Europe owed her freedom. Fortunately England had still got brave men. Jellicoe was doing the same work as Nelson did. French and their soldiers were checking the terrible danger which was threatening the Continent in the same heroic manner as Wellington. He (the speaker) had faith in England yet: he believed that she would see another Trafalgar and another Waterloo. The British Empire would never pass away if they did their duty. He was try- ing in his own way to help his country. Many had gone out to do their duty, but many were hanging back. He believed in his country with all his heart and soul; through all the ages it would still last. England would always stand by and never again would freedom be trampled upon; there would be no more oppression. Amidst loud applause Alderman Bennett then presented Jessie Atherton with a number of books. - -</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Jackson Holmes proposed a hearty vote of thank to Alderman Bennett for his address and for the prize. This was seconded by Mr. J. Longland and was carried with loud applause. The children sang a number of sonzs, includ- ing: "Before All Lands," "Red, White and Blue," "A Soldier's Farewell," and "Home,</p>
        <p>Sweet Home." A poem, "Trafalgar," specialty composed for the occasion, was recited by Miss Atherton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>AT BFAMONT SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>AT BFAMONT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Many Teachers And Old Boys Wearing</p>
        <p>The Khaki.</p>
        <p>At the Beamont Council School, Alderman Bennett presented a similar prize to Thomas Hazelhurst, of 25, Cornwall-street, Warrington. Making the presentation, Alderman Bennett said that he had always had a great interest in the Beamont School, as the foundation-stone was laid when he was chairman of the Educa- tion Committee, and he performed the opening teremony. It was the first Council school built in Warrington. Hazelhurst had sent in an excellent essay. Mr. J. Moore Murray moved a vote of thanks to Alderman Bennett, and this was seconded by Mr. Mason, who said that three pupil teachers and five assistant masters of the school and over 60 per cent. of the old boys had joined the Army. Practically every bey in the school had a relative serving. The motion was carried with loud applause. The scholars sang the songs: "Keep the home fires burning," and "Ye Mariners of England." Thomas Richardson recited the poem "A Year Ago," which had been specially written for the occasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>AT THE SECONDARY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>AT THE SECONDARY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Old Scholars Serving On Land And</p>
        <p>Water And In Air!</p>
        <p>At the Secondary School in the afternoon, Alderman Bennett had a most cordial welcome. Addressing the students the new Principal (Mr. J. S. Broome) said that from its inception Alderman Bennett had always taken a great interest in the Secondary School and its staff and children. He asked the Alderman to con- tinue to show that interest in the school under the more or less new regime which was being inaugurated. The school ought to be excep- tionally proud of the old boys who were serving their country, for over 40 per cent. of those who were eligible had joined and were doing exceptionally good service. They were above the earth, on the earth, and some were under the earth—in the trenches; they were above the sea, on the sea, and some were in the sea in the submarines. Last week he heard of an old boy who was going to fly across to France to take up his duties. Alderman Bennett expressed his pleasure at being able to visit the Secondary School. He had known Mr. Proome for some time and it was a matter of treat personal satisfaction to him to know that Mr. Broome had been ap- pointed to follow the late Mr. Robson as Principal. He was quite sure that, as years went by, the school 'would obtain new laurels. It was very inspiring to hear that 40 per cent. of the old boys were serving, and that two of them had rgicle the supreme sacrifice. Alder- man Bennett went on to refer to Nelson and Trafalgar Day, after which he presented prizes to Kathleen Brown and Dudley P. Bayley. He also presented two prizes—given by the Head- master for the Juniors—to Annie Duckworth, of 56, Gorsey-lane, and James Woodhead, of 24, Oldham-street. Moving a vote of thanks, Mr. J. Moore Murray spoke of Alderman Bennett's high 'ideals. The vote was heartily accorded. At the close three cheers were given for the school and the new "Head." During the afternoon A. Greenough sang "The Old Brigade," and recitations were given by Muriel Critchley (" The Eve of Waterloo," by Byron) and Lillie Weston (" Pro Patria," by Owen Seaman). The students sang "-ye. Mariners of England" and "The International. , Hyrum"-</p>
      </div>
