      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 23, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>SERGEANT HORACE MAYES.</p>
        <p>Killed in Action</p>
        <p>German line here, and no sooner did he show himself than he was shot through the head by a German sniper. One small consolation may be that his death was instant and entirely pain- less. He is a very great loss to us, not only to my platoon, but to the whole of the battalion, as the work he has done on many occasions has been excellent."</p>
        <p>So wrote Lieut. H. C. Brown Douglas, of the Rifle Brigade, to Mrs. Mayes, of 26, Plumpton- street ; thus informing her of the death in action of her son, Sergt. Horace Mayes, also of the Rifle Brigade. Letters have also been received from Captain C. E. James, Wesleyan Chap- lain, 60th Field Ambulance, France, and Sergt. J. H. Martindale, also of the Rifle Brigade. " He Was A Brave Lad."</p>
        <p>Captain James' letter ran as follows :—" I am afraid I am the bearer of very bad news to you. It has been my painful duty to bury Horace this afternoon. He was killed in the trenches this morning. I knew him very well. He was one of the most popular men in his, battalion, and every one respected him. He usdd to come to my- services, and it was a real pleasure to know him. He Was a brave lad. He brought honour to his company more than once, and he met his death in An act of bravery which many would not have dared. I do sympathise with you, but I am sure your sorrow will be blended with thankfulness, that you had such a son. I am certain that such men as he will meet with an everlasting reward and a glory that will not. fade."</p>
        <p>Sergeant Martindale wrote :—" Just a few lines to express my heartfelt sympathy with you in the death of yout son Horace, who was killed yesterday afternoon. He was doing some observation work in the trenches when he got hit, and the bullet passed through his brain and killed him instantly.</p>
        <p>"As Brave As A Lion."</p>
        <p>"Your son was my assistant in the platoon, and I can assure you that he was as brave as a lion, and at times inclined to be a little rock- less. No one will miss him more than myself. Kindly extend to all his relatives the sympathy of myself and the rest of the platoon."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Twice Wounded.</head>
        <p>Twice Wounded.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Mayes, who was twenty years of age, was, before the outbreak of war, employed by Messrs. Albert Thorne in the erection of War- rington Bridge. He was a frequent attender at the Bold-street Wesleyan Chapel. He joined the Army on September 19th, 1914, and had been twice wounded before meeting his death. At the present time Sergeant Mayes' father is in Canada.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>PRIVATE HARRY SEED.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE HARRY SEED.</p>
        <p>"Better Soldier Never Li % vd." Say Ilk</p>
        <p>"Old Pals."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <p>PRIVATE H. SEED. Killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <p>I Mr. and Mrs. Samuel geed, of :17, dreenail- ' street, Warrington, have received a communi- cation from the Commanding Officer that Private Harry Seed, King's Own Scottish Bor- derers, was killed in action in the engagement of September 25th. A letter has also been re- ceived by his parents, dated 30th September, from of his, comrades in the same batta. lion, which reads as follows "It is with much regret we have to inform you of the death of your son Harry, who has been killed in the great charge, which our regiment took, part in. We cannot explain to you our feeling, for he is a great loss to us, his old pals. He was very popular and made many - friends in his battalion. A better soldier never lived. He was one, or the best. We may tell you it was a sight that will never be forgotten by those who were in it. It was a miracle how One came out of such a Hell. Keep up your hearth and remember that he died for his King and country. We hope to share with those who mourn the loss of their brave sons."</p>
        <p>Private H. Seed enlisted in the King's Own Scottish' Borderers on September 2nd, 1914, and was drafted to the Front on June 26th, 1915. Previous to his serving the colours he was employed as an apprentice-electrician at Messrs. Vlasto, Clark and Watson, engineers, Stockton Heath, for twelve -ife6tiths: "Before that period he was in the employ of Diem's;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>MISSING.</head>
        <p>MISSING.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE W. T. ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>Whereabouts A Mystery For Nearly</p>
        <p>A Month.</p>
        <p>Mrs. England, of 295, Winwick-roaa, has been officially informed that her husband, Private William Thomas England, of the Royal. Welsh Fusiliers, has been missing since September 25th.</p>
        <p>Private England, who is forty-two years of age, was educated at St. Ann's School. He was formerly employed by Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, Ltd. He enlisted shortly after the outbreak of war, and has been out at the Front about three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>PRIVATE THOMAS LESTER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE THOMAS LESTER.</p>
        <p>Birthday Passed Off Nicely, But Is Now Missing.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. W. B. Lester, 12, Villars-street, Howley, have this week received official notifica- tion from the War Office that their son, Private Thomas Lester, of the sth Royal Irish Regiment, is posted missing and wounded, whilst operating in the Dardanelles on August 19th, 1915. Private Lester is 24 years of age and unmar- ried. Joining the forces in September of last year, he .was drafted to the Mediterranean some time last July. The last communication re- ceived from him by his parents is dated July 23, 1915, wherein he states:</p>
        <p>"Just a few lines to let you know I am all right at present. It is very warm where we are just- now. We have been sailing 12 days, and it is beautiful. How is my brother John going on? It is such a long time since I heard any- thing from him. No doubt when you get this letter I shall be in the thick of it. My birthday passed off very nicely. I hope to celebrate my next one at home."</p>
        <p>Private Lester prior to enlisting was employed as a roller at the Union Tannery, Howley, and as a boy attended the National School. A keen angler, he was a member of the Warrington Anglers' Association, and won molly prizes. He was also an ,excellent exponent of bowls and billiards, besides being one of the most skilful roller skaters in the town. Private Lester's brother, John, who, until the outbreak of war, was in the employ of the Firth Co., Ltd., is at</p>
      </div>
