      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. Hendy Cock On Praying And Working.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Warrington and , District Sunday School Teachers' Association was held on Monday evening in the Church of St. Margaret and All HallOws, Orford. Amongst those present were the Rev. S. U. Stanton, Parish Church, and the Rev. J. Davies, St. Ann's. The service consisted of shortened even- song, conducted by the Vicar, the Rev. 0. Vale Owen, assisted by the. Rev. Canon Willis. The. preacher was the Rev. W. Heady Cock, War:, rington Training College, who took for his text Romans ii, 21, " Thou therefore which teachest "another, teachest thou not thyself? 'Thou that preachest a man must not steal, dost thou steal?" Speaking as a teacher, he said they could- not expect to teach others, unless they studied. They must study Eastern. methods and countries in order to understand and be able to .explain as if they were there. Most people were too apt to rely either altogether on the Holy Ghost or entirely on theit own in- telleet. Both sets of people were wrong. They must use the faculties which had been given them, ,and pray in order to do what they had power to do themselves. Intel- lect would make a clever man, but not a good man. They must not _neglect . the 'spiritual faculties given them. It was useless to try to teach others unless they themselves lived a good life. There was too much "Do As I say, and not as I do." People were apt to follow the actions and not the words of their teachers. Therefore they must be always on guard. Sunday school teachers were never "off duty." They must always do the right thing, not only at school, but in their everyday life and business. They ' must also study the minds into which they wanted to pour knowledge. They must ,remem- ber that a child's mind is small, and say a few things in a great many interesting ways. They must be careful not to surfeit a child, even with good maxims, as it was possible "to have too much of a good thing," and by trying to drive too much into a child's mind, they might turn that child against it. They must be careful in their method of application, and see that the child understood for himself, instead of accept- ing it because the teacher said go. They must not teach anything they did not implicitly believe themselves. Above all things, they must remember the power of prayer. "Work without prayer is Atheism; prayer without work is presumption." The collection was given to the Warrington and District Sunday School, Teachers' Associa- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>WORKING MEN'S MISSION. Coming-of-Age Of The Christian Endeavour Society.</head>
        <p>WORKING MEN'S MISSION. Coming-of-Age Of The Christian Endeavour Society.</p>
        <p>"It is not in length of years, nor in numerical strength that the society's progress will be estimated, but, in the influence it has had on the lives of those uho have been con- nected with it." This was a sentence from the evening sermon, based on the words "I remember," delivered by Mr. W. Mort, of Lymm, on Sunday, who was the preacher at the coming-of-age celebrations of the Christian Endeavour Society associated with the Work- ing Men's Mission, Bank Quay. The services were well attended, and in the evening a solo entitled "Lead, kindly light," was sweetly sung by Miss E. inewitt, while a semi-chorus was rendered in capital style by several mem- bers of the C.E. Society. On Wednesday evening a tea and re-union were held, about 150 being present. An inno- vation during tea. was the handing round of slices of birthday 'cake, giving the proceedings the true nature of a natalitial anniversary. Before leaving the tea tables the Society's health was proposed in felicitous .terms by Mr. Mort, supported by Mr. Hues.ion (secretary of the Warrington Christian Endeavour Union). The President of the Society (Mr. W. Worrall) presided over the re-union gathering, and gave a resume .of the work done by the organisation during its 21 years of service. - He also 'read extracts of messages sent , by old , members congratulating the society -upon reaching its maturity.. Trie speakers were Mr. W. If. Starr (president of the • Wigan C.E. Union), who spoke on "Patriotism and Sacrifice," and Mr. A. Mounfield, whose address .was on "The Christian Endeavour Society and what it stands for." Solos were rendered during the evening by the Misses E. Tanner and 0. Dale. Inter- spersed into the proceedings were three inter- esting presentations, the first two taking the form of books to Mews. T. Dale and W. Worrell, while the third was the handing over into the safe custody of the 'church a 'hand- some silver rose bowl and flower vases, the gift to the C.E. Society of Mrs. A. Edwards, to perpetuate the memory of her parents, the late Mr. and Mrs. T. Taylor.</p>
      </div>
