      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>INSPECTOR'S PRAISE.</head>
        <p>INSPECTOR'S PRAISE.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley presided over the fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians, which was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>WILL THEY GET IT?</head>
        <p>WILL THEY GET IT?</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) reported the receipt of a letter from the Town Clerk of Widnes, with reference to the proposals which are being put forward by the Corporation of that town for the extension of the boundaries of the borough. Mr. Bottomley explained that the application which it was proposed to make would include the taking of a portion of the township of Cuerdley, which was at present within the 'Warrington Union. When he received the letter from Widnes lie at onto wrote to the Town Clerk and pointed out that the resolution of the Town Council gave no reasons why the application for an extension of boundaries was being put forward and 'asking to be informed why the borough wished to take the particular part of the Union named from the Guardians. So far no reply had been re- ceived to that letter, and jie thought it would be best to wait for one before 'proceeding further. The Board agreed, the Clerk adding that his own opinion was that the present was not at all • an opportune time for considering questions of boundary extension. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL AND OLD AGE PENSIONS.</head>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL AND OLD AGE PENSIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chas. Dukes, the secretary of the -War- rington Trades and Labour Council, wrote that at a meeting of his Council, held on October 12th, the following resolution was unanimously adopted: "We, the Warrington Trades and. Labour Council, appeal to the Local Govern- ment Board to suspend, at least for the, period of the war, the clause in the old Age Pensions Act which prohibits old age pensioners from obtaining Poor Law Relief and to empower Boards of Guardians to give, grants in aid of old age pensioners."</p>
        <p>The Chairman: It is only a matter of informa- tion; it needs no resolution to-day,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>DEARER MILK!</head>
        <p>DEARER MILK!</p>
        <p>Mr. John 'Willow, of the - Farmers' Stores Dairy, Bewsey-street, wrote that owing to the increase in the price of milk he was actually paying more for his milk than the 2s. 9d. per dozen quarts at which he contracted to supply it to the Workhouse. Consequently he was in for a serious loss unless the Board consented to his request to reconsider the matter of the tender and grant him an increased price, which. he suggested, should be 3s. 2d. or thereabouts. Moreover, he would point out that when he entered into the contract with the Board the military hospital (Whitecross) did not exist as such.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Mather thought there was a great deal of justice in Mr. Witlow's application, for when he entered into the contract nobody knew. what was going to happen. He proposed that the request be acceded to. Speaking AS a farmer he considered that in.' the future the question would not be one of price, but one as to whether it was possible to get milk at all. Mr. J. W. Knowles seconded, remarking that one had only to look at his daily paper to see that the farmers were getting rid of their dairy' stock all round, one cause being that farmers could not get people to milk the cows, and another that the cost of fodder for the animals was practically double what it was ten 'years age, _ _ _</p>
        <p>-Mr. Shaw thought the contractor's request reasonable.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. C. Brooks moved as an amendment that the Board pay 3s. and not 3s. 2d. per dozen quarts.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that the Board's business relations with Mr. Willow—and they extended over many years—had always been of the most satisfactory character, and the quality of the milk he bad supplied had invariably been excel- lent. Personally, he thought Mr. Witlow's suggestion a very reasonable one.</p>
        <p>Eventually Mr. Brooks amended his amend- ment' to read to the effect that 3s. should he paid for the Workhouse milk and 3s. 3d. for that supplied to the military hospital. The .original' • prom*tion, however, was carried.</p>
        <p>SHOULD EVERYBODY KNOW?</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Cole, assistant overseer for the Urban District of New ton-in-Makerfield, wrote stating that when he submitted statements of accounts relating to that parish to the overseers they in- quired for the lists of " indoor" and " outdoor " poor, or whether he had anything to report thereon. Consequently, he desired to know whether those lists would be supplied, as he could not find any lists referring to this year. It would appear, in fact, that the issuing of such lists must have been discontinued for a long time. There was a strong feeling that, as the overseers have to raise the money for the Guardians, they were entitled to receive some account as. to its distribution, a . contention which articles in the General Orders of Accounts seemed to support. "And," the letter concluded, "although they have decided not to pay any sum under the Contribution Grders until the lists are received, I trust that there will be no necessity. for the overseers to do any- thing further in the matter." During the course of the discussion which en- sued, it was pointed out that the information required could only be given by the direction of the Guardians, who had discretion in the matter.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that at one. time it was the custom to display the lists of recipients of relief on the church doors, but the present tendency was all in the other direction.</p>
        <p>The Hey. A. M. Mitchell —Moved that the in- formation asked for be supplied, contending that the people of Newton-in-Makerfield had a perfect right to know how the money required from their district Was spent, as they might then be able to ssuggest_ economies.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Billing see-ended, but on a vote being taken the reverend gentleman's proposal was defeated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INSPECTOR'S COMMENTS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INSPEC- TOR'S COMMENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Elias, the Local Government Board In- spector, who had been present throughout the meeting, took the opportunity, at the close, to say a few words to the members. These, he said, were days when there was much to do and but little to say. He would like to acknow- ledge the action of the Board in-setting aside their new hospital for wounded soldiers. He had had to do with offering and .suggesting places in the district where soldiers could -be accommodated and treated, and the Whitecross Hospital was one of the places which he did not think could have been set aside. He was grati- fied to find that he had been mistaken, and that the new block had made such a good military hospital. As to the Workhouse itself, he thought the Board had made a most interesting departure in altering the dietary table. .He had recommended other Unions to send to War- rington for copies of the revised table, and he understood that it was in Much request. Mr; G. Mather: Have we not made it a copy- right? (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>'ri`he Inspector : am afraid you can't copy- right it now, for I have been pirating it a good deal. Mr. Elias added that he had been pleased to find that everything at the Padgate Cottage Homes was going on satisfactorily. He found. there_ a large number of young children who were formerly in the Workhouse, and who con- stituted a ,very great responsibility for the staff, but that was no time for suggesting that the staff to increased, and he was pleased to note that the existing officials would do their best to carry on in the future .as they were doing to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
