      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 23, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>A;t...1i.:•••044.:••:..):4 ::'•:t... 0 4:. 4, 4,4,4+ 4.4:,..:••2. +44+4,0 +++.4004.0.0 4&gt;+++04++++1•44...+4)..›.) ...PO. '+++4,..:•++,.:••:...)•:••:.•:•..)+4.4.0. ++.:••••:•++.4.0.4•4•04..04,0).***++++*4 +40.2.44:04.•440 +.:••:- 44 ; - • - -- - .4* .4, .., ' .;:,.. - 3 : , ...:::. ::-.,., ..,-„,-., :::::-1. ~ .; ~„. - 0 ''''''. 'i- A -‘ S _ WORL,Di , %...„-.,- ~....,.....„,,.: .0 :‘... ~ ,• .4? , . • . - I</p>
        <p>Women In Munition Factories.</p>
        <p>The employment of women in munition fac- tories is not as new as it may appear to be. During the. Boer War many of the automatic tools in the Midland factories were worked 1)3, girls and women. Comparatively little skill was demanded by the machines then in use. Those of to-day are quite different, and the strictest attention and considerable skill, combined with quickness and intelligence, are imperative if success is to be attained. Sir William Beardmore has lately given his ex- perience of. the eight hundred girls 'employed in his factory at Glasgow. The results are astonishing and surpass expectations. "Al- though new to the work the girls became adepts in the processes to be carried out in a remarkably short space of time. Their output has been wonderfully high, and the various operations have been performed exactly to gauge. Only one operation in the making of a shell is performed by men. Autumn Items of Dress.</p>
        <p>Attempts have been made in some quarters to change the outline of the now universal full short skirt; with what success time will show. The hip yoke is losing favour, which is unfor- tunate since it is becoming to most women. That fact probably accounts for its disappear- ance, since, as soon as a style becomes general, La Mode casts it aside, and introduces another that may or may not be as admirable. Materials for winter wear are going up in price, although at present- there appears to -be no shortage in the. supply. An even nore serious matter is the enhanced price of gloves and footwear, because both are indispensable to the comparatively poor, as well as the rich woman.. A novel feature in trimmings for dark dresses is a small amount of embroidery in the brilliant colours that form the flags of the Allies. Florence green is a rich and beautiful shade which is _frequently used for afternoon and evening blouses, and with a delicate tracing of scarlet on the collar and cuffs a very rich effect is obtained. Gold and silver braid is used also effectively on evening gowns .of black or coloured net. An Economical Garment.</p>
        <p>The merits of the blouse are more apparent this season than ever before, for in addition to being a 'comfortable and adaptable garment, it is in every sense .of the word an economical one. That its popu- larity is waxing, rather than waning is evident to all who have taken note of this winter's dis-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE,</p>
        <p>War and Furnishing. Michaelmas Day was this year remarkable in London and its suburbs for few changes of residences. Many families, however, would leave their large houses, which are now too expensive, if they could find smaller ones to suit them. But London is attracting Tnore people, in spite of Zeppelin raids, and as build- ing operations are practically at a standstill there is a scarcity of low-rented houses in every district.. Conventional furnishing, too, is giv- ing place to what may be called fieakish economy, and newly-married couples are fitting up town fiats and small houses like week-end cottages in times -of peace. Floors are stained, walls distempered, carpets are dispensed with, and the furniture is of the simplest descrip- ' tion, not one unnecessary article being bought.. But many of these modest 'homes are quite artistic, and excellent taste is displayed in the colouring and arrangement, and the choice of the simple, but often comfortable furniture. The Dark Winter.</p>
        <p>A London paDer suggests that we should put the daylight-saving proposal into operation. That might have been a useful idea six months ago, but with the winter at hand there is no spare daylight to save. Many Londoners go to bed earlier than before, owing to the darkened streets at night, and other disabilities due to the war, and they consequently desire to get up earlier. Early rising is at all times a great virtue, but it is not much use to get up at five or six o'clock in the morning to make use of the daylight when there is none to speak of until eight o'clock. Mr. Willett's suggestion to put on the clock applied only to the summer half-year, when by lying in bed we do waste the morning daylight. Upon the whole, Lon- don appears to be less affected by the war than most visitors would expect. The restaurants, picture palaces and theatres are mostly as crowded as ever, and it is only at night that the outward appearance of the Metropolis is so changed. It then almost looks as if the ninth plague of Egypt had fallen upon the city.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>lIONIE NOTES.</head>
        <p>lIONIE NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes And Useful Suupsilons. Pillau.</p>
        <p>This is a Persian dish, which practically con- sists of savoury rice and either boiled loin of mutton, fowl or rabbit, the two former being usually preferred to the rabbit. Boil the mut- ton with a small onion in the water,- and when done strain sufficient of the liquor in which to boil the, rice, allowing about one pint, or rather more, of the liquor to a teacupful of rice. Mean- while keep the meat warm in the remaining liquor, and while the rite is cooking slice and fry two onions in hot fat with about a dozen raisins, i'itoned and split. When the rice is tender stir to it salt, cayenne, cinnamon and cloves to taste. Put the meat on a dish, with the rice round it, and some on the top, and garnish with the fried onions and raisins some slices of hard-boiled egg and some blanched and split almonds.----" Guardian." Baked Lentil Mould.</p>
        <p>Another delieious way of using red lentils is to take 41b. of these; measure them by means Of a cup, and put them, with an equal quantity of water, into a double saucepan. Add a little salt and pepper, and cook slowly until the lentils are tender. Mince two or three shallots or medium-sized onion, and fry it, until it just begins to take colour, in 2oz. of good dripping; then add 3oz. of fine breadcrumbs, and fry them in the fat for a few minutes. Mix these with the lentils, add pepper, salt, and a grate of nutmeg, and stir in a well-whisked .egg. Put into a greased mould, and bake in a moderate oven. Served with potatoes and some kind of green vegetable this is excellent. If desired, more than one egg may be used.</p>
        <p>The Price Of Sugar. . The Royal Commission on the Sugar Supply wish it to be known that in charging 6d. per lb. for cube sugar retailers are not making any larger profit than before, as, owing to the neces- sity of restricting the demand in view of the limited supplies available, the Commission has advanced the wholesale price of that class of sugar to 50s. per cwt. The large profit that this price represents on the cost of production will accrue, not to the manufacturers, but to the general public; and in addition it will materially assist towards maintenance of the reduced level of prices that has recently been established for granulated and other classes of sugar which con- stitute the great mass of the sugar consumed. The proper retail prices for sugar in ordinary circumstances are at present as follows: Granu- lated, 4d. per lb; white Javas, 31d. per lb; cubes,</p>
        <p>61. An additional Id. per lb. may in certain cases be justified, but in the absence of special circumstances the Commission will cut off the supplies of any retailer who exceeds the prices mentioned. The public are asked to report tq the Commissioners any cases of overcharge. Wheatmeal Bread.</p>
        <p>Sieve 41b. of ordinary flour into a bowl, tnen add lb. of wheaten_ meal (unsieved). Now add one teaspoonful of caster sugar, half a tea- spoonful of salt, one small teaspoonful of bicar- bonate of soda, and one small teaspoonful of cream of tartar. Rub in 10z.,0f salt butter or lard, and one teaspoonful of syrup, and then stir in sufficient sweet milk to form a good dough. Turn on to a floured board, form neatly into shape, and place in an oiled or buttered cake or loaf tin. Set before the fire to rise for a quarter of an hour, place in a moderate *oven and bake for one hour. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <p>0000000,:•000000.00 00004.0 0 . : Children' 8 Corner. 0 O 4. O 0 + # O Conducted by UNCLE NED. 4. # •:. .#########44.4l•oo*.####</p>
        <p>Children, Join This Corner! Enter The,Competitions! Write Your Best! Always Be Glad! Uncle Ned; " Examiner," Warrington.</p>
