      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>FAVOURITE SONGS.</head>
        <p>FAVOURITE SONGS.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,- 478, Kathleen lioscow, 1, Longshaw-street, does not win the prize in our "Favourite Song" Competition, but made the best choice, and her letter must he put first by any old uncle who loves his King:— My favourite song is the anthem of dear old England, that is " God Save the King." This has been sung by many of our brave boys whd are fighting at present in France,.. Belgium, Russia, Servia, Egypt and the Dardanelles. They are all fighting-iith the motto in their hearts "Right is Might." • After .it must come the letter from 511 Niece Gladys Taylor, 25, Fitzherbert-street:— How pleased I am to write about my favourite song. It is the patriotic song `.‘ Rule, Britannia, Britannia rules the waves; Britons never, never, never shall be slaves." The truth of the words in this song have been proved over and over again during this war. It has meant our salvation, and we shall go on singing it when all is over. The winner of the "Favourite Song" competition is 517 Nephew SAM BROWN, 40, Wakefield-street My favourite song is "The Marseillaise." It was written by Rouget d'lsle, and is called from the town of Marseilles in the South of France. This famous Song was written for a march through Paris in the time of Louis XVl.,in the year 1793. It is now known all over England. Yes, and a splendid sign of national friend- ship it is, that one country's national anthem should be known all over another country, isn't it? 35G Nephew Joseph Kitchen, 41, Bewsey-street, wrote:— My favourite song is the French National An- them. It is a very patriotic song, and is sung mostly by the French. In this terrible war it would be charitable to sing such songs to the wounded French soldiers, as other such songs to other soldiers. Another member chose this song: 518 Nephew Harold Brown, 40, Wakefield-street : lam writing about " The Marseillaise." It had jts name by the, town in France. It was written by a French man, and was sung while maraing through Paris.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>Her Qualification. Several men were talking about how they happened to marry. "I married my wife," said one, " because-she was different frofti''any Other woman I had ever met." "How was that?" .chorused the others. "She was the only woman I met who would have me!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <p>4</p>
        <p>A Fact Which Everyone Has Noticed. "I wonder," mused the young father, " what there is in a baby's make-up that prompts him to drop things. It isn't really dropping though —it's throwing. My baby is good about sleep- ing and behaving when there is company, but everything he can snatch he immediately flings to the floor. I've noticed and known a lot of others, too, who do the same thing. It's not only the joy of throwing, but the delight in seeing somebody pick the stuff up. Babies cer- tainly seem to take a fiendish delight in watch- ing their fathers and mothers or nurses pick up the toys and other things which they throw out of their beds, carriages and chairs. My boy used to be quite pleased with a rubber toy at- tached by a string to his carriage so that it just escaped the ground. He would grin and dangle it for hours. Now he yells as soon as he discovers it is fastened, and the minute we give it to him loose, hang! it goes on to the ground while he laughs aloud in his joy. There's prob- ably a reason, and the psychologists will dis- cover it some day."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <p>plays. Blouses and skirts are shown in endless variety, but all are simple in style and service- able. Shirts depend on cut and finish, quite as much as upon decoration, ,for individuality and distinction. Pretty frills and handsome buttons with chic little vests, form the ususl decorations. For the tailored shirt crepe de chene, or ninon, are in the front rank, as far as materials are concerned, although Japanese and other silks are still largely used. The trans- parent blouse is in favour again, but to be really successful—that is to ensure the required soft effect—care must be bestowed on the camisoles worn with it. Two of these under- garments are necessary, and the upper one must also be transparent, the other may be of chiffon, crepe de clime, or very thin silk.</p>
        <p>The Transparent Blouse. A transparent blouse should consist of two materials, cut exactly the same size, and laid one over the other. Underneath these layers a net ,slip is required, and another of silk—very thin—gives the softened effect fashion demands. Shadow lace, white, ecru or black, is a favourite fabric of the moment over ninon. Or a charm- ing and very richipappearance is given by bands of coloured ribbon velvet, laid on net, chiffon or . veiling. Gold lace and embroidery are used in the same way, and a narrow width serves admirably. Padded velvet and silk buttons, suspended from quite a long stem of twisted silk, are suitable for this style of blouse. Some- times the buttons are embroidered in a tiny device with coloured beads. If a design which requires a dainty vest is chosen Valenciennes lace is preferred to any other, on account of its lightness. Elbow sleeves are seen on a number of these blouses, otherwise the bishop type prevails, the fulness being drawn in to form a deep frill which falls over the hand. A now collar which is becoming to the face has points in the shape of petals of flowers. A Woman's Sacrifice..</p>
        <p>No more heroic deed of man or woman could be imagined than that performed by, a young Welsh lady, Miss K. Margaret Davies. A test case was wanted for an antidote discovered by a professor in the Pasteur Institute, Paris, for gas poison gangrene. Miss Davies is a clever bacteriologist on the staff, and without inform- ing her confreres, infected herself with the poison and then telegraphed to the Professor to acquaint him of the fact. ITO at onto adminis- tered the antidote—hydro.chlorate of quinine. which proved quite effectual, and in twenty-four hours Miss Davies had recovered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular Language.</p>
        <p>Care Of The Teeth.</p>
        <p>The teeth should be cleaned every wonting before breakfast with a soft brush and luke- warm water; a little salt may be added to the water a couple times a week. After each meal brush lightly with the.soft brush and water for a • few seconds and the mouth should be thoroughly washed out with a'weak solution of permanganate of potash or other antiseptic once a day. To scrub the teeth more especially if the brush be hard several times daily is in- jurious to their structure. Thejeeth should be cleaned and looked after by a good deutist at least once a year.</p>
        <p>How To Purify The Blood, For the making of pure blood the first essen- tial is pure food, and not blood or blood-cou- taining food, as the mistaken notion is among people who eat great quantities of meat for blood-building purposes. If the blood has been greatly reduced in quantity, it is important to take food rich in proteins; that is, food contain- ing a lArge proportion of nitrogen. This is fur- nished in abundance by 'many of the natural foods which_ have been prepared for man's use. Among the choicest of these are nuts, especially almonds, peanuts, Turkish hazlenuts, or filberts, pecans, and all nuts from which the shells and skins can be readily removed. A pound of nuts contains more blood-forming material than a pound and a half of beefsteak. Peas, beans and lentils are also splendid blood formers; cell- taming more than one-fourth their weight cf blood-making material, and each pound -ual in food value to three pounds of beef. Whole-meal bread and gluten preparations of all sorts are also good blood-making foods. All foods which hinder digestion, and which give rise to fermentation or other disturbances. must be carefully avoided, as the acids formed by the souring of foods serves to lessen the alkalinity of the blood, and thus to deteriorate its quality. This is not true of the acids found in fruits. These are food substances, and un- less taken in very great excess are in no wise harmful, but, on the other hand, are highly beneficial.. These acid fruits. encourage the action of the kidneys, and thus aid in the removal of poisons, while they at the same time disinfect the stomach and intestines, and thxts prevent the formation of poisons by fer- mentative and putrefactive processes.—Dr. Kellogg, in "Good Health."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <p>received. My favourite song is entitled "Tipperary." It is a very popular air, and is known by almost every person in Warrington. This song is called after a town in Ireland. Soldiers when they are going away sing this lively song. I would not be at all surprised if you knew it.</p>
        <p>507 Niece Louie Wilson-, 27, Golborne-street, also chose ". It's a Lon t Way to Tipperary ":</p>
        <p>Many sing it, but they do so _because it is a</p>
        <p>great favourite with the general public. The reason it is. my favourite is because it is so popular with our brave lads at the Front, and because it refers to a spot dear to the hearts of all Irishborn people. Its meaning is quite' true; it is a long way from the Front or the site of war, and it keeps many hearts up to think they will come back victorious.</p>
        <p>482 Niece nettle Ditelifield, 51, Tanner's-lane, sent this My favourite song is entitled " Keen the Home Fires Burning." It was composed by Ivor</p>
        <p>Novell°, and was written ,by Lena Guilbert Ford. It is very appropriate in time of war, and is sung by our " Tommies " in the trenches.</p>
        <p>483 Niece Lily Booth, 47, Green-street, said</p>
        <p>My favourite song is a song that I learnt at school, and it is called "The Drum Taps." I like it because it is a patriotic song, and is meant for the boys who can, and have not, enlisted. Another reason is that it communicates with the war. 496 Niece Margaret Massey, 80, Bostoel-- street, says My favourite is called "The Young Recruit." Although it is an old song it is a very beautiful one. It is about a young man who is going to fight for his country, and he is thinking of the things he will see on the battle field. He tells his sweetheart that he is a soldier, and ,that she must not weep, for he will soon be home again; and he tells her that there is nothing like the life of a young recruit.</p>
        <p>500 Niece May Williams, 26, Garibaldi-street, posted me this:—</p>
        <p>My favourite is "An Englishman's Home." It is about our Colonies and their men. In the chorus it -says: " Rally round the flag, whate'er may befall." I think it is a nice chorus, and it has come true now. We _want our men to rally round the flag and to follow it to victory. If the men did not, we should be beaten.</p>
        <p>380 Niece Jenny Dunlop, 52, Chester-road, sends word : My favourite song, which is a patriotic one, is called " Soldiers and Sailors." 1 like it because it contains very true and beautiful lines. It was composed by Adele Milligan Allmann, who is a teacher in one of the London schools. The pro- fits of this song were divided between the National Relief Fund and Queen Mary's Work for WoMen Fund. 509 Niece Florence Crompton, 4, Factoty-lane, writes :</p>
        <p>'Twas with a broken heart that a woman bid a last adieu to her soldier husband, who, in spite of his trying to keep back the tears, could do noth- ing but let them flow freely from his eyes. At the farewell, my favourite song rang forth from the poor woman's lips." She sang it so sweetly and with such-feeling•that no one -could-help cry- ing, The two sang to each other, and then with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>G , : 't 't .... .., • ..,•-.• --, ,-,t, t '.--:',.. ,i 0 R : :, -,.. .:, ~.,: ~_ ,::. 5 .; , , ------</head>
        <p>G , : 't 't .... .., • ..,•-.• --, ,-,t, t '.--:',.. ,i 0 R : :, -,.. .:, ~.,: ~_ ,::. 5 .; , , ------</p>
        <p>AND DISTRICT PUBLIC.</p>
        <p>RM. EDWARDS, the Famous Eyesight Speoialisit, of London and Paris, has arrived. OPENING MONDAY NEXT, for One Week Only, at the WINNIARLEIGH CAFE, Sankey Street.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edwards, tho London eye-testing expert, has arrived in Warrington. His great scherm to afford Warrington people a genuine oppor- tunity of securing expert counsel and advice regarding their eyesight free of all charge begins Monday at the Winrnarleigh Cafe, Sankey-street. For the next week Mr. Edwards places his ser- vices freely and generously at the disposition of Warrington.</p>
        <p>clear--his services are free to all. Every man, woman and child in Warrington should clearly understand that they can come to the Winmarleigh Cafe, Sankey-street, at any time during next week and consult Mr. Edwards freely and for nothing. He will test their eyes for them by the latest scientific methods, will examine each individual case thoroughly and carefully, and will give the best and soundest advice. If glasses are not neces- sary for them, Mr. Edwards will frankly say so. If glasses are needed, he will prescribe for them, and tell them exactly what kind and strength will suit them best.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edwards has his headquarters in London and Paris, where during a long experience of optical work he has tested the eyes of countless thousands of people, amongst them some of the highest and most prominent members of the political and social world. Warrington is a great industrial centre, where thousands of workers have to labour long and late hours at the machine, the work-table, or in the office, frequently at close work involving a very con- siderable strain on the eyesight. There are consequently a great number of people in War- rington who have urgent need of expert advice and guidance regarding their eyes.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edwards will do more, he will ensure the prescriptions he makes being attended to properly. It can readily be understood that when he has advised a certain shape, strength, and quality of lens, it is impossible for him to be sure that his instructions are carried out unless the glasses are prepared under his direc- tion and supervision. Mr. Edwards therefore brings with him from London a fully-qualified staff of optical experts to prepare his prescriptions when they ars required. This staff enables Mr. Edwards to turn out beautifully-made glasses of the best quality of material and excellence of workman- ship. And, owing to the extent of the business he does, Mr. Edwards can offer this workman- ship and finish to the people of Warrington at remarkably low charges. For example, the average cost of the lenses, which are ground from specially-prepared colourless crown crystal, is 4s. only. Of course, in astigmatic and special cases extra charges must be made, but the prices are always extremely low con- sidering the high quality. There is no differ. once in the charges to rich or poor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>Import:in! \taller.</head>
        <p>Import:in! \taller.</p>
        <p>To the thousands of workers upon whose sight depends their existence, the proper care and attention of the eyes is an all-important matter, and, should glasses be necessary, their selection requires the utmost circumspection. Too much care cannot be taken to see that they exactly meet the wearer's requirements. Un- suitable glasses are far worse than no glasses at all. The eyes should first be carefully and conscientiously tested by the latest scientific methods, and with the aid of the most modern sight-testing apparatus. Some of the latter ore very elaborate and expensive instruments, and they are not always available in provincial towns. It therefore happens that rough-and- ready methods of testing the sight have frequently to be resorted to, or a pair of glasses are chosen haphazard because they seem to assist the vision. The inevitable result is that in hundreds of cases the glasses chosen are entirely unsuitable, and after they have been worn a month or two cause serious eye trouble.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>National Benefactor.</head>
        <p>A National Benefactor.</p>
        <p>A specialist usually wants two or three guineas for a consultation, and to a great majcrily of workers this has been out of the question.</p>
        <p>It is no longer so. Mr. Edwards is devotin one week to Warrington, and during that time will be entirely at the disposal of any and every one who wishes to consult him, and he will make no charge whatever for his services.</p>
        <p>Services Free To All. Mr. Edwards wishes that point made quite</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>POSSIBILITIES OF HAY :1\1) A WOODEN BOX.</head>
        <p>THE POSSIBILITIES OF HAY :1\1) A WOODEN BOX.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the question of heating and fire is not. SO acute in the _country as in town, but everywhere ladies are interested in knowing bow to cook a dinner without much work or trouble, and for this reason I am going to mention the making of a "hay box," says a writer in "Farm and Home." With such a box you can prepare a dinner without a fire. Procure a good sized box and well paper it inside so as to cover all cracks, then well pack with hay, leaving a nest- like centre to hold the saucepan. You will require a feather or hay cushion large enough to fill the box well after the saucepan has been placed into position, and also a lid for the box itself, although this is not absolutely impera- tive. This, then, is all that is required for a "boy box." To use it, supposing you wish to have porridge for breakfast, cook it in the ordinary way on the stove or fire, for about ten minutes, then while at boiling point place it in the hay box and 'well cover with the hay cushion, leave all night, and in the morning you will find the,porridge done "to a turn" and smoking' hot. ready to eat. Stews require cook- ing from to lof an hour before being packed into the hay box, and should be left there at least four hours to finish. It is the knowledge that, whilu you are, perhaps, absent from home, or doing work about the house, the hay box is attending to the dinner, which cannot under any circumstances burn or spoil, which is so satis- factory. A few experiments, and you will soon ascertain the length of time required for cook- ing in this method. Never attempt to put any- thing in the hay box before cooking it a little at first, and also seeing it is at boiling heat when placed in the box, as otherwise it will not do at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <p>bitter sorrow they parted in tears. The song was called "I'm Going Away to Foreign Regions." 504 Niece Annie Parker, 7, Railway Institute, writes the second best letter : My favourite song is " Home, Sweet Home." The words of this song are true, because there is no place better than a sweet home.</p>
        <p>503 Nioco Florence Smith, writes froni 18,</p>
        <p>My favourite song is " Home, Sweet Home." John Howard Payne was the author of this most beautiful song. To my mind the words are most beautiful and impressive. They are full of love and sympathy, and the humblest of people have been known to burst in tears at the lovely tune and always join in the chorus. It is a very great favourite amonethe soldiers that are now fight- ing at the Front, for at the last concert given by some of our noted singers at the Front some of our soldiers asked that this song might be sung. No greater tribute could be made to any man than was made to Mr. Payno• in the words which are inscribed on his tombstone:</p>
        <p>Sure when thy gentle spirit fled To realms beyond the azure dome, With arms outstretched God's Angel s Aid: Welcome to Heaven's Home, Sweet Home. 514 Niece Sarah Cook, it, Oakland-street, writes to me:—</p>
        <p>My favourite song is "The Light of Day." It is a very pitiful piece of music; it tells us about a blind boy who wishes his 'day to come when he should see the light of day. The first time I heard it was when I went to a birthday party. It sounded so well on a phonograph that I told father about it, and he bought it for the gramo- phone.</p>
        <p>505 Niece Louie IN.linsholl, 29, Wakefield-street, puts this:— It is called "A Dolly and a Coach." .Itis a most beautiful song, and has such a lovely tune. If-ever there was a song that I like best it is this one. I learned. it at school, and I am delighted when we are told to sing it.</p>
        <p>478 Niece Sarah Stephens, 15, Hoyle-street. chooses :</p>
        <p>My favourite is called " Daffodils a-Blowing," a most beautiful song, with beautiful -words and music. We are learning it at school.</p>
        <p>FOUR NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Besides Nephew Joseph Higgins, whom I have already mentioned, we have three more new members this week.</p>
        <p>Florence Crompton. 4, Factory-lane, says her brother would liko to become a member. Very good. I welcome him,. and his number- will be 520. Will 'loosen(' me his name, please? Then Niece Mona Archer says her prize has enabled her to get two new members: 521 Niece Mond Ingham (13), 35, Gladstone- street; and 522, Niece Hilda Smith (13), 121, Bostock- street. These I very heartily welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>THIS WEEK: FAVOURITE ANIMAL.</head>
        <p>THIS WEEK: FAVOURITE ANIMAL.</p>
        <p>This week's letter is on "Your Favourite Animal." Just write -a nice short letter, please. Closing day, Monday.• Your loVing • • UNCLE NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>Ten' Years' Guarantee.</head>
        <p>Ten' Years' Guarantee.</p>
        <p>One point to be remembered is that purchasers of these glasses can at any time write to or call at Mr. Edwards' head establishment, 12, Piccadilly Arcade, London, W., and have their eyes re-examined and the lenses changed, if necessary, free of all charge. As a matter of fact, each client is given a ten years' guarantee to that effect. It is furthermore Mr. Edwards' intention to pay Warrington an annual visit, when he will be able to personally make any changes which natural causes have made neces- sary to his clients. -</p>
        <p>Mr. Edwards' Consulting Rooms are at the WINMARLEIGH CAFE, SANKEY-STREET, and will be open for One Week—Monday, October 25th, to Saturday, October 30th, to the Warrington public. The hours of consultation are from 10 to 1 in the morning and from 2 to 7 in the afternoon. Special appointments may be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>EASTERN LIFE.</head>
        <p>EASTERN LIFE.</p>
        <p>Mounfield On Customs, Parables And Metaphors.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Mounfield is delivering a series of Friday tvening lectures which are being given under the title, "Eastern Life as a Bible Commentary," the purpose of which is to describe the life and habits of Palestine for the help of Bible students. The subject of the second lecture was "Eastern Agriculture." The land- system .of Syria was described and the method by which the villagers obtained their land. Much of the land was held under a com- munal system, and tile villagers drew lots for the various patches of 'land. This explained the Bible references to the "lines falling in pleas- ant places" and to the removal or maintaining of landniarks. Palestine had two seasons—the rainy season and the dry -season. The plough- ing must be done in the cold and rainy weather, hence the Bible statement that the sluggard who would not plough because of the cold should beg in the harvest. The sowing was done before the ploughing, and the plough cut the ground and covered up the seed. An interesting descrip- tion was given of the yoke, and many Bible pas- sages which refer to "Taking the yoke" and being "unequally yoked" were thereby ex- plained. The parable of the sower was found to be much more full of teaching by a knowledge of an Eastern field, and the statement that some seed brought forth a hundredfold was explained. The Orientals suffer so many losses that one- third is allowed for loss and one-third for blast- ing and mildew. Seed which multiplied itself by ten would therefore be described as produc- ing thirtyfold, and that which brought forth thirty-three of its kind would be said to have yielded a hundredfold. At the reaping a portion was always left aside "for God," and was left for the stranger, the poor and the gleaners. The corn that was within reach of the paths was free for all, and Jest's and the disciples followed the common habit in-gathering the ears as they passed by. The threshing was done upon a threshing floor— a raised-piece of land or hill top—and the wheat was trodden by oxen and threshed by a "tribuins," that is, a flat board studded with iron. • The winnowing was the process of separat- ing the chaff and wheat, which was tossed into the air and the chaff " driven away " by the wind. The -sifting was the separation of the wheat from the husks, and was performed by the women. The " sealing " referred to in the Bible was the device for marking ,the heaps of corn to protect it against the thief. This ex- plained the metaphor "pealed unto the day of redemption." The cultivation of the grape was also described. The wine press and the tread- ing of grapes,- with the making of sour wine (the " vinegar " which was given to Jesus on the Cross) wore found to explain several Bible texts. Among many Bible stories which Mr. Mounfield introduced as being illuminated by a knowledge of Eastern agriculture were the story of Ruth and Boaz, David and his threshing floor, and the passages " Thei shall beat their swords into ploughshares," "I have trodden the winepress alone," "Cast thy bread upon the waters," "The lion Shall eat straw," were explained. The lectures are under the auspices of the. Sunday School Union, and are being delivered in Friars', Green Schoolroom. The subject of next Friday's address is "Town Life in the East."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
