      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>A TotteAng Empire.</p>
        <p>I have enjoyed the advantage of hearing the lecture which the Rev. J. T. Wardle Stafford, D.D., is todeliver at Bold-street WesloYan Church next Monday week. Beyond com- mending it would not be fair to anticipate it; but, while I have no intention of "giving the' show away," I may state generally that Dr. Stafford quaintly describes Germany as " a dead angel." It is common knowledge that her military officers are the most arrant snobs in the world, and he gives an amusing instance from the pre-war experience of a friend of his own who was dining at a Berlin hotel when a party of them entered, whereupon the waiter went up to him and said the military gentle- men did not purpose to offer any objection to his presence; in other words, they regarded it as an act of condescension to allow a civilian who was paying for his oft dinner .to sit in the same room with them!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>" Zepp." Experiences.</head>
        <p>" Zepp." Experiences.</p>
        <p>The other day I met a young official of the highways department of the St. Helens Cor- poration who had a memorable adventure under the big Zeppelin raid. He was in Lon- don for his final examination, and the end of the building in which he had lodging was struck by a bomb. He was himself in a theatre at the time, and the police kept the audience inside until the Zeppelin had passed. In the course of the last few months I have encountered four others who have had similar experiences. One was :i clergyman who was spending a holiday far from here, of course (the names of these places have not been allowed to appear in print, and rightly so, therefore I shall not indicate the localities), and he had a narrow escape. An- other was a stationmaster in West Yorkshire who had been home on a pop visit, and his part of the town was raided that very night. The others were a Lancashire man and his wife who had acted as club stewards in that same district. They told me enough to satisfy one that this form of German "frightfulness" is utterly futile; all the same, the account of a recent " rehearsal " in their own country warrants the conclusion that reprisals on one of their communities would leave the Teutonic inhabitants panic-stricken. A Foolish Exhibition.</p>
        <p>Last week's story of the meeting of an auto- mobile and a load of hay in a narrow road reminded me of a similar occurrence in a town in which I used to live. The pony carts of rival vendors of ice-cream simultaneously entered a back street from opposite direc- tions, and, as it was impossible for them to pass each other and neither would give way, the display of temper was a sight for the gods. With the " barrows" drawn close together, the respective drivers remained stupidly glaring at one another until the police removed the obstruction—and the scandal— by making each of the silly fellows back out by the way he had come.</p>
        <p>Laughable Slips. On Monday morning I met a worried mother who had evidently left home on a little errand, and, her departure being noticed by her child, who was playing in the street, he ran after her. Just as I passed, she turned upon the kiddie and exclaimed, " Here, ,if you're going to follow me like this, you'll have to come in front, where I can see you!" Said a trades- man of my . acquaintance to his young hope- ful, " If So-and-so calls tell him I'm not in." The scheme worked well enough up to that point, but when the caller proceeded to ask, "What time will he be in?" the boy shouted through the door communicating with the rear, "What time have I to say you'll be in, father ?" Another " Tail." Mr. A. Barnett's demonstration at last week's meting of the Photographic Society somehow recalled a period when the wags of My then neighbourhood were fond of using the word " narrative " as a synonym of " tail." Once the illitcorate wife of a well-to- do citizen of those parts called on a mutual friend. It was the latter's "At home" day, and the hostess's pet dog received the stout, over-dressed newcomer very graciously. Apparently proud of her acquisition, and de- sirous of showing how up-to-date she was, the caller exclaimed, " Oh; see how it's wagging its negative !" The same voluble lady con- fided to. my wife that " harmonium " was "just splendid for cleaning carpets."</p>
        <p>Long-haired Musicians. Last week we had the "justifiable infer- ence" that Nero had long hair because he was " one of the most celebrated fiddlers of his day." A resident in a district in which I formerly rosided was in the habit of thus stating the situation conjured up by the ." Examiner " tit-bit in question: We are too often captivated by oddities of manner, by curious forms of dress or appearance in an artiste. These little weaknesses are often, in a great man, but evidences of a healthy im- patience—a protest against many of the prosaic and meaningless conventions of every- day life. The great man sees so clearly through our conventionalities, and often rightly despises. There is no essential respec- tability in a black coat, nor any particular manliness in regular haircutting. He sees this, and sometimes affects a fantastic cos- tume, flowing locks, an odd kind of collar, or some mannerism of speech or action which shall satisfy his own hatred of mere fashion. But we for our part should remember that the argument works both ways. A man is not a statesman because of his Gladstone collar, any more than he is a gentleman because his clothes fit him. We should therefore look beyond the mere eccentricities of an artist, and inquire if ho has a real message to deliver to us, if he does deliver it, if it reaches and benefits us. It is not the diamonds of the singer or the velvet jacket of the player that matters. After all, Nero might have affected "long, flowing locks" and yet h.slve been so poor a violinist as to have merited the com- ment of the wit who, tossing a coin to an itinerant fiddler, told him that one "scraper" at the door was sufficient. A Chestnut—of the Oldest.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding that other little story in last week's issue respecting the show of resignation on a bored wife's part on being told by her " hubby " that he had some " chestnuts "for her, that interesting column about the battle of Hastings almost compels one to repeat that ancient " wheeze" con- cerning the old lady who, when the guide of the tourist party she accompanied impres- sively said "This is the place where Harold fell," promptly returned "And no wonder; I nearly tumbled there myself." I hasten to apologise to long-suffering and too indulgent readers. My excuse for mentioning it is that precisely the same story is told with Nelson's old flagship as the scene and our most famous admiral as the hero in the case.</p>
        <p>Not a Sweeney Todd. " A man can't talk back to 'nis dentist and doesn't dare to his barber.". So I further read in last Saturday's paper. It brought to my mind the case of a " tonsorial artist," who, during Moody and Sankey's evangelistic tour, " g'ot religion," and, with the con- vert's proverbial excess of zeal, set to work to impress hi s customers. A stranger seated himself in the operating chair,, and the tact- less barber, having lathered ,him, said in solemn tones as he prepared to use his razor, "My friend, are you prepared to die?" He was himself taken aback when the affrighted visitor sputtered through the soap, " Oh, Lor', you're never going to kill me, are you?"</p>
      </div>
