      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 23, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>features as carefully as we can examine those of Rameses, the Pharaoh of the Oppression. The story of Joseph is the most complete and romantic in all the Old Testament, and if the young people of to-day were asked whom they would like most of all to see of the heroes of the past there is little doubt that they would desire to see the face of the man who was great enough to forgive his brothers, and great enough to climb to a seat at Pharaoh's right hand. More valuable to us will be the records and marks which will accompany the , bodies of Jacob and Joseph. The Egyptian ' usage always carefully marked the rank of , the individual, and we shall at last have definite evidence as to the position Joseph held in Egypt. The lonely tomb of Rachel will be ap- proached with no small curiosity. For generations it has been guarded against the tread of the foot of man. For generations before that it was an object of veneration to the Jews. When the people were led to cap- tivity tradition had it that a low wailing was heard from the tomb—" the voice of Rachel weeping for her children and refusing to be comforted." It is hardly likely that we shall see the features of the woman for whom Jacob laboured fourteen years, but it will be deeply interesting to discover in what man- ner Jacob laid her to rest.</p>
        <p>of their deep sense of the eminent services rendered by him to the cause of education in the borough and of the great pleasure it has always been to all those who have worked with him during the many years he has been associated with the committee."</p>
        <p>The following letter from Father Wilson, addressed to Councillor 11. Roberts, the chair- man of the Education Committee, was read : "As I am leaving Warrington I send in to you, not without sincere regret, the resignation of my membership Of the Education Committee. I have had the honour of being a member of the committee since its inception, and the personal kindness and courtesy I have always experienced from yourself and from previous chairmen, and from all other ~,members will abide with me always as a very pleasing remembrance. As far as my information goes few committees have succeeded in administering the Act of 1902 with so little friction and in working so liar- moniously with the Tanagers of the unprovided schools as the Warrington committee has done. The broadminded manner in Which it has always dealt with the schools I was appointed specially, 1 suppose, to represent, has been a matter of great satisfaction to the Catholic body in the town as well as to myself. I earnestly hope the same good relations and cordial co-operation in the supremely important work of education will always continue. I desire also to express my appreciation of ,the courtesy and willing help I have unfailingly experienced from the Director and his staff.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>WHEN THE TURK IS DISMISSED.</head>
        <p>WHEN THE TURK IS DISMISSED.</p>
        <p>Some Of The Things That Await The Explorer in</p>
        <p>Palestine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>TOMBS THAT HAVE NEVER BEEN OPENED.</head>
        <p>TOMBS THAT HAVE NEVER BEEN OPENED.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR</p>
        <p>• Under the strain of the anxieties of the war, few of -us,heive: had any 'thought to give to its possible results in Palestine. For eight centuries the Turk&amp; have held the old home of the Hebrew people, and have kept a most jealous eye and hand upon its relics, but it may be taken for granted that at the close of the war it will pass to the care of a Christian Power.</p>
        <p>The prospect is interesting from every point of view, for there is no strip of land of equal size in the world which has witnessed so much that has mattered to mankind, and whose future, in consequence, holds and deserves more of human interest.</p>
        <p>Thus far the war • has gone cruelly with Palestine. Her young men have been drafted into the Turkish Army. The burden of taxa- tion, always heavy, has become intolerable. The poverty of the people, always dire, has been felt as in no recent time. Added to this has been the cessation of the tourist traffic on which Palestine depends for ready money and for the sale of her merchandise. The immedi- ate future is dark indeed for the down-trodden dwellers in "the Land." Of the further future few of us will care to speak with sureness, but it is quite certain that a new era is dawning. That which the Crusaders failed to do will be done as a result of the present war. The Holy Places will be wrenched from the Turk, and will pass under Christian domination. The moment is a con- venient one to recall the past.</p>
        <p>In the year -1076 the 'Seljouk Turks took poisession of Jerusalem. At first they had little idea that they would be able to hold it, and they determined to make bay while .the sun shone. Their oppression of the native population yielded them a considerable but insufficient revenue; and for the rest, they made exactions on Christian pilgrims who came from Europe to pray at the Holy Sepul- chre, to kneel in the cave at. Bethlehem, and to bathe their heads in the sacred waters of the River Jordan.</p>
        <p>Such pilgrimages had been in vogue from a very early day. St. Helena, the mother of Constantine, visited the Holy Land early in the fourth century. She it wa.s who built the Church of the Nativity in Bethlehem; she it was who identified what she believed to be, the veritable tomb of Christ and the place of His crucifixion, now crowned by the Church of the Holy Sepulchre. To visit these sacred scenes, to tread in the veritable footprints of the Saviour of men, was regarded as a most praiseworthy and desirable act, and the monarchs of the Roman Empire, as well as the heads of the Church, encouraged the pil- grimages. About the year 1064, during the reign of Constantine X., a caravan of 7,000 pilgrims passed through Constantinople and Asia Minor, and when in the neighbourhood of Jerusalem were attacked by the Bedouins. They lost 3,000 of their number, and were unable to visit either the Jordan or the Dead Sea. When a dozen years later the Seljouks came into actual possession of the Holy City itself, a cry of horror rose throughout Chris- tendom. Peter the Hermit went from coun- try to country preaching that God willed the deliverance of the Holy Land from the cruel and licentious Turks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>CRUSADES.</head>
        <p>THE CRUSADES.</p>
        <p>There was little need to exaggerate the conditions which pilgrims encountered on their way to -Jerusalem. The Turks insulted the poor and plundered the rich' they made merchandise of the most sacred shrines on earth; they desecrated the altars of God and trod under foot the relics of the saints. It was not difficult to rouse Europe to what it believed was righteous zeal for the deliver- ance of the Holy Land from the infidels. The first crusade began in 1096, and moved on with tremendous zeal. In June, 1099, -the Christian warriors came in sight of tho walls of the Holy City, and in about a month cap- tured it and took possessioti in a sack which spared neither ago nor sex. For a little tinv; Jerusalem was in the hands of the Christians. Thus far the story is stirring, but it has a pathetic sequel. The armies were dissolute, and the conquerors displayed every possible kind of incompetence and unworthiness. The first crusade . was followed by another, and this by many others. The high impulse under which they had begun sank lower, and was dragged through much of mire. The cross, which the Crusaders believed to be the verit- able one on which their Lord had been cruci- fied, was carried into battle. Under the brow of the Horns of Hattin, the supposed scene of the Sermon on the Mount, this sacred relic was lost and never recovered. Like. the Ark of God, which the two sons of Eli took into battle and lost to the Philistines, the Crusa- ders, wearing the sign of the cross, lost to their enemies the cross itself.</p>
        <p>It is now nearly 840 years since the Turks captured the Holy City. The Crusades have continued to thrill the Christian imagination with pride and to humiliate it with shame. The Holy City is still in the hands of the Turks. A guard of Turkish infantry, armed with guns, encamps in the front corridor of the Church of the Holy Sepulchre. Viiitors have often bribed a soldier to push a way for them through the crowd. It has been the same at the Church of the Nativity. Its door is built low, and one must stoop to enter, in order that it may never be accessible to the invasion of cavalry. But a squad of infantry camps in the door, and Christian pil- grims have walked by the guns of Moslem soldiers keeping guard in the most sacred shrine of Christendom.</p>
        <p>Happily, there are not now the exactions and abuses of early centuries, for the modern Turk has had nfore than one good reason for treating pilgrims with an approach to decency.</p>
        <p>Archreologists look forward to the close of the war with unconcealed hope and expecta- tion. With the Holy Land under the con- trol of a Christian Power, there will open a new and very interesting chapter in the his- tory of explorations. The prospect is fascinating. Palestine con- tains the Cave of Machpelah, where Abraham, Sarah, Isaac, Rebecca, Jacob, and Leah were laid to rest; it has also the tombs of Rachel, Joseph, David and Absalom. But these places have been holy ground to the Moham- medan as much as to the Christian•, and every</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.-</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.-</p>
        <p>continued.</p>
        <p>harvest festival brought to my mind how the Rev. Mark Guy Pearse, the eminent Wesleyan minister, was wont on similar occasions to take with him into the pulpit. an ear of wheat and discourse thereupon in his refreshingly original and altogether delightful.way. He it was, as many readers will remember, who, along with the late Rev. Hugh Price Hughes, started the now famous West End (London) Mission. Although, temperamentally and in many respects, the two preachers were wide as the poles asunder, neither xfas willing to enter upon that enterprise without the other, and for years they co-operated with extra- ordinary success. There are many Cornish preachers in the Methodist churches, and I have known some half-dozen, .but Mr. Pearse was quite inimitable as a teller of Cornish stories,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>MOUN FIELD.</head>
        <p>MOUN FIELD.</p>
        <p>effort made towards their exploration has been vigorously resisted. Christian feet have never (save in the case of Edward VII. when he was Prince of Wales) pressed the sacred ground of the Cave of Machpelah, and only rarely have Christian pilgrims stood on David's threshing floor, over which the Temple of Solomon was built. Not even, the heir to the Throne of England was permitted to enter the tomb of Rachel, for the reason that no man had ever been allowed to ap- proach her coffin. Under Christian rule it is quite certain that these ancient resting-places will be reverently opened, and with the new knowledge that will be acquired there will come a widespread re- vival of interest in some of the great names of the Bible.</p>
        <p>Let us briefly mention some of the results that may flow from new conditions in Pales- tine.</p>
        <p>It is well known that the Mosque of Omar at Jerusalem covers the threshing floor of David. The piece of elevated rock chosen for the threshing and winnowing of wheat may still be seen under the dome of the mosque. Here was built the stately temple of Solomon, long since broken by the hands of destroyers. In the eyes of the Mohammedan this spot is only a little less sacred than the Black Stone of Mecca, and no explorer has yet been able to search for whatever may still be there of the walls and foundations of the temple of Solomon.</p>
        <p>Destruction foil so suddenly upon the temples of Solomon and Herod that it is more than likely that many interesting things were buried in the ruins. What if the archives of Solomon should be uncovered and ancient writings once more should meet the light? The speculation is full of interest. It has long been the opinion of scholars that the earlier writings of the Bible were originally written in cuneiform characters and upon tablets. If inscribed in the Bahylonisb fashion upon clay or stone they would be written in separate and disconnected episodes. These would be copied in connected form upon papyrus or parchment in later years, but nothing is more certain than that the original records would be carefully preserved. Ex- perience has shown that -clay tablets, if they rest in a dry place, will last for thousands of years. It is, therefore, not impossible that we may yet discover some of the original writings of the Bible. And there is no likelier place to look for them than in the labyrinths beneath Solomon's Temple. THE CAVE OF MACHPELAH.</p>
        <p>The tombs of the Patriarchs offer another field for speculation. The ancient Hebrews were most careful as to the burial of their dead. One of the first acts of Abraham after he settled in the Land of Promise was the purchase of the Cave of Machpelah as a place of burial. Here, according to Genesis xix., 31, "They buried Abraham and Sarah his wife; there they buried Isaac and Rebecca his wife." And Jacob, when he died in Egypt, asked his 'sons to lay him there by the side of Leah his wife. Rachel, the mother of Joseph and Benjamin, had died while travel- ling and had been buried- in a separate tomb near Bethlehem. When Joseph died he asked his brothers to swear that whenever they de- parted from Egypt for Palestine they would take his body with them.</p>
        <p>As the Hebrews are not likely to have prac- tised elaborate mummifying we cannot be sure that we shall ever look upon the faces of Abralam and Isaac. It is quite probable, however, that we may see the features of Jacob, for he died in Egypt, and would be embalmed in the careful Egyptian fashion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>JOSEPH'S MUMMY.</head>
        <p>JOSEPH'S MUMMY.</p>
        <p>Most interesting to look forward to will be the uncovering of the face of Joseph. He would be embalmed in the most finished man- ner, as became his rank, and we may reason- ably hope that we shall be able to study his</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>UNEXPLORED JERUSALEM.</head>
        <p>UNEXPLORED JERUSALEM.</p>
        <p>In the valley of Jehoshaphat is the line of tombs called the Tombs of the Kings, where, according to tradition, Absalom was buried, and at whose memorial the Jews flung stones to show their abhorrence of the misguided son who filled his honoured father with anguish. These sepulchres are undisturbed, and await the hand of the arclueologist. When the explorers have a free hand in Jerusalem they• will hardly know where to begin. Probably next after the site of Solo- mon's Temple they will uncover the reputed tomb of David and ascertain whether his body is really there. According to Kings ix. 10, " David slept with his fathers, and was buried in the City of David." There is no reason to doubt the tradition which locates his tomb in the tower called after him, but we shall never know until some digging is done there and the stone coffin, probably with an identifying inscription, is brought to light. If his body has been preserved by embalming, we shall know the features of the sweet singer of Israel, and most beloved of all her leaders. Some fascinating documents have been found in Eastern tombs. Who shall say what these undisturbed graves will yield to us when we have done with our Armageddon, and ,:an follow the peaceful arts once more?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>RETREAT. August 23.</head>
        <p>THE RETREAT. August 23.</p>
        <p>Loud did the trumpets sound on English shores, Our heroes answered the call— Fathers and sons, with stout hearts, brave and true, There for their country all.</p>
        <p>Swiftly they marched away in fine array; Courage and pluck were splendid; ,To glorious Mons on that fair summer's day, No duty they suspended.</p>
        <p>Fighting and bleeding on that battlefield On August twenty-third, They shed their blood for country and for home. And honour they deserve.</p>
        <p>Bravely they fought until forced to retire By overwhelming number. Ah, who retired? Our losses we don't say— Our Army rent asunder.</p>
        <p>They started out on their successful march, The German hordes pursuing, But not a single man surrender would, Glory or death his doing.</p>
        <p>At last there came a wonderful relief— The Frenchmen did unite With our beleaguered gallant little group, Fighting with all its might.</p>
        <p>And both advanced again with courage high, Their German foe to meet, And forced them backward to the flowing Ainse: In hastening retreat.</p>
        <p>To the young men of England unenlisted, Let this a message be "Your valuable services are wanted For Country, King; for Law and Liberty."</p>
        <p>Written by J. Ridgway, Ist Canadian Division, 8.E.F., France.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>"COURTEOUS AND WISE IN HIS JUDGMENT."</head>
        <p>"COURTEOUS AND WISE IN HIS JUDGMENT."</p>
        <p>In proposing the adoption of the minutes of the School Management Sub-committee. The Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson said that so far as that sub-committee was concerned he would like to take that opportunity of express- ing their very deep sense of the loss they had sustained through the departure of Father Wilson from the town. Father Wilson had, throughout his long connection with the com- mittee, ever been courteous, and wise in his judgment and, moreover, he had supported every measure that was for the educational good of the town. Ile (the Canon) was sure he was only expressing the feelings of every member of the committee when he said that they sin- cerely hoped that free from his more onerous position as Rector of St. Mary's-T-n position which' he had held so long and worthily— Father Wilson might, in his new and com- paratively quieter and more retired position, enjoy the remainder of his life with some measure of peaceful leisure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>PUT EDUCATION FIRST.</head>
        <p>PUT EDUCATION FIRST.</p>
        <p>Upson also paid a tribute to the good work Father Wilson had done for the schools of the town. The Father's interest in educa- tional work was very much wider than the interests merely of the group of schools he more particularly represented. First and foremost, his interest was in education as,a thing in itself. (Hear, hear.) Father Wilson was a member of the School Management Sub-committee, and he (the speaker) thought that that body could take to 'themselves the flattering unction that they put educational interests first and all other interests second. Father Wilson did that pre- eminently—education was his first concern—and the work of the sub-committee, as also of the larger committee, would suffer by his with- drawal. Every one present, the speaker was sure, felt personal regret that Father Wilson had been compelled to leave the sphere in which, in his quiet, determined manner, he had done so much, showing deep devotion to the cause of education and doing everything he possibly could in every way to help on the great work which he and they • all had at heart. (Hear, Lear.) .</p>
        <p>"NEVER TIRED OF DOING HIS DUTY." Mr. J. Plinston, speaking as one who for twelve years had been associated with Father Wilson in the educational work of the town, said that the Father gave him his kindly aid and assistance when he (the speaker) became a member of the School Attendance Sub-com- mittee, and helped him to an even greater extent when he was appointed chairman of that body. Father Wilson was a perfect gentleman and one who never tired of doing his duty. Moreover, lie was one who was perfectly cleati- handed with all he attempted, and by his re- moval the Education Committee had lost a very valuable member. (Hear, hear.) The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) said that when he heard that Father Wilson was leaving the town he took it upon himself to write to the reverend gentleman expressing not only the sense of loss which that committee would feel, but also expressing the sense of dis- tinct loss which would be felt by the town generally, and hoping that his career in his new sphere would be as successful as it had been in Warrington. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>GOOD WORK FOR THE TOWN AS A WHOLE.</head>
        <p>GOOD WORK FOR THE TOWN AS A WHOLE.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett said he would like it to be understood that whilst that evening they were more particularly concerned with the good work the Father had done as a member of the Educa- tion Committee, they were by no means blind to the good work he had done for Warrington as whole. When the Father told him that he was going it gave him quite a shock, and lie felt a sense almost as of a personal separation. They had learned to lave Father Wilson and his figure had become so well known to them all that they could hardly think of Warringtou. without him. Father Wilson was always glad to co-operate in any local public function, and in that respect he showed a broad catholicity, in the true sense of the term, which every citizen appreciated. The loss to the Catholic Church must be very great indeed. He (the Alderman) had thought that some more tangible mark of appreciation might have been offered the Father after his long years of service to the town, than. just an offi- cial record. Surely something more than a formal vote of thanks was due to the man who had spent twenty years or so in the town, and had dime so much for it, and had worked so 'hard for every good cause associated with the place. The Vice-Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) said he could corroborate all the good things that had been said about Father Wilson, speaking from his personal knowledge and experience of the gentleman. The town was the poorer for his departure; its public life had suffered • a great loss, for the Rev. Father was one who, in the best and highest sense of the words, was a Christian gentleman. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>CHAIRMAN'S PRIVILEGE AND PLEASURE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman expressed his entire accord With the words of eulogy uttered concerning the services of Father Wilson, adding that he had had the pleasure of knowing all the Catholic clergymen in Warrington for more than forty years, and had, in consequence, learned to respect them and to admire them greatly, not only for their devotiou to their owu special</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>FR. WILSON'S DEPARTURE.</head>
        <p>FR. WILSON'S DEPARTURE.</p>
        <p>Education Committee Record Their " Unfeigned Regret."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>MEMBERS' EULOGIES.</head>
        <p>MEMBERS' EULOGIES.</p>
        <p>At their monthly meeting on Monday evening, the Warrington Education Committee received. with regret the resignation from membership of the Rev. Father Neville Wilson, the rector of St. Mary's, Buttermarket-street. As we announced a few weeks ago, the rev, gentleman has been appointed to another church at Brown- edge, near • Preston, and is accordingly severing a connection with the town of War- rington which has lasted since 1896; the year in which the Father came to St. Mary's. Not only has Father Wilson accomplished- -great and good work for the church of his adoption, but he has also played PO small part in the public life of the town, having been a member of the present governing body of the schools since its inception; whilst at one time he was actively identified with the administration of the Poor Law as a member of the Board of Guardians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>FATHER WILSON ON TACT AND HIGHMINDEDNESS.</head>
        <p>FATHER WILSON ON TACT AND HIGHMINDEDNESS.</p>
        <p>The printed minutes for Monday's meeting of the Education Committee announced the fact that the Father had written resigning his position on the several sub-committees of which he was a member.</p>
        <p>"My association," he wrote, to Canon Stevenson, chairman of the School. Management Committee, "with your committee, to my sincere regret, comes to an end. It has been my privilege to be a member of this com- mittee from its beginning. Its work seems to me to be unsurpassed in importance by that of any other. Its special scope is such as to bring it into touch with elements of such an intimate and personal character as often to be outside the reach of strict law and regulation. It has been my extreme good fortune always to find kindly consideration and generous assistance accorded to the management and teachers of the schools I have represented. That there has been, so far as I can recall, no instance of friction or difficulty between your committee and these schools is a fact most gratifying to me and eloquent of the tact and high-minded- ness of the committee. For these happy results and for unfailing personal kindness, at all times from yourself and the other .members, I tender my very sincere thanks. " Earnestly trusting that the harmonious working between the committee and the management of our schools will always continue. I am, yours very sincerely (Signed) NEVILLE WILSON."</p>
        <p>The committee resolved "That there also be included a record of the committee's unfeigned regret at the loss consequent upon the Rev. Neville Wilson's departure from the town and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>IN TILE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN TILE COURTS.</p>
        <p>BIT 'THE POLICEMAN.</p>
        <p>Woman Al-ho Had Been "A Perfect Nuisance All Week."</p>
        <p>That she was drunk and *disorderly, and that she assaulted P.C. Cameron whilst in the execution of his duty, was the dual charge pr - ferred at Monday's Police Court against Catherine Swift, a married woman, of Cloth Hall-yard.—She admitted both offences. The evidence of the officer was to the effect that when he was called to the woman on Saturday he found her behaving in a very dis- orderly manner—fighting and using very in- decent language. He took her into custody and, when in Bank-street, she became very rough and threw herself on the ground and kicked, and bit witness twice on the arm, making two nasty wounds. With the assistance of P.C. Hewitt witness succeeded in handcuffing the woman and bringing her to the Police Station. She had been a perfect nuisance all the week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>Said She Was Excited.</head>
        <p>Said She Was Excited.</p>
        <p>Prisoner told the Court that she was excited; she had been assaulted by a woman, who had left a mark on her arm.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: She could vot bite the woman who had been troubling heii, so she bit the officer. (Laughter.) The Chief added that there were previous convictions. A fine of 19s. 6d. was imposed, the alternative being 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>GRAVE CHARGE.</head>
        <p>A GRAVE CHARGE.</p>
        <p>Young Man Remanded For Unlawfully Wounding Woman. A charge of unlawful wounding was, at A Monday's Police Court, preferred against a diminutive young man, named Patrick Murray, who was described as labourer, and whose address was given as 55, Oliver-street: The Chief Constable asked for a remand for seven days in order that it might be seen how . the prosecutrix—a woman named Harriet Senior, of 3, Six-houses--progressed. The man was charged with unlawfully wounding Senior by kicking her. It appeared that at about 4.30 on Sunday afternoon the prisoner went to the house where Senior lived, with her blind father. Because Senior rejected a certain proposal which the man made, he savagely assaulted her. He pulled her about by the hair of the head and kicked her in the face and otherwise maltreated • her.</p>
        <p>Shouts and Screams.</p>
        <p>She shouted and screamed, but as he had locked the door she could not get out. Eventu- ally the prisoner left the house, and some young men then went in, and, noticing the woman's condition, took her to the Infirmary. She suffered a good deal from loss of blood, and the doctor said that, had the wound in her face been half an inch nearer the eye, she would have lost the sight of it. There could be no doubt but that the assault was a most brutal one.</p>
        <p>" I Did Do</p>
        <p>Inspector Foster deposed that at 6.15 on Sun- day evening he was at the Police Station when the woman Senior came in and complained of having been savagely assaulted by the prisoner. Witness went to the mans address and found him there, took him in custody and brought him to the Police Station. In reply to the charge. he said, "It is no use telling a lie about it! I did do it!"</p>
        <p>Asked whether he had anything to say why he should not be remanded the prisoner said, "I would sooner be tried now !"</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: That is impossible; indeed, I have very grave doubts as to whether he can be dealt with at this Court at all, unless we considerably reduce the charge. We must see how the woman gdes on before we think of that, and that is why I ask for a remand.</p>
        <p>Prisoner was remanded in custody for a week.</p>
        <p>NEGLECTED WORK.</p>
        <p>War-Worker Twice Stayed In Public- House And Drank.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's Borough Police Court there were on the Bench Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), 4nd Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes and T. v-Locket.' • ' - " """ "</p>
        <p>From Herbert Warburton, of 36, Pierpoint- street, a local firm claimed £5 damages for neg- lect of work. • Defendant admitted the offence.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. S. Steel (instructed by Messrs. Henry Greenall and Co.) supported the claim. On Monday, the 11th, at six o'clock, the man did not put in an appearance; and the foreman went to his house, but he was not at home. Coming back to the works the foreman called to look for him at a public house and found him there. With great 'difficulty he persuaded the man to come to the works. Defendant was under the influence of drink, and after working on trial</p>
