      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>FREE TO ALL SUFFERERS</head>
        <p>FREE TO ALL SUFFERERS</p>
        <p>If you feel*OUT of SORTS' 'RUN DOWN' 'GOT the SLUES' SUFFER from KIDNEY, BLADDER. NERVOUS DISEASES. CHRONIC WEAKNESS,ULCSRS,SKIN ERUPTIONS,FILES, write for FREE CLOTH BOUND MEDICAL 1350 E ON these diseases and WONDERFUL CURES effected by THE NEW FRENCH REMEDY. Nell '44 14,3 THERAPION anddecidefor yourself if it is the remedy for YOUR OWN ailment. No 'follow up' cit. oulars. Send stamp address envelope to DR. LECLERC EIED.Co.HAvERsrocic RD,HAMPhTEApLoppyi.,:"Z:i9 THERAPION either No. Price 2/9 LEAD INGC VIEWSTS.</p>
        <p>work for a short time fell, and was unable to steady himself sufficiently to carry out his duty. At 7.20 he left work. The foreman being un- able to fill his position—as the work was difficult</p>
        <p>to perform—the mill was stopped, with the result that eighteen men lost wages totalling £8 4s. Bd., and that to the company there was serious loss of war service, valued at £3B 9s. 2d. One's Absence Affects Many.</p>
        <p>The men worked in squads, and if one was away all the lot were‘ thrown out of work. Owing to a large number of en!istments the firm had no reserve men to draw -upon, and the fore- men were always anxious that the men should turn up to their work. Not content with what had happened on the Monday, defendant, on the following day was again away, and was found by a police constable drinking in a public house. His conduct had not been satisfactory for some considerable time. The firm's one desire was that the men should attend to their work regularly; they did not vrant to prosecute them. The man earned gooe money-12s. 6d. a shift.</p>
        <p>Defendant promised that in future he would attend to his work and keep to it. He was thereupon ordered to pay the £5 damages—at the rate, he agreed, of 20s. a month. The alternative, it was stated, would be 28 days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>EXPENSIVE DRUNKENNESS.</head>
        <p>EXPENSIVE DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>Stiff War-Time Penalties For Old Topers.</p>
        <p>The borough magistrates have time after time declared themselves as determined to do all in their power to stamp out drunkenness, par- ticularly at the present juncture, when, more than ever, it is a sorry offence against society. At the Police Court on Monday the usual crop of " drunks " made more or less apologetic appearances and the penalties' meted out were, in most instances, stiffer than those that would have generally obtained not so very long ago. For instance, one delinquent was fined 255., with the option of 21 days' imprisonment, whilst another hapless individual, who, it is true, was making his fourth appearance inside seven months, was offered the opportunity of paying a fine of 335. 9d. or going to gaol for 28 days' with hard labour.</p>
        <p>The magistrates on the Bench were the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) in the chair, Aldermen Smethurst, Bolton and James Evans, and Messrs. W. Furness, S. Wright and T. Locker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>AN OBSTINATE DRY% ER.</head>
        <p>AN OBSTINATE DRY% ER.</p>
        <p>Made To Pay For Obstructing</p>
        <p>Tramcar.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Thursday 1 morning, before Mr. A. J. Jolley (in the chair), Dr. Naden and Mr. Wm. Roberts, William Warren, of 301, Wigan-road, Leigh, was sum- moned for impeding the passage of a tramcar in Knutsford-road, an offence to which he pleaded not guilty. Inspector Forster deposed that on Saturday. the 2nd inst., he boarded the 3.10 p.m. tramcar from Rylands-street to Latchford. As the car approached St. Mary-street he l?.eard the driver sound his gong pretty frequently and, looking ahead, saw a pair-horse waggonette, laden with people and driven by the defendant, proceeding along the tram track. Despite the fact that he must have heard the bell, the defendant refused to pull his vehicle out of the way and impeded the passage of the car for quite half a mile. There was nothing to prevent him drawing to the side and allowing the ear to pass, and when he heard the gong s..undxl he merely looked round in a sneering way. John Clarke, of 420, Knutsford-road, the iriver of the tram, gave corroborative evidence, said that the passengers in the waggonette told the defendant that there was a tramcar trying to pass, but all he did was to turn round J yid laugh.</p>
        <p>Defendant, who said he got off the rou.i soon as he could, was fined 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>STREET GAMESTERS.</head>
        <p>STREET GAMESTERS.</p>
        <p>Penalties For Coin Spinners And Card</p>
        <p>Players.</p>
        <p>"This Sunday aite-rrioo—n--gambling is becom- ing far too frequent," remarked the Chief Con- stable at Thursday's Borough Police Court, apropos of a case in which four men appeared for gaming with coins in the vicinity of Kimber- ley-street. He added that he understood that the men were employed on Government work during the week, and consequently would be able to pay substantial fines.</p>
        <p>The men were each ordered to pay an inclusive penalty of 10s.</p>
        <p>Four youths succeeded the four men in the defendants' box and pleaded guilty to playing "banker," also on a Sunday afternoon, and in the same locality as the grown-up quartet. Two of them were fined ss. and two 10s. the Chief Constable remarking that it was no doubt a case of the younger defendants being taught by the older ones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>SLAPPED SOLDIER'S CHILD.</head>
        <p>SLAPPED SOLDIER'S CHILD.</p>
        <p>\ inwick Woman Bound Over For</p>
        <p>Assault.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, before Messrs. H. ROberts and Wm. Fairelough,</p>
        <p>Alice iSeverel, of 17, Hollins-lane, Winwick, was summoned for, on October 7th, assaulting Vera Nichol, the eleven-year-old daughter of Mrs. Annie Nichol, the wife of a soldier who was formerly an attendant at the Winwick Asylum. The mother stated that her daughter came running towards her as she was standing at the back door, when defendant caught hold of the child and slapped her on the ears about five times.</p>
        <p>The girl admitted in evidence that her brothPr had been throwing a stone at defendant's daughter. Defendant said she chastised the girl and thought she was justified in doing so because of her cheek and impudence. Defendant was bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>TWELVE YEARS APART.</head>
        <p>TWELVE YEARS APART.</p>
        <p>husband Summoned For Failing To</p>
        <p>Keep Wife. Before Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst and Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes and W. T. Williams, at Wednesday's Borough Police Court, James Riches (55), an Army pensioner, residing at 58, Upper Hough- ton-road, Southport, was summoned for neglect- ing to maintain his wife, whereby she had be- come chargeable to the Warrington Union. Mr. Shearer, relieving officer, said that the defendant deserted his wife many years ago, and she was now in,the Workhouse.</p>
        <p>Defendant said that it was nearly twelve years since he had lived with his wife. . He left her because she turned his home into a dog's home. 6 He denied that he deserted his wife; it was she who went away from him. The case was adjourned for a month to enable complainant to take proceedings for obtaining a separation order.</p>
        <p>SOLDIER'S WIFE'S HONOUR.</p>
        <p>Allegations That Led To A Summons.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, yesterday, before Mr. S. Wright (in the chair), Aldermen Pierpoint and Smethurst, and Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W". Furness and T. Locker. Mrs. Eliza Hill, of 4, Gilbert-street, was summoned by her daughter-in-law,. Mrs. Edith May Hill, of 15, Dial-street, for assault.—Mr. T. S. , Steel , a ppeayed ._f or the complainant. .Defendant pleaded guilty "to -"striking in self-defence." _ _ _ _ _ - _</p>
        <p>Outlining the facts, Mr. Steel said that the honour of a soldier's wife was concerned. The complainant's husband (the son of the defend- ant) was at present serving with the Army Seryice Corps in France. It appeared that the defendant had been making serious allega- tions against the complainant, and the latter visited her with a view to putting a stop to such allegations being made. In her defence the defendant maintained that she was struck first. Defendant was bound over for six months and ordemi to pay the costs-ss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>VOLUNTEERS ON THE RANGE.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Shots Return Some Good Cards.</p>
        <p>A series of competitions for the members of the Stockton Heath detachment of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment who have taken their class firing test was held on Saturday afternoon on the Appleton Rifle Club's range, about 50 mem- bers taking part. The competitions lasted some three hours, and at the close the 'prizes were presented by Mrs. Warburton, the wife of the" Platoon-Com- mander, Mr. F. Warburton. On the motion of Mr. J. H. Fletcher a 'vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs. Warburton.</p>
        <p>Platoon-Commander F. Warburton replied on behalf of his wife, and said how pleased she was to co-operate with him in the work. -</p>
        <p>THE PRINCIPAL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The conditions in the principal competition consisted of 10 shots at a decimal 50 yards target at 25 yards range, with open sights, and no slings; five sighting shots being allowed on. a separate target. Handicap points were added to the score. The commanding officer's prize was won by Volunteer J. Swinton, with a score of 93, and a handicap of 15—total 108. The ether prize-winners in the order of merit were : Score. H'cap. Total. ' J. Smith A. Hulme T. Fletcher J. H. Fletcher T. Clarke H. Young ....... 94 6 100 Two prizes were given for the best results in each seetion:—</p>
        <p>Section 1. Score. H'cap. Total. J. Smith T. Fletcher ......... 95 6 101 Section 2. J. Swinton J. H. Fletcher Section 3. T. Clarke G. H. Eccles "87 12 as Section 4. A. Hulme A. Shepherd</p>
        <p>A special prize was given by Volunteer W. Russell for the best grouped target. This was won by Volunteer L. R. Rowson with a group of under one inch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>SECTIONAL TEAM RIVALRY.</head>
        <p>SECTIONAL TEAM RIVALRY.</p>
        <p>A match took place between five men from each section for a challenge trophy presented by the Society of Miniature Rifle Clubs. No. 1 Section proved the winners by 4 points, the detailed scores being:—</p>
        <p>Section 1.</p>
        <p>R. M. Swinton T. I. Fletcher . H. Young L. Fairclough E. Bradshaw</p>
        <p>Scction 4.</p>
        <p>A. Shepherd ... A. Hulme J. McDermid J. F. Fergusson M. A. Piercey</p>
        <p>L. R. Rowson J. H. Fletcher B. W. Clough 3. Kelly - E. G. White .</p>
        <p>SEction 2.</p>
        <p>Section 3.</p>
        <p>98</p>
        <p>97 91 90</p>
        <p>A. Worrall . W. Howard A. S.. Wright C. J. Smith T. Cox</p>
        <p>..... 73</p>
        <p>, 373 SKIRMISHING TEAM SHOOT.</p>
        <p>Teams of three men were selected from sec- tions for a skirmishing team competition. The conditions were unlimited firing for one minute at a "rapid 50 yards green target" at 25 yards range, the team registering the most value in hits to be the winners. A team from No: 4 Section, composed of Musketry-Instructor A. Shepherd ..(captain), Volunteer W. C. Williams and Volunteer T. Dakin, registered a total of 150 points, and were declared the winners.; The Organisirig_ and Handicap Committee who had charge of the competitions were: Sec- tion Commander F. Fergusson, Musketry In- structor A. Shepherd, and Volunteer R. M. Swinton.</p>
        <p>Eighty per cent. of the members of the detach- ment have passed the class firing test, and 28 per cent. of them have qualified as marksmen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>AVARRINGTON WORKING MEN'S</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>N.A. Centre.</p>
        <p>There was not a specimen recorded last week- end, the first week this has happened for many months. The Gowy, Shropshire Union Canal, Norton, Chester (Dee and canal) were all visited by members, and those who caught fish were very fortunate. Those who visit the Gowy are wishing daily for a fresh to shift the loose duckweed and to give a little colour to the stream. Members who have fished the stream week after -week ,iiave. watched it become more sluggish and finer every time they have. paid. it a visit. One member reported that in one favourite haunt he-could see his bait on the bottom, and the water here is seven feet deep.</p>
        <p>Our delegate who attended the Executive Council meeting-held at the Fleece Hotel, .St. Helens, on October 9th,. reports that a better watch is to be kept on the Shropshire Union Canal in future. Certain anglers have been re- ported fishing this water without holding cards, but the offictals have now taken the matter up, and some remedy will be found to stop these poachers. A deputation was formed to approach the management of the Lancaster Canal. A reporf was brought back that was very satisfactory to the meeting. The •elO that tke company spends yearly in stocking the canal with perch is to be spent this year in roach, if' a suita,ble market can be found. Bank-rangers have been in- structed to, sell no day tickets until after close season, as soon as the seaSon'closes.</p>
        <p>oirell, near Wigan. has been accepted in the N.A.A. The club will form a new centre, and will take in clubs from Billinge and Skelmers- dale, and increase the N.A.A. membership greatly. e The .Earl of Sefton's steward has been. ap- proached, and more privileges have been. ob- tained on the Leeds and Liverpool Canal. On the off-side. Messrs. A. and Ti. Howard and two other gentlemen are to stock the Leeds and Liverpool Canal to the amount of .40. The annual general meeting is to be held at Crewe on November 27th.</p>
        <p>Nothing Like Keeping At It. A , 'gentlemanly looking. pedlar entered a business man's office and coughed slightly to attract attention. The occupant of the office kept at his work -until he reached a convenient stopping place and then turned abruptly to his caller. "Well," he asked, "what can I do for you?" "I am introducing," the pedlar began, "a patent electric hairbrush—" "What do I want with a hairbrush?" growled the business man. " Can't you see I'm bald?" "Your lady, perhaps—" "Bald, too, except when she's dressed up." "Yes, sir: But you may have at -home a little child—' "We have. It's one month old and quite bald." "Of course, at that age," said the pedlar. "But," he persisted, "maybe you keep a dog?" "We do," said the business Man. "A hair- less Chinese +dog." The pedlar dived into another packet. "Allow me," he said, "to show you .the latest thing in fly paper."</p>
        <p>Some Siwprising Results In Saturday's Games.</p>
        <p>There were some big surprises in the Northern Union games played last Saturday, especially so when one learns of such clubs as Wigan, Oldham, Halifax and Broughton Rangers retiring at the close of hostilities without even registering a single point against their respective opponents.</p>
        <p>The biggest of these surprises was the trouncing of Wigan by that junior and enthu- siastic organisation St. Helens Recs., who defeated the Central Park team by no less than 18 points to nil. It is true that Wigan had not a "great" side out, but to be defeated by so huge a margin is indeed a "hitter pill" for the Wiganers to swallow!</p>
        <p>Oldham were defeated on their own ground by the other St. Helens thirteen by 13 points to nil; and of the other games in the Lanca- shire Section Leigh were successful over the reorganised Broughton Rangers' ,side by 10 points to nil; and Rochdale Hqrnets had the misfortune to go under to Dewsbury (who, by the way, were greatly strengthened ,)33- the Wakefield men) by the odd point in eleven. Young again turned out at half-back with the Hornets, but he was early in the wars, and must have received a severe gruelling owing to the weakening of the Rochdale side through accidents. In fact, so hard were he and Tay- lor (the other half-back) worked, that the latter had to pay the penalty at the close of hostil- ities—a penalty which might have had a tragic sequel. He collapsed in the dressing-room and fainted in the bath, and was under water when his unconscious condition was noticed, and it was some time before the attendants brought him round again.</p>
        <p>The Leeds team are now the only side who have not tasted. defeat, Hull going down to the Headingley side by 8 points to 3.</p>
        <p>The results of Saturday's matches were as follows :</p>
        <p>Swinton, 4 points; *Salford, 3. *St. Helens Recs., IS; Wigan, nil. St. Helens, 13; *Oldham, nil. *Leigh, 10; Broughton Rangers, nil. Dewsbury, 6; *Rochdale Hornets, 5. *Leeds, 8; Hull, 3.. *Huddersfield, 5; Hull Kingston Rovers, 5. *Featherstone, 19; Hunslet, 13. Bradford Northern, 23; *Halifax, 0. Bromley, 16; *Brighouse, '2. *Denotes home club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The Intermediate League results for Satur- day, October .16th, were as follows:— Sacred Heart, 15 points; Whitecross Ropery, 3. Heathside, 8; St. Janes' 0. ' Bolton, 0; Parochial, 0. Rylands's Rec., 5; Beamont, 2.</p>
        <p>Silver-street, 14; St. Mary's, 0. The match, St. Thomas' v. Wycliffe was not played owing to the inability of the latter to :aise a team.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>BEAMONT v. RYLANDS'S RECS.</head>
        <p>BEAMONT v. RYLANDS'S RECS.</p>
        <p>This match took place on No. 2 ground in Victoria Park. Rylands's•won‘the toss and they decided to play. down-hill. From the kick-off the ball was taken into the Beamont half, when the defending side were penalised. From the free-kick •Dumbell, the Recs. captain, landed a goal." After this reverse a lot of give and take play took place and ended in Hill, of Rylands's Recs., registering a good try. \ The goal-kick failed. Half-time: Rylands's Recs., 1 goal, 1 try, 5 points; Beamont. nil. The second half started in rare style, both teams putting "in' a great lot of footwork. Rylands's were penalised and the Beamont team landed a goal. Result: Rylands's Recs., 1 goal. 1 try, 5 points; Beamont, 1 goal, 2 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>BOLTON v. PAROCHIAL.</head>
        <p>BOLTON v. PAROCHIAL.</p>
        <p>- - This match was played at the Park. The start was delayed, three of the Bolton players failing to turn up to time. Both started at full strength, but one of Parochial's had to stand down owing to injuries the week before. It was soon seen the game was going to be keen, Parochial using their weight well in the pack. They got possession often, but failed to make headway through the sound defence of Bolton. In the loose the Bolton forwards were far nippier, and fairly hampered the Parochial backs. Many good movements on both sides were spoiled at times through the ground being a shade slippy. The interval came with no score.</p>
        <p>The second half was pretty nearly like the first till close on time, when the referee had to caution the Parochial boys for rough play. They were trying very hard to score, and came very near to doing it with a splendid drop at goal. The ball just missed by inches. They got the ball many a time, and seemed certain to score when three to one, but at times they failed miserably, and at other times the Bolton backs were splendid in saving their line, both catch- ing and kicking well. Towards the finish the Bolton lads were beginning to look tired, but managed to keep the Parochial from scoring. Every praise is due to the Bolton team, as this is only the second point they have lost for two seasons, and the first time they, have failed to score. It was Bolton who took the point fr.,in them last season. It was more like a cup-tie game. The game finished with no sr:r.re.</p>
        <p>SACRED HEART v. WHITECROSS ROPERY. At Wilderspool. Whitecross won the toss. and Sankey, for the Hearts, set the ball rolling. After one or two scrimmages near the White- cross line, Mercer dashed over for the Hearts. Following this, Sankey gathered near the White- cross- -25'"- line,, and, with several Whitecross players at his feet, managed, to get the Hearts' second try. No goal resulted from either try. Half-time arrived with the Hearts leading by two tries to nil.</p>
        <p>After the interval the Hearts' forwards began to push the Whitecross pack all over the field. From a scrimmage near the line, Farringtan gave to Leah, who went over, for the Hearts' third try, no goal resulting. ',Millington then scored for Whitecross, running three-parts the length of the field— The Hearts got two tries in quick succession, the referee awarding a try for an obstruction on Buckley, when going well for the line. Leah got his second after a round of passing. The whistle blew with the score: Sacred Heart, 5 tries (15 points); Whitecross Ropery. 1 try (3 points). For Whitecross, the full-back was the out- standing figure. For the Hearts, Clarke, the full-back, and Farrington were the pick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>JUNIORS.</head>
        <p>THE JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>Heath Side, 8 points; St. James', 2.</p>
        <p>MATCHES' FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>On Warrington Ground.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe v. Heathside. Kick-off 3 p.m. Referee,. Mr. Bibby. Bolton v. Silver-strect. Kick-off 4 p.m. Referee, Mr. Bibby.</p>
        <p>On Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>Kick-off 3.30 p.m. Ground I.—St. James' v. St. Thomas'. Referee, Mr. R. Williams. -</p>
        <p>Ground 2.—Beamont v. St. Mary's. Mr. G. Crozier. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Ground 3.—Parochial v. Sacred Heart. Mr. J. Beardsell. . Ground 4.—Rylands's Rec. v. Whitecross. Mr. D. Aikenhead.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>BEAMONT V. ST. MARY'S.</p>
        <p>On Vittoria Park. No 2 ground. Beamont.—A. V. Lawrence; W. Wych, A . Makin, John Banner and R. Whitfield; W.</p>
        <p>Wilton and H. Melbourne; T. Percival, H. Pemberton, F. Jones, J. Higham, T. Littlewood and A. Guest.</p>
        <p>Reserves.—T. Archer, C. Owen, Winstanley and J. Nolan.</p>
        <p>SACRED HEART v. PAROCHIAL.</p>
        <p>No. 3 Field, Victoria Park</p>
        <p>Sacred Heart.—J. Clarke; H. Baker, F. Leah, A. Plant, A. Buckley; P. Farrington, P. Hill; J. Dorrian, J. Sankey, A. Titchard, A. Mercer, S. Lunt, S. Harrison. Reserves: Reay, McFarlane, Appleton, G. P. Crannigan. . •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>RYLANDS'S RECS. v. WHITECROSS.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS'S RECS. v. WHITECROSS.</p>
        <p>At Gorsey-lane. Kick-off 3.30. Rylands.—T. Archer; Wilkinson, Swanton, Littlewood and Hill; Robinson and Sloane; Dumbell, Latimer, Lee, Sutton, Savoury and Mulvery. • Reserves.—Maskell, Miller and Butler.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>MR. MEE "JOINS UP."</head>
        <p>MR. MEE "JOINS UP."</p>
        <p>'Owing to the Secretary, Mr. E. J. Mee, having joined the Army, all communications respecting the League should be addressed till the next monthly meeting on November 4th, to Mr. A. Malcolm, 113, Wilderspool-road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Notes On League And Friendly</p>
        <p>Matches.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District League only one game was played last week, and this resulted in an easy victory for the Hollins Green team over the Central team by four clear goals. This was a heavy defeat for the Central. They opened very strongly, but their forwards were disap- pointing in front of goal, and in the second half an injury to Glover caused a re-arrangement in their defence. This, to some extent, was accountable for their downfall.</p>
        <p>A friendly game was fixed up between the Fairfield United and the Aluminium Co.'s F.C., and was played on the Orford-road ground. Both these clubs have sent in their applications-to the Warrington League to be admitted as mem- bers, which will in all probability be sanctioned by the League Committee. The United secre- tary was able to get a fair team together, whilst the visitors, for their opening game, are to be complimented upon the game they played against their more experienced opponents. They were successful in opening the score, and before the close of play, when it appeared probable they were going to suffer, defeat, they made strenuous efforts, and succeeded in equalising the score. J. Banner, who last season played for the Great Sankey Club, is captain, and by his dash and daring raids he gives plenty of en- couragemoit to his colleagues. Davis and Walker were also very prominent in the half- back line, and Llewellyn did well at centre-for- ward. Woodhouse and Redford made a good wing for the United. Gregory played a hard game at centre-hAlf, and .Spencer and Monks de- fended well.</p>
        <p>In the " friendly " between the Elmwood Con- gregationalists and the Emmanuel Institute there was much keen rivalry and too muck feel- ing shown between. some of the players: The referee found it necessary to award three penalty kicks in the first half and one in the second, and there were plenty of other instances when a free kick ought to have been awarded to one side or the other, which were allowed to pass. At the interval the. Congregationalists were in arrears, but in the second half they gave one of their best displays and gained the lead, Knight, in the visitors' goal, not having any chance with the shots that beat him. The 'Stute had a good chance of equalising a few minutes before the close of. play, Crozier making a poor attempt to score from a penalty kick.</p>
        <p>In the Junior League some keen and interest- ing games , were played last week, the most interest centring on the Stockton Heath Scouts and the Fairfield Juniors match. It was un- fortunate for the visitors to lose the services of their goalkeeper, and much credit is due to them for the game they played, as for three-quarters of the game they had only ten players. After being two goals in arrears they succeeded in equalising, and it was only in the last minute that the Scouts gained the winning point.</p>
        <p>Orford and Mersek Juniors was well contested throughout. the Orford lads having to play a hard game to secure the lead:</p>
        <p>Padgate C.L.B. and St. Elphin's proved a very one-sided game in favour of the Saints.</p>
        <p>Latchford Cadets strived bard against the lads from Bewsey, but failed to penetrate their defence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>HOLLINS GREEN V. WARRINGTON CENTRAL.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the ground of the former club. The home captain won the choice of ends, and set the visitors to defend the bottom goal, From the kick-off the visitors played strongly, and during the first twenty minutes had the best of the exchanges, but the home defence would not yield their fort to the attack. Gradually the home forwards began to have more of the play, and transferred the hostilities to their opponents' end, their inside right regis- tering the first goal. Even play followed until near the interval, when the home team, en- couraged by their supporters, attacked the visit- ors' goal very strongly. and gained, their second point. Half-time: Hollins Green,. 2 goals; Cen- tral, nil. In the second half the visitors had the slope in their favour, and were expected to improve their position, but unfortunately for them Glover, owing to injuries, had to change posi- ons, and this weakened their defence. The home team increased their lead. the inside left scoring the point. From now till the close of play the home team had the best of the game, the visit- ors' defence failing to hold them, and another goal was added to their score. Final: Hollins , Green, 4 goals; Warrington Central, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>ELM WOOD CONGREGATIONALISTS v. EMMANUEL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>The above clubs were engaged in a friendly on Saturday on the ground of the former, the following players turning out Elmwood Congregationalists—Roberta; nix and and Oxley; Clayton, Ashton and Hazeldine; W. Phenix, Molyneux,, Kendrick, Wells and Littlewood. _ _ _</p>
        <p>I nstitute.—Knight ; Crozier and Hughes; Ayres, Hardy and Parr; Eaton, Booth, I'. Hughes, Oakes and W. Eaton. Referee, 11. Berry.</p>
        <p>The. Institute won the toss and played up the hill. The game opened very evenly, and keen rivalry was soon evident in some of the exchanges. The visitors drew first bloo.l. Booth receiving a centre from Eaton, placed the ball between the uprights after fifteen minutes' play. The Congregationalists then pressed hard and were awarded a penalty kick. Ken- drick failed to score, and soon after the referee awarded the Institute a penalty kick, Crozier shooting hard, Roberts touched the ball but failed to divert its course. Shortly before the interval Wells scored from a penalty kick for the Congregationalists. Half-time: Institute, 2 goals; Congregationalists, 1 goal. The second half commenced in favour of the Institute, the home defeneers clearing with difficulty. Midfield play followed until Ken- ' drick broke away, but his final effort was high over the bar. The Congregationalists now improved greatly and gave one of their best displays, showing keenness and dash in their attacks, and Ashton equalised very smartly.</p>
        <p>They were very much encouraged by this suc- cess, and were now having the best of the game. The visitors' forwards, whenever they appeared dangerems, were smartly driven back by Phoenix and Oxley. Knight was called upon to clear twice in quick succession. Kendrick made a smart attempt with an overhead kick, Knight saving well. The visitors made several danger- ous rushes into the home territory, and Moly- neux secured from a long kick and scored th( Congregationalists' third •goal with a capita shot. Near the finish the Institute had anothe] penalty kick, Crozier missing a fine chance o. equalising. Final score: Elmwood Congregr tionalists, 3 goals; Emmanuel Institute, 2 goal</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD UNITED v. BRITISH ALUMINIUM F.C.</p>
        <p>The above clubs played their opening game 0, the season with a friendly engagement on th ground of the former at Orford-road. •The fo7 lowing players turned out : United: Norke; MOnks, Spencer; Pritchard Gregory, Cole; Boscow, Jones, Stubbs, Wood. house and Redford. Aluminium: Bond; Northall, Heeley; Chil. ton, Davis, Walker; Kennedy, Wells, Llewellyn Titchard and Banner.</p>
        <p>Referle, 3. Short. The United won the toss and set the visitors to defend the railway goal. The opening stages were evenly contested, the ball being quickly transferred from end to end. Spencer, of the United, kicked strongly, and the visitors, by strong play on the left, forced Monks to concede a corner kick, which was well placed. Gregory clearing nicely. After midfield play Llewellyn, the visitors' centre, placed the ball nicely for Banner to rush past the backs and beat Norke with a low drive and score the first goal for the visitors. Encouraged by this success, the visit- ors played keener, and Llewellyn hit the cross- bar. From the rebound the home team tratt:- ferred to the opposite end, and after sevez al shots had been repulsed, Stubbs equalised with a shot well out 'of the reach of Bond. A few minutes later the same player caused Bond to make a splendid save at the foot of the inst. Half-time arrived with the scores one goal fach. The second portion was more keenly contested, the visitors having more confidence than in the first half. The defenders on both sides were fre- quently called upon and keen tackling was seen in midfield. Stubbs gained the lead for the United, and then the visitors renewed their efforts and made some strong attacks on the home goal, and succeeded in gaining the equalis- ing point. Titchard doing the trick. Even play followed till the call of time. Result: Fairfield United, 2 goals; British Aluminium, 2 goals. ST. JOHN'S CHURCH v. PADGATE UNITED. The above teams met on Saturday in Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>The players of St. John's were: R. Shaw; It. Stark and W. Stringer; Carruthers, R. Brown and R Stringer (captain); A. Nurse, C. Lloyd, J. Bell, R. Wright and J. Brown.</p>
        <p>St. John's won the toss and played with the wind. Both sides opened well. When half-time came St. John's had scored two and Padgate nil. A. Nurse and R. Wright were the scorers. In the second half both teams played well for the first ten minutes, then Padgate scored. St. John's backs were playing a safe game, keeping the ball well down the field, but Padgate got possession and rushed up the field and scored a second goal. A few minutes from the close the inside-right scored a third. Result: Padgate, 3 goals; St. John's, 2 goals. The referee was Mr. Wrench, of St. John's.</p>
        <p>F- le 11 if s. ll- le</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>11 XRRINGTON &amp; DISTRICT ASSOCIATION JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>11 XRRINGTON &amp; DISTRICT ASSO- CIATION JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Results to October 16th.</p>
        <p>Goals. P. W. L. D. F. A. P. St. Elphin's Stockton Heath Scouts 3 3 0 0 7 3 6</p>
        <p>Orford Juniors _7 2 4</p>
        <p>Howley Juniors Bewsey Albion Fairfield Juniors iMersey Juniors "Bewsey .2 4 0 , Latchford Cadets Padgate C.L.B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>ST. ELPH I NI'S v. PABGATE C.L.B.</head>
        <p>ST. ELPH I NI'S v. PABGATE C.L.B.</p>
        <p>The above teams met at Padgate and the lads kicked off with the wind in their favour. They held the advantage for the first few minutes and the " Saints" gained relief trom a free kick. Boyle opened the account for the "Saints,' and Hough added the second goal with a splendid shot, and Boyle, by an individual effort, added the third. Half-time: St. Elphin's, 3 goals; Padgate C.L.8., nil. In the second half the " Saints " did very much as they liked, Main scoring twice and Hough four tithes. The " Lads " scored just ' before the call of time. Result: St. Elphin's, 9 goals; Padgate C.L.8., 1 goal.</p>
        <p>STUCKTON HEATH SCOUTS v. FAIRFIELD</p>
        <p>JUNIOR S.</p>
        <p>On the Walton Club ground, before a good number of spectators. The visitors were the first to become dangerous, mezr final efforts being wide of the goal. From the goal kick the Scouts, by good combination, transferred play to their opponents' end, when a long shot by Farnell hit the upright and glanced through the goal, Lockhart being -unable to save. After this reverse the visitors played more keenly and Foster and Cooper for their respective sides defended very well. Another strong attack by the Scouts led by Williams, who centred well followed, and Parnell, heading the ball into goal, collided -with Lockhart, the visitors' goalkeeper. Unfortunately the latter had to retire from the game owing to injuries. The visitors were now handicapped, but held their own. At half-time the Scouts were leading by 2 goals against none. On resuming the visitors became aggressive, and the home defence was severely tested. The visitors, not to be denied, renewed the attack and a long shot by Kidd was punted out by, Hampson, but George returned the leather and scored the Juniors' first goal, and a few minutes later they were awarded- a penalty. kick, from which they equalised the scores. Both teams now tried hard for the lead, and as' the close of play was drawing. near the Scouts made a final bid. Their .fol;wards rushed down in a body and Parnell scored his third goal in the match. Directly after the final whistle blew. the result being: Stockton Heath Scouts, 3 . goals; Fairfield Juniors, 2 goals. LATCHFORD CADETS v. BEWSEY ALBION. On the Arpley ground. The visitors soon asserted themselves, and after ten minutes' play Miller scored with a long shot for the Albion. The Cadets made an effort to equalise, but were repulsed. Directly after they secured a corner kick but failed to improve on it. From the goal kick the Albion forwards showed good judgment in their passing, and when getting dangerous were smartly checked by Ashton. They quickly returned to the attack and gained their second goal. The Cadets could not get out of their own half at this period, the visitors playing a strong game. The visitors did net relax their 'efforts until they had scored ther third goal. Even play followed. Half-time: Albion, 3 goals; Cadets. nil. After changing ends the Cadets played a rery much improved game,. and each defence was keenly tested, but proved too good for the attack. No further goals were scored, the final result being: Bewsey Albion, 3 goals; Latchford Cadets, nil.</p>
        <p>MERSEY JUNIORS v. ORFORD JUN!ORS. The above teams met on the Arpley ground The Mersey captain Won the tcss an.l defenled the top goat.' Straight from the lick-off the visitors made tracks for the home goal, and it was only by steady play of the backs that pre- vented a store. Then followed a spell of mid-. field , play, all the half-backs tackling keenly. Some good combination play between Walters and Willoughby resulted in the visitors' goal being captured, Willoughby doing the needful. The visitors replied with a strong attack, but G. Walters in goal proved safe. The visitors returned to the attack, and a splendid in- dividual effort by their inside-right secured the equalising goal. Half-time One goateach.</p>
