      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <p>...</p>
        <p>illibit,</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 23, 1915. DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>Shops, Businesses, &amp;e.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>Continued from Page 3.</head>
        <p>Continued from Page 3.</p>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Devotional Meeting.—On Tuesday evening the Wesley Guild held their opening devotional meeting in the Schoolroom. The speaker was the Rev. H. T. Dixon, who discoursed earnestly on the subject of prayer. Mr. S. Plumpton was the soloist, and there was a large •attendance. Wesley Guild Social—On Saturday the Pen- keth Wesley Guilds held their opening social in the Schoolroom. A well-arranged programme included vocal solos by Miss Kershaw and Mr. Plumpton, violin solos by Mr. B. Smith, and a humorous recitation by the Rev. H. T. Dixon. After the music refreshments were served, and the rest of the evening was given over to games and various amusing competitions, the prize- winners in the latter being: Mrs. IT. Davies and Mr. Clatworthy, and Mrs. M. Ray and Mr. W. Foden. There was a fairly large gath- ering and a very pleasant evening Was spent by all. More For The Front—The exodus of recruits last week has had its moral effect, for a further hatch of young men have offered their services to King and country mid have beer accepted. There are also signs of more to follow. The names of those who, so far, have shown that they do not intend to wait until they are conscripted are: George L. Parsons, Albert J. Richardson, Walter J. Foden. Alec Plumpton (Royal Army Medical Corps); Leonard Smith (Cyclists 1 Sec- tion, Royal Sussex Regiment), and Edward J. Mee (Aimay Ordnance Corps).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>AVIIAVS IN A NAME?</head>
        <p>AVIIAVS IN A NAME?</p>
        <p>Parish Council Thinks There May Be Something.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Parish Council was held in the Council School on Monday evening, when the following members were present :—Dr. Murray (chairman), Messrs. J. G. Withinshaw (vice-chairman), W. Davies, J. Woodward, and Miss Tinsley; with _Mr. E. Foster (clerk).</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the London and North-Western Railway Company acknowledg- ing the receipt of the Council's letter of the 19th inst., asking that the name of Fiddler's Ferry and Penketh Station should be altered to Penketh and Fiddler's Ferry. The matter, the letter said, had been carefully considered by the Company, who regretted that they did, not .see their way at present to make any alteration.</p>
        <p>The Chairman asked if it was thought neces- sary to press the matter further. Mr. Withinshaw suggested that the Clerk write again to the Company, saying that the Council were very dissatisfied with their answer and that. for the convenience of the district the name should be altered. Mr. W. Davies seconded and it was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>Christ Church Harvest Festival.—The harvest thanksgiving services $n conneetion with the Parish Church were held,on Sunday, the morn- ing preacher being the Rev.- A. Baines, vicar of St. Helens, and the Rev. J. R. Jones, vicar of Great Sankey, preaching in the evening. In the afternoon a special children's service was held, presided over by the Rev. J. Hirst. A very interesting address was, given to the scholars by Mr. R. Dickinson. The flowers, fruit, cigarettes, &amp;c., which were brought by the children, were sent to the wounded soldiers in the local military hospitals. During the day special music was rendered by the choir, the anthems "I will feed My flock" and "He watereth the hill" being very well. rendered. The soloists were Mi. S. Oddy and Mr. W. Fryer.</p>
        <p>Presentation To Church Choristers.—At the close of the harvest festival services at Christ Church on Sunday, very interesting presenta- tions were made by Mr. Percy Royston to two members of the choir—Mr. S. Oddy, who i- leaving the neighbourhood, and Corporal Fred Young, who is on active service. Mr. F. Blackwell, the choirmaster, said that Mr. Oddy had been a splis.-did supporter f the choir for about nine years, and the wardens and his fellow members of the choir wished to present him with a memento of his work amongst them at Padgate. Speaking at Col- poral Young, who is on active service, he said they wished to mark his departure *by a small gift in the shape of a beautifully bound copy of the Cathedral Psalter and "Hymns Ancient and Modern," and a similar gift was made to Mr. Oddy. Owing to Corporal Young being unable to be present, the gift was accepted on his behalf by Mrs. Young.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>"POETRY OF PATRIOTISM."</head>
        <p>"POETRY OF PATRIOTISM."</p>
        <p>Local Wesleyans Listen To Delightful</p>
        <p>Lecture.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Geo. A. Jack-son, of Didsbury College, paid a visit to the Padgate Wegleyan Church •a. Th .r.:day, and ander he auspices :it the Wesleyan Guild, gave an address in :the evening on "The Poetry of Patriotism?' ;He also `conducted a service in the afternoon.. Mr. J. Bailey, J.P., presided over the lecture—Which was very well attended—and also on the plat- form were the Revs. A. J. Morton and W. T. Derry (superintendent minister of the circuit), and Alderman Bennett, of Warrington. In his opening remarks the Chairman recalled the fact that it was nearly 32 years ago since he first visited Padgate. Patriotic• Spirit Of Old Testament.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Geo. Jackson said that the first book the lover of literature would turn to for an illustration' of the poetry of patriotism was *the Old '1 estalstent. 9.)me who had read the BI ,k all their lives did not realise what a variety of literature was to be found in that book. In it were to be found law, history, prophecy, drama and fiction, and there was also to be found "poetry of patriotism." The greateSt of all contributions to that poetry was known as the "Song of Deborah," which is 'contained :in 'the fifth chapter of the Book of Judges. Proceed- ing, the lecturer said that there were two points in common between the Scdt:and the Jew. The first was a love of travel and the second was a clinging to the homeland and thoughts of homeland. To support this he read a poem on Edinburgh, written by the late Robert Louis Stevenson when resident on a Pacific Island, and compared it with the psalm "By the waters of Babylon we sat down and wept When we remembered thee, 0 Zion." The poetry of patriotism had a magnificent place 'in English literature. Early English literature had a tinge of the sea in it, but Chiefly consisted of ballads of war. The lecturer's first -quotation from English poetry of patriotism was the familiar couplet by Shakespeare : " Naught shall make us rue If England to herself do rest btit 'true:"</p>
        <p>Talking of the 17th century, Mr. Jackson said, that in spite of the stirring events of that period, very little poetry was yielded that came under the category with which he was dedling that night, but there were a few patriotic poems, and as samples the lecturer read .a couplet by Lovelace and extracts from Milton's 'address to Cromwell, and his" Epitaph to a Brave Man." ' Wordsworth's Place In Poetry.</p>
        <p>In the 18th century came Cowper, Burns and Wordsworth, and, in his opinion, Wordsworth was the :greatest Of the three, and he advised the members of his audience to read " The Happy Warrior" as soon as they could. Refer- ring to the well-known "Burial of Sir John Moore," the lecturer said that this was first Printed in an Irish newspaper, and that "its authorship had been claimed by liar after liar?' Mr. Jackson also read extracts from two poems by Browning, namely,'" Home Thoughts From Sea" and "The Patriot." 4The latter, he said, was .one of the most pathetic. things he knew of from Browning's pen. He also quoted a few lines from Tennyson's " Ode to the Death of the Duke of Wellington?' and from Francis Hast- ing DoYle's "A Private of the Buffs." As a very fitting conclusion to a most enjoyable lec- ture Mr. Jackson recited Kipling's "Reces- sional."</p>
        <p>ILLNESS-OF DR. W. G. GRACE. Dr. W. G. Grace is suffering from •slight cerebral hemorrhage, affecting the speech centre' only. His condition* is stated to be satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>Wedding Present.—Mr. and Mrs. A. Mayer were the recipients, on Sunday afternoon, of a plant pot pedestal, subscribed for by the Sun- day school teachers and scholars as a wedding present. The Vicar made the presentation. Mr. R.. Brandreth said a few words wishing the couple much happiness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>"OUR DAY."</head>
        <p>"OUR DAY."</p>
        <p>Warrington Raises Funds For</p>
        <p>Red Cross Work.</p>
        <p>In common with scores of other towns in England, Warrington observed Thursday as "Our Day "—the day which marked the first anniversary of the amalgamation of the British Red Cross Society and the St. John Ambu- lance Brigade, and the day on which special efforts were put forth throughout the length and breadth of the country to swell the funds of those.two splendid organisations, who be- tween them have done so much to alleviate the sufferings of the men broken in the war. Warrington's special effort—like the special efforts of its near neighbours, Stockton Heath and Lyinm—took the form of a splendid even- ing concert, which was held in the Parr Hall and was exceedingly well attended, there being but very few vacant seats even in the highest- priced part of the building.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>AN IRRESISTIBLE PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>AN IRRESISTIBLE PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The splendid cause apart, the programme was of such a degree of attractiveness as was almost bound to ensure success, the list of artistes including the names of those who are best and most fayourably known on the local concert platform. Thus, there was Madame Sara Silcock, whose delightful soprano voice has delighted many thousands of Warrington- ians in the past, and, it is to be hoped, will last to enchant thousands more. Madame was in splendid voice and she made Landon Ron- ald's delicious little chansonette "Down in the Forest" a thing of real Leauty and joy, her treatment of Cowen's trifle "A Birthday." being no less effective, with the result that an encore was inevitable. For this Madame Silcock chose a piece of quite, Another character, sing- ing that ear-haunter " The Rosary," and thereby winning for herself fresh torrents of applause. Madame E. A. Sanders also scored a big hit. Her first song was Forster's " Mifanwy "—a decidedly pretty ballad—and she treated it with such a degree of charm and sweetness that the audience would not let her go until she had given that modern number which is rapidly springing into great popularity, "No Rose in all the World Until You Came." Later on Madames Silcock and Sanders earned more plaudits from those present with the duet "Elfin Call," in which they have been oft-times heard locally, and in which their voi4s blend beautifully. Mr. W. H. Rigby gave an effective rendition of the old Sullivan favourite "The Lost Chord," and had to respond to a voci- ferous recall, contributing a lightsome balladette that had to do with a surreptitious but per- fectly innocent kiss on the stairs. Mr. W. J. Hopkins was the humorist of the evening, ami his droll treatment of Banks' "I Followed Her Here !" proved so to the taste of those present that they demanded and got another merry ditty. The Brooklyn Quartet Party—Messrs T. Crowe, J. Sephton, W. Dutton and W. Hopkins —certainly proved one of the biggest hits of the evening, loud and prolonged applause greeting their every effort. They sang " Jenks's Vege- table Compound," "Come Where My Love Lies Dreaming" and "Little Tommy Went a-fish- ing," and were equally successful in the humor- ous numbers as they were in the charming old sentimental one. For the rest. 'the programme was sustained by the band of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment (P.W.V.) Under the able _</p>
        <p>conductorship -of Mr. R. E. Cahill they gave ex- cellent performances- of some excellent music, whilst they also added to' the lighter side of the programme a laughable mtisical humoreske entitled "I'm Afraid to Come Home in the Dark." Their more serious contributions—each played in a manner betokening painstaking re- hearsal—included the delightful old Savoy opera, "Haddon Hall'; the ever-popular " Zampa " overture; the carillon from Bizet's "Suite, L' Arlessienne," a fantasia on the work of Grieg ; and a selection from Jean Gilbert's "The .Cinema Star "—one of the latest rages The band brought the highly successful enter. tainment to a close with a pot pourri which introduced the National Anthems of the Allies, finishing, of course, with "Rule Britannia " and "God Save the King."</p>
        <p>"TRAFALGAR DAY" AND "OUR DAY."</p>
        <p>During an interval the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) introduced to the audience Miss Sylvia, Broadbent, who is the viCe-presi- dent of the local divisional organisation. of the British Red Cross Society, and who thanked all who had helped to make the concert a success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>AMBULANCE WHIST DRIVE.</head>
        <p>AMBULANCE WHIST DRIVE.</p>
        <p>A whist drive, organised by the Nursing Division of the St. John Ambulance Brigade, was held in the Cairo-street School on Wed- nesday evening, and proved to be a great suc- cess. No fewer than 177 ladies and gentlemen sat down to play, and the very fine prizes on offer were given by the following: Messrs. Miller &amp; Co., Sankey-street; Mr. Eaton, Buttermarket- street; Mrs. Bate, Orford-lane; Mr. Davidson, Bridge-street; Mrs. Blease, Cairo-street; Mr. E. Jones, Butterraarket-street; Mr. T. Wads- worth, Church-place; Mr. Williams, Bridge- street; Messrs. Hill &amp; Co., Buttermarket-street; and the Nursing Sisters. The refreshments</p>
        <p>were given by the following :—Mesdames Willett (Dalton-bank), Blease (Cairo-street), Lilley (Whalley-street), Wright (Fennel-street), Kempster (Birch Tree Farm, Stockton Heath), Roberts (Lovely-lane), Oultram (Kendrick- street) and Mocatta (Hanley-place), and Miss Wardle (Longford-street). Chocolates were also given by Mrs. Wright (Buttermarket-street) Mrs. Turner (Church-street) and Mrs. Rath- bone (Friars' Green), and they were sold by Miss Edna May Baldry, dressed as a nursing sister.</p>
        <p>At the finish of play Mrs. Mocatta, who was accompanied by her husband, Colonel Mocatta, kindly distributed the prizes. Mrs.? Mocatta was presented by Miss Baldry with a bouquet on behalf of the Nursing Division. Superintendent Baldry officiated as M.C. The proceeds were in aid of the "Our Day" effort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>BUFFALOISM.</head>
        <p>BUFFALOISM.</p>
        <p>Unique Ceremony At Earlestown. R.A.0.8., G.L.E.</p>
        <p>A Buffalo ceremony, the first one of its kind in Earlestown, took place on Wednesday evening at the Railway Hotel, Earlestown, when Primos Siddeley and Swift were elevated to the rank of Knighthood. The ceremony, which was under the auspices of the Lancashire, Cheshire and Isle ,of Man Province, was performed in a most impressive manner by Sir John Holden, K.0.M.. K.G.H., P.P.G.C. (of Warrington), assisted by the Rt. Hon. Sir Wilfred ;3. Rawson (South- port) R.0.H., K. 0.5., P.P.G.P., P.G.Sec., P.G.C. , Eng., Sir Thos. Richards, K.0.M., P.G.P. (Liverpool), Sir Walter Sutton, K.0.M., K.G.H., P.G.C. (Warrington), and others. •</p>
        <p>His Worship the Mayor of Warrington (Bro. Peter Peacock), the guest of the evening, in pre- senting the K.O.M. apron to Sir Tom Snideley, TCO.M.; said that he (Bro. Peacock) was pleased to have an opportunity of showing his apprecia- tion of one who had done so much fcr the Oi der. The Rt. Hon. Sir Wilfred Rawson, in present- ing the Collarette of Knighthood to Sir Tam Siddeley, complimented him upon being the recipient of an honour, which had been as well conferred as it had been honourably won. The toast of "The Visitors, including Our Honoured Guest, His Worship the Mayor," was very .ably proposed by the Founder of the Lodge, Sir Walter Sutton. The Rt.- Hon. Sir Wilfred B. Rawson, in pro- posing the health of the Installing Officer, said he had had the pleasure of working with Sir John on a great many occasions in the Provin- cial Grand Lodge, and also in Grand and Minor Camps of the K.G.H., and had often noticed his whole-hearted and zealous devotion to the duties of the important offices assigned him, and he. as Past .Grand Chamberlain of England, had not. the slightest hesitation in saying that the Order would be all the better for a few more Sir John Holdens. They were indeed very proud of 'him.</p>
        <p>The Installing 'Officer thanked the Mayor, not only for his kind expressions and good wishes, but also for honouring the Lodge, the newly- raised Knights and the members by his pre- sence. He also thanked the P.G. Primo of the Lancashire, Cheshire and Isle of Man Province for his kindness in coming among them at great inconvenience to himself, and Sir Wilfred Raw- son for his unvarying kindness. To his mind that densely packed flssemblage of breth- ren was a happy augury of the future welfare, not only of the "Tom Siddeley " Lodge, but of the'Order4o which he was proud to belong.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>FL \ AND FROLIC AT THE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>"'Ave Some Sense," the revue with the curiosity-raising title, is drawing big houses at the Royal Court Theatre this week. And no wonder, for it is a perfect riot of that kind of fun which nowadays is in special favour, whilst the girls who take part in it are pretty, the costumes chic, the comedians genuinely funny, and the music_ tuneful. What more would pin have if you are in search of a two- hour divertisement?</p>
        <p>The principal funny-man is Albert Bruno, and at the laughter-raising business he is a host in himself, possessing a quaint style that is all his own and would extract chortles from ithe most miserable, moping misanthrope that ever refused to smile. It is one of his oft- repeated tags that provides the title for the piece. Albert is loyally supported by the rest of the company; indeed, they make the show go with just that degree of " swing " which is necessary to the salvation of a heterogeneous hotch-potch of the kind. . " Lancashire And Proud Of It."</p>
        <p>Next week Royal Court patrons are to have the pleasure of witnessing an all-Lancashire revue with the above title. Judging from the fact that Harry Tate, Joe Gorman and Wal Pink are associated in putting the merry trifle "on the road," it should be "a perfect scream" and well worth seeing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>REVUE WITH A PLOT AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>REVUE WITH A PLOT AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The revue entitled 'The Red Heads,' which is occupying the boards at the popular Macnaghten house during the present week is one of the best and prettiest shows we have seen for some considerable time, for, unlike most revues, it has a plot. "The Red heads" is presented by Sydney Blow and Will Collins, proprietors of several revues now touring, and is one of their most successful. It is enacted in the Kaufski showrooms, where a brilliant array of costly and artistic gowns, creations of the latest fashion are displayed on many chic and winsome models. The company in general is a good one, and is assured of a long tour. Several capital turns are given by the Brothers Webb, entertainers; Vera Caine, described as the British athletic girl; and Tew and Tea, who create abundant merriment with their parodies on nursery rhymes. The " Gaumont Graphic" completes a full and in- teresting night's amusement. Next Week's Fare.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Lasses will next week top a bill of special excellence at this popular place of amusement, and other attractions will in- clude: Wilf. Burnand, in character studies; Levand, in mysteries and illusions; Cissie Lupino, a charming and clever dancer; Phillip Williams, in humorous and topical sketches at the piano; Albert Voyce, the new monologue comedian; Williams and Warden, Irish come- dians; and the Wonderful Poppeseus, on the horizontal bars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>FINE WEEK-END FILM AT STAR</head>
        <p>FINE WEEK-END FILM AT STAR</p>
        <p>KINEMA.</p>
        <p>The big film at the Star Kinema during the forepart of the week was "The Heart of a Child " which told a deeply human story and was most popular with the largo auaiences who assembled to witness it. "Never Despair !" was the title of another excellent drama- subject, to which a moral was effectively attached. "Pitching a Tale" proved to be a first-rate comedy, and the "interest" film showed some beautiful and fascinating views of the Moorish city of Tunis, and its environs. The war pictures were particularly good, and one of them depicted the Czar in the field, another King Albert, a third President Poin- care, and a fourth General Joffrei the President and the General being happily filmed whilst rewarding some of the gallants among the French troops. This week-end's show fully maintains the usual high standard which patrons have learned to expect from this popular picture. house, the programme being chock-full of interest and amusement. The big feature is an animated photographic adaptation of "The Shulanrifre," the novel with which those two popular writers, Alice and Claude Askew, scored. such a signal success. Next Week's "Top-Liners,"</p>
        <p>The "star" films at the Star Kinema next week will be: "Beulah "—founded on the famous old novel of that name—and "Her Triumphs," the former showing on the first three nights and the latter on the last three. Both films are spoken of as "tip-toppets."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>" CA BIRIA."</head>
        <p>" CA BIRIA."</p>
        <p>This wonderful produCtion by the Itala Film Co., from the Scenano. by Gabrielle D. Annun- zio, the world-famed Italian patriot and War Secretary, is announced to visit Warrington at an early date. This film marks the highest point yet attained in kinematography. Words cannot adequately describe the wonderful scenes, portraying the eruption of Mont Etna, the earthquake, the destruction of the city of Catania. and the gigantic spectacular effect obtained at the ceremony in the Temple of Moloeh (God of Love). Attention must also be drawn to what hitherto has never been obtained in any other film, viz., stereoscopic effect. In " Cabiria " patrons are introduced to a number of such scenes that will call forth well merited appreciation from any audience. All picture lovers in the district will be well repaid by a visit to• this wonderful picture. " Cabiria," when it comes to Warrington. Further particulars will be announced next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>For Convalescent " Tommies."</head>
        <p>For Convalescent " Tommies."</p>
        <p>For the benefit of the patients in the Thelwall Heys Military Convalescent Hospital, another Pound Day is to be held on Thursday next, the special requirements being stationery, tobacco, cigarettes and potatoes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>"The Pudding Lady's" Visit.</head>
        <p>"The Pudding Lady's" Visit.</p>
        <p>Miss Florence Petty. (" The Pudding Lady ") will give Jectures at the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street, on Tuesday next, at 4.30 and 7.30 p.m. The hall will be open half an hour before the lectures in order that the food samples and economical dishes may be inspected. Miss Petty will give lecture-demonstrations in war-time housekeeping. In the afternoon she will speak of her experiences as a social worker. Admission lcill be fr. .</p>
        <p>B.R.C.S.</p>
        <p>, Under the auspices of the Special Service Section of the local Voluntary Aid Detachment of the British Red Cross Society, a public meet- ing is to be held in the Cairo-street Schools on Wednesday evening next for the purpose of dis- cussing the work now open to women in hos- pitals, in addition to nursing. Miss Sylvia Broadbent is to preside and Miss Knowles will be the speaker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>Agricultural Jumble Sale.</head>
        <p>Agricultural Jumble Sale.</p>
        <p>In the Brewery Field, Wilderspool, on Thurs- day, November, 18th, a -jumble sale of stock, produce; agricultural implements, &amp;c., will be held in aid of the Agricultural Relief of Allies Fund and the British Farmers' Red 'Cross Fund. Mr. Harry Gandy, Bridge Foot, is act- ing as •secretary for the sale, and he will be pleased to receive gifts of suitable articles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>Limiting Coal Merchants' Profits?</head>
        <p>Limiting Coal Merchants' Profits?</p>
        <p>A report of the General Purposes Committee presented to the Town Council at their monthly meeting on Tuesday evening, set forth that a letter was read to them from the Board of Trade suggesting an arrangement with the local coal merchants for a limitation of the profits to be made by them on the sale of house- hold coal.. It was resolved that the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock), Deputy Mayor (Dr. Joseph), Aldermen John Evans and Tina= and Councillor Pemberton be appointed a sub- committee to confer with the coal merchants on the suggestion. Subsequently the sub-committee met and tha following prices were 'agreed upon, with repre- sentatives of the leading local firms :—Best house coal, in bags. 275. 6d. per ton, or is. 41d. per cwt. (loose, 265. Bd. per ton); second quality house coal, in bags, 255. 10d. per ton, or is. nd. per cwt.. (loose, 255. per ton). Fuller details will be found in our advertisement columns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>ST. LUKE'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. LUKE'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services in connectian,, with St. Luke's Church were continued Sunday, when there were good congregatloo throughout the day. The preacher in the mall; ing was.the,Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth, Vie of St. liernabas'. In the afternoon Mr. Livo:6' Police Court Missioner, gave an address to to° Sunday school children, and at the evening scr- vice the preacher was the Rev. T. T. Lee-Joie!, vicar of Daresbury. , The collections were vel/ satisfactory.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY MISSION, BANK QUAY'</p>
        <p>Last Sunday highly successful harvest festivl services were conducted morning, afterno6n an" eVening at the above Mission. The inissn was decorated with fruit and flowers, .whic were subsequently sent to the Haddon Courow and Whitecross Military Hospitals and military wards at the Infirmary. w. Miss Bogstrom was the preaeher, and Mr. 11. Rigby read the lessons at the evening F.el.- d vice. Mr. Malcolm presided at the organ. 00 Miss Elsie Hewitt sang "Come unto Ifinl th</p>
        <p>(Mindelssohn). Miss Bessie Dutton spoke of the good work: which had been continued at the Mission (1°1; ing the past twelve months, and said 11° satisfactory had been the work of Miss B°g.: strom, as secretary, as well as the help gice:c by the people in general at a time when 1111°' of them were hard pressed.</p>
        <p>Printed and published by John Walker (Warrington), Limited, at the Ex olio' General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, VV,B4 rington ; also published by them at Is, EXAMINER Offices, 2, Victoria-road, Widne t; 32, George-street, St. Helens; 3, Market-streer, Earlestown, all in the County of Lencaste_. and at Church-street, Runcorn, in the Coul3lo of Chester.</p>
        <p>--</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-10-23_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>'Or"</head>
        <p>'Or"</p>
        <p>`1"'144.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Is The War A Sign Of The Second Coming? Highly successful harvest thanksgiving set' vices • were held in connection with the Emmanuel Reformed Episcopal Church, Bold - street, on Sunday, there _being • large attend- ances both morning and evening, and also in the afternoon. The church was decorated with seasonable fruits and flowers, which, on Mon- day, were divided among the local militarY hospitals. The t•pecial preacher for the day was the Rev. J. P. Hodgkinson, of Wigan. In the evening the reverend gentleman took as hie text the words from St. John: "Verily, .verily, I say unto you: Except a corn of wheat fal! into the ground and die it abideth alone; mbr if it die, it liringeth forth much fruit." 'lle preacher argued that the corn of wheat referred to by Christ was symbolic of Himself and 1118 Church. Whilst Christ was on earth His Church, e- was but a small one, numerically; but what change there was after the Crucifixion! -.Christ died in the earthly sense, even as the corn of wheat died: but the Divine that was within Him lived and grew and brought forth a rich harvest, even as the germ- that was within als corn of wheat appeared to die only to 117: forth into fresh life and vigour and IntiltlP itself abundantly. A Joy Greater Than Harvest. • Though the heart rightly abounded .with OY for the blessings of the harvest, how nille 13. greater should its joy be when. it thought uP°ll, the spectacle of the Master dying for the sin mankind—paying the penalty that they "nig', enjoy everlasting life—and rising in triurnPu and ascending to the right hand of the Fathel, all for their justification? Perhaps some et them allowed their minds to dwell too much 011 things natural rather than on things eternal: but he (the preacher) believed—and he WaA alone in so believing—that the coming again of the Lord drew nigh. The signs of the times on the Continent of Europe would. seem t° argue the nearness of the Second Coming, and ought they not to consider what had been &amp;nil° and was being done's° that they might be reaaf to meet Him? Special music was rendered at each e,erOee by the choir, the evening anthem, which wa°- specially well rendered, being entitled Eyes of All !" Mr. J. W. Jowett presided 81, the organ.</p>
        <p>Choir's Busy Day!</p>
        <p>The choir sang appropriate -Music at all sel,' vices. In the morning . they gave Maunders anthem "While the earth remaineth," in tll° afternoon the same composer's "0 worship the King," and in the evening Clare's "The eYe4 of all wait on Thee." The solo part in the ever ing anthem was taken by Miss Miller, whii!: at the same service, Miss Wardle gave effective rendering of Dr. Liddle's setting °lt " Abide with me."</p>
        <p>ELMWOOD-AVENUE CONGREGA' , TIONAL CHURCH. Harvest thanksgiving servieeS will be held • the Elmwood-avenue Congregational Chureh to"-'' morrow, when the preacher will be the Rev. efi P. Pitt. in the afternoon the choir of the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church will render the cantata "The Two Harvests," and the Rev. Ogden, of Golborne-street Baptist. Church, give an address. On Monday the custowAr7 harvest social will take place.</p>
