        <p>RUNCORN GUARDIANS. Workhouse Master Joins The Forces. Mr. J. L. Jones presided, on Tuesday, over a Meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians, • which was held at the Dutton Workhouse. L TWO OPERATIONS. 7- , i: The Local Government Board wrote sanction- -1 ing the payment of £26 ss. in respect of two -Operations performed at the Workhouse upon -an inmate. LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD AND YOUNG CHILDREN. A letter was read from the Local Government' :Board agreeing that Article 4 of the Poor Law Institution Order should not apply to the Run- Torn :Union, so far as regarded children between three and five years of age, until September Qitli, 1916. If circumstances made it necessary, 142 e letter added, further application should be 'lade prior to the date named. It should be 4_ll4derstood, however, that this right of applica- , 'ion would not be renewed indefinitely: "NOBLE AND SELF-SACRIFICING." A vote of appreciation of the conduct of Mr. Parsons (the Workhouse Master) who is about • tO join the Army, was passed. It was stated that this was not an ordinary ease of a man giving up a comfortable berth and . joining the Army. Several members spoke of the "noble and ‘ Bell-sacrificing" act of Mr. Parsons. RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL. - Projected Hospital For Con- sumptive Patients. 'A meeting of the Runcorn Rtiral District Council was held on Tuesday, M. Antwis pre- siding. CAREFUL WITH THE GOLD! A circular letter was read from the Treasury on the question of economy in the use of gold coin. Whilst appreciating the conduct of most , employers in this matter, said the letter, greater stringency would be needed. . A resolution was passed giving instructions to the cashiers not to apply for gold at the bank for the purpose of paying wages. TO REPRESENT THE COUNCIL. A letter from the Cheshire Agricultural War Committee notified the formation of that body, at the request of the Board of Agriculture. They wished the Council to nominate one or more representatives to serve on the committee.. Messrs. Woodward and Linaker were appointed. SCARLET FEVER SETS IN. The Medical Officer of Health reported eleven , cases of scarlet fever, at Halton 0. Neston (2), k Norley (2), Stockton Heath (2), Walton Inferior and Astmoor. • FOR TUBERCULOUS PATIENTS. A letter from the County Council submitted a scheme for providing a site for the erection of a pavilion for tuberculous patients. /It was suggested that the Rural Council were to pro- vide the Site, undertake the administration and supervision, give use of laundry and other appli- ances,, and build and furnish the pavilion. The matter was referred to the Isolation Hos- • Pital. Committee with a view to their Meeting • the Medical .Officer. '. ' The Chairman commented that this .seemed to he getting in the thin edge of a wedge that might - develop into a big scheme.</p>
