        <p>my progress, but I hope to be better shortly, when, maybe, I shall be able to come home." Driver Leigh has two brothers at present in the •Army, one of them stationed as a signal instructor at Great Crosby, attached to the 3rd 'South Lancashire Regiment; the other is at present in the Dardanelles, where he has witnissed many ,stirring engagements. ~( . PRIVATE JAMES BOOTH. , "Germans As Thick As Leaves, But Away We Went." Private James Booth, Cross Brow, Statham, of the Welsh Regiment, ,was wounded in action whilst taking part in the great British advance in September. His wounds consist of bullets lodged in both legs. At present he lies in the Red Cross Hospital, Winsford, where he is recovering as well as can be expected. Private Booth's military career extends over a period of 14 years. Whilst with the Welsh Regiment he saw service throughout the late South African campaign. At the outbreak of the present war he volunteered for active ser- vice and was eventually drafted to France on January 11th, witnessing many stirring en- gagements. He carries the Queen's Medal with six clasps, and the King's medal, with two clasps. ' Private Booth was educated at the Lymm Parochial School and attended the Parish Church, and takes a keen interest in all classes of sport, particularly the doings of Army regi- mental football. He has two brothers at present serving with the colours, one of whom is with the Welsh Regiment in France, and the other is in training with the Lancashire' Fusiliers. He also has a brother-in-law, Sergt. J. Hewitt, who has seen 26 years' service, which includes the late Boer War; and a nephew,- Private George Booth, of the Welsh</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH. Scarlet Fever Cases.—At a meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council on Tuesday, the report of the medical officer stated that two cases of scarlet fever had been reported from Stockton Heath. Volunteers For Munition Work.—The mem- bers of the local detachment a tfre Volunteer Training Corps have offered their services as munition workers for Saturdays and Sundays, and will begin 'work forthwith. A Recruiting March.—The Rand of the 4th South Lancashire (Territorial) Regiment played various selections in Victoria Square on Mon- day evening. Torches were used. to lied the bandsmen's music, and about 500 people were attracted to the Square. The hunt! afterwards marched round the district. Bandmaster Oxley , was in anent.. Whist Drive at the Adult Sch6al.—A. whist drive was held at the Adult School on. Satur- day evening. There was a fair attendance, and the prizes were won by the following :—Ladies: 1, Mrs. W. Mann; 2, Mrs. J. Holt; 3, Mrs. T. Norbury. Gentlemen: 1, Mr. Mellor; 2; Mr. J. Holt; 3. Miss May Sankey (who played a gentle- man's hand). MISSING. PRIVATE H. WEBSTER. A Scottish Borderer. Who Did Not Answer The Roll-Call. Private Harry. Webster, son of Mr. and Mrs. William Webster, of 20, Mitchell-street, Stock-</p>
