        <p>PRIVATE JOHN DA.CEY. Killed.</p>
        <p>miss him,- for he, was a very popular chap among us all. I know this sad news will be very hard to bear, but you will have the privi- lege of knowing he died a soldier's death. His pals, inclUding myself, express their deepest sympathy to you." Private Dacey was the son of- Mr. Thomas Dacey, 24; Bluecoat-street, a Corporation Gas Works? employe, who lost another son, killed in action on May 4th. This son was Rifleman Thomas Dacey, who also worked at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles', and was eighteen years of age. We recorded the deNth at the time and gave the gall-tint Rifleman's portrait. Mr. Dacey .has four children left, his only remaining son being only eight years old. He himself served in the Army for ten years. En- listing in the King's Liverpool Regiment (2nd Battalion) in 1879, he was in India nine years and saw active service in Burma, 1885-7. Private Dacey's grandfather—Joseph - Bibby— fought at Inkerman and Sebastopol. An uncle and frve cousins are serving in the present war.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. CORPORAL J. G. LEIGII. Shot In The Ankle And Now In English Hospital. During the fighting in the big advance, Cor- poral James Gordon Leigh, a married man, of 11, Clegge-street, and the only son of Mr. James Leigh, of 13, Pinner's Brow, received .a shrap- nel bullet wound in the ankle. He is now pro, gressing favourably in a Northampton hospital. Corporal Leigh. who is twenty years of age, was edutated at St. Ann's School, and was em- ployed, before the outbreak of war, by Messrs.</p>
