        <p>KILLED. SERGEANT H. MAYES. "Sniped" Whilst Observing Foe Over Parapet. "It is with very great regret that I have to write to inform you of the death of your son, Sergeant Horace Mayes, of this battalion. He was killed at about 5 p.m. on October 13th while sniping over our parapet. I cannot express to you in writing my deep sympathy with all his family in this bereavement, as I understand that other brothers of his have also given up their lives in this war. Sergeant Mayes had only. been-attached to my platoon for a month, but in that time we had all learned of his courage and devotion to duty. He was entirely fearless, and had been warned many times of the danger of firing over the parapet by day.. We are only about 90 yards from the</p>
        <p>Rylands 8r0%., Ltd. (staple department). He was educated at the National School, and attended St. Clement's Mission, where he leaves a host of friends and admirers mourning his loss. Private H. Seed was 18 years of age and single. PRIVATE SAMUEL DEAN. Grandson Of Former Local Tradesman. An official intimation has reached Mr. Henry Dean, of 36, Chorley-street, to the effect that his second son, Private Samuel Dean, of the Royal Welsh Fusiliers, was killed in actica on Sep- tember 28th. Private Dean, a single man, thirty years of age, had been in the Army several years, having served in India. He was the grandson of the late Mr. Samuel Lomax, of Sankey Green, a former well-known local tradesman. Two of his</p>
