        <p>WOUNDED. PRIVATE W. JONES. •• il I,Rylands's ll(3e.'s Harrier Gaski Wk Bomb-Tlirowinll. Private W. Jones, Highland Light Infante?; lies wounded in the North General Hospital, Leicester. A single man, 22 years of age, he was educated at the National School, eventually entering the employ of Messrs. Rylands Bros, Ltd. He was at one time a member of the , harriers itettita associated with the firm's recrea- tion club. Latterly he worked at the Vulcan Foundry, NeWton-le-Willows, but the call of duty being strong he enlisted soon after the commencement of hostilities. Writing home i 6 his parents, . who reside at 14, Albert-street, he' sfates :----" Here in Leicester we are having a lively time. • We are well looked after and have the best of everything. When in France I was 0, bomb-thrower, and was wounded in the head, as welt as being gassed, but, ,I am now doing famonsty. I was in the big" 'chatge.. We took a lot of Germans and accounted for more. I was in tit' trenches six</p>
        <p>PRIVATE SAMUEL DEAN. Killed. _ brothers are in the Warrington Territorials, and a cousin, Private W. L. Ward, is in the Liver- pool" Pals" Regiment.</p>
        <p>days before the big charge came off. One night we were washed out." Private W. Jones, hopes to be in Warrington again on a 10 days' furlough as soon as he is well again. • ACCIDENTALLY HURT. SAPPER W. BEBBINGTON. Doing Well After Losing Three Fingers. Sapper William Percy Bebbington, of the Royal Engineers, whose home is at 6, Marson- street, has been wounded. He is twenty-three years of age, and prior to enlisting on August 12th of last yeay he had done four years in the Ambulance Section of the Territorials. He enlisted in the Engineers on August 12th of last year, and was sent out to France on February 26th. He wrote home—with his left hand—to say that he had lost three fingers of his right hand. The wounded man is now in hospital at Leicester and is doing well. A few months ago he, along with three other men, received .high commendation from his officer for his gallantry</p>
        <p>''',ltj::':: . ' ' --. ..,,,Opi-.• • • .4tA,4-:„4,:,*0,•,,..,:,_,:::::E,,,;..-:„.., .. - I': -;•••:•:.•;:',' . 4.,• , .. &lt; - . , . ,• • ::0'.",',..Z;; ..:•.••"4$ . • ''' .4'.' '•'''•:•:::,4k.4.,"•::."r4 •••':•=';','%. ':::iiif4''.''.l.'•':'''..v. . , ..' ''''ttl.7l'l.•'•:to•ffii'&lt;;::.-1;7.*.' '..',.'.. . • ?it,* '''''."-,..k'';„::,...",..10iiii!;;;;r1.:4:- ..'':'...1.;•:',..,';:•‘;'../T.:•'•,,•••••• •••,• .... . . ...'''''t '...ti1i;:•'&lt;f,?''.'''..,,•:.•.•;i:0%,,:••••'::•K4,. ' • . 7.•.:.:::: ..:':::',.A.-:•:;;l'kl.ti:i:f'ii4.l4,4iNill•rt4.f:F.',. .1 ....1:''i1i...::.i&lt;'•:::j.f7.&lt;q•1hia:;:...•:.,...... 104gailltia.141g4'. ::i.",:',.''',ilWA.:::;; ''..:;7lf:r::',l4i4i'::!.,..''';;".::::'''''.....l.:',•;:ni,'.s4iingiVAlttgli. ' .:#o'••%•.:l';'''. . n'.4.l2''' ligfitNtili.:il ~,„. tlM.,•f'''''.-.'.. :' ''.••••;',.'''t*.•;b/,...!':V1 f..!,:.,,i.. .F.•.•:,.."' .: . :.•."..„,.;1,1'.'?.'-':,i.:P':':,.l ...?:::::..`Xfaiglt•':lif.illi. ' ' , •••••." .1:A,..;.‘•0; 4.:';''..Al'•:„ ..11-'::::-..f.',;:.:!..'. .:401'••'''''','„I.:.:F.1</p>
        <p>under very trying circumstances. He is a single man, and he has a brother—John—serv- ing in the Royal Field Artillery, and who is looking forward to going to the Front at no distant date.. Prior to the outbreak of/war Sapper Bobbing- ton worked at the London and North-Western Railway Co.'s locomotive shed in Liver Pool. He made his home with his mother. How It Happened. • Sapper Bebbington has explained how he met with his injury to friends who have visited him in hospital.. He came out of the trenches on M6nday, October 4th, he says, after doing fourteen days in them, and he was set to saw- ing timber for making huts for the troops. He had been at the job some time when he acci- dentally got his hand under the circular saw, with the result that three of pis fingers were taken off and a fourth seriously damaged. HoW it happened, he said, he did not know. Writing more fully re the matter of his officer's commend/11413n, the Sapper says :—" I have been recommended for braveri and my name, along with that ,of the-other three, has •to be. published. That fact was read out to us." • WOUNDED. PRIVATE U. BA II R ATT. Loses "Thumb As Resuli Of Bullet- -I%ound. Private Robert Barratt, a married man, forty years of age, whose home is in Stanhope-street, off Winwick-road, and who is a 'member of the Royal Welsh 'Fusiliers, is in hospital at Chichester. His left thumb has been ampu- tated, as the result of a bullet wound received during the big advance. , Private Barratt was educated at St. Ann's School, and was formerly employed by Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd. • PRIVATE. E. HARDING. ' Old .Campaigner Wounded In Three . Places, But Progressing. Private Ernest Harding, the King's (Liver- pool) Regiment, whose home address is 6, Mar- .-bury-street„ Latchford,, is now lying ,in the Crescent-road Military Hospital, Crumpsall, . Manchester, through wounds sustained -on October 3rd. Private Harding is an old soldier, having seen 16 years military service; chiefly in --Burma, India and South Africa. He Was recalled to the Colours in August, 1914, and had been through several engagements prior to his being wounded by a shrapnel bullet in the left arm, one in the left thigh, and a piece of shell in the left ankle. Mrs., Harding, who Visited her husband last Thursday, found him progressing as satisfactory as could be expected, although the wounds are still ,painful. The shrapnel has not yet been located, and in consequence Private Harding is to undergo an operation in- a few days. This old soldier is well-known in Warrington, having been attached to the Post Office staff. Whilst in India he was jservant to Colonel Tripp, a well-knAn Devonshire military officer. PRIVATE G. SAUNDERS. • f Doctor Takes A Bullet From His . . Lung. Private George Saunders, a married -man, of 40, Forster-Street, and a member of the 6th Battalion of the Shropshire Light Infantry, has received a serious wound in the lung, and is in hospital in France. Writing to his wife he says: "I am doing very nicely, considering the state I have been in. . . . The doctor told me when he took the bullet out of my lung I was a very lucky man to be lying on my back in bed." As a boy Private Saunders lived in Earles- town, -where he received, his education. Just before the outbreak of war he was employed by Messrs.. Pearsen and , Knowles, Ltd.. He is thirty-five years of age.. PRIVATE G. NEWNS. Fought In South Africa And Again Volunteered. Private Georgp _Newns,.,of the South TAMIL Regiment, son of the late gr. ~ N_ ew110) gentleman well-known in military and musical circles, was wounded in the back and shoulder during the advance on September 25th, and '.s now in the Wharneliffe War Hospital, Sheffield. lie is making satisfaetory progress towards retomy; qjthotgli the progress Is slow, He is an, old soldia; having fought in the late South African. campaign. On the declaratiOn of war ite figaiii immediately volunteered for active sertl4.•:„ Private Newns, who resides at 1, Bramhan-street, is a single mitt and has been an emptike, Of the.,,Mersey White Lead Works for upwaids' Of 16 years, where he Is widely respected.</p>
