        <p>I GRA MOP HON E WANTED, ‘Varriligton 50...1,41C•nr-Ltiads Who Find Time baw • el • Private J. T. Tichard, who is siva -Front with the King's Own Yorkshire LI, Infantry, writing 'under date October 6th, to a friend, Mr. A. Morris, of Halfields-road, Orford, says "We are having rather a rough time out here. We have the worst of weather to con- tend with, and it has been very wet most of the time. We have plenty of work to do, mostly at night time. The most important thing is to keep, the boys in the trenches well suplitied with rations and Lloyd George's pills for the baby killers and sausage makers': We usually leave, our head- quarters for the trenches at dusk, arriving there in about an hiyur's time. Risky Work. "The work itself is very risky, for yon may be perhatiS' traVelling gang a narrow lane, when a shell will burst close at hand. I can astare' ,you there:is Aiist Intteli tinier to. take coot: r</p>
