        <p>• A new member this week is 519 Nephew Joseph Higgins, 37, Bewsey-street. He wrote : I wish to join your regiment of children, and write up for every competition I can. My age is 11 years, and I attend St. Alban's School. The song which is most to my liking is the Belgian National Anthem. I have heard my father call it La Brabaconne. I have heard it many a time, but I hay never known the name of it until about two weeks ago. I am sure it speeds many a soldier on when he is downhearted. So it does, ioseph. I welcome 1-ou to our Corner. 474 Niece Beryl Malkin, Royal Exchange Inn, Golden Square, also chose La Brabaconne : I am sure it will be sung a great deal after this war, because it tells us about a king who after fighting gets his right and liberty. That is just what we are fighting for, so that the Belgians may regain their land, right and liberty. 352 Niece Lilian Himsworthi 15, Fitzhcrbert- street, says:— I have chosen the song we all know. It is the Russian National Anthem. Regarding the tune, it is more like a hymn. I think they are lovely words. During this great crisis it has been taught at the day schools, and I, may say we have learned it' at our school. 510 Niece Mabel Hewitt, wrote, the third best letter:— It is " Angus Macdonald." I think that the music, is very inspiriting, and the words full of pathos. Every time I hear it sung it thrills me through and makes me wish the terrible war was over, and the dear boys returning home. . 515 Niece Evelyn Molyneux, 1, Orford-avenue, writes of "Land Of Hope and Glory ": My favourite song is called " Dear Land of Hope." The first time I heard this song was at school, and I was that taken up with it that I asked the teacher, Could all our class learn it? She said, " With pleasure, if you like." I think it is a beautiful song. It has been sung in the Parr Hall by Madame Clara Butt at a patriotic concert; of course, it is a patriotic song. 434 Niece Hilda Hassall, 36, Factory-lane, says My favourite song is one which is called "There's a Land." Madame Clara Butt sang it when she was at Warrington. This song tells us that slaves are not found in England. It also says if a slave came to Englairiirnediately he . put his feet on British soil he free. 508,Nieco Elsie (lave; 27, Golborne-Street, said of this. song .The land ta,England, which is a cheerful and tearful land. At the present time it is, a tearful land, but we are hoping it will be a cheerful and a happy_ land when this great European war is over.. This Song was also chosen by 479 Niece Maud Hickey, 35, King-street; 486 Niece Violetta Crighton, Scenic Gallopers, Flag-lane; 487 Niece Florrie Stanway, 18, Edgeworth- street; 475 Niece Ethel Gibbons. 82, Catherine-street; 492 Niece Doris Blackshaw, 3, Kendrick-street. 381, Niece Mona Archer, 11, Treatbside, says : Let me thank you very sincerely for the prize 1 -</p>
