        <p>people, but also for their work generally in the town. Perhaps Father Wilson stood first among them, in the sense that the impression which would permanently remain of his work in the town would, perhaps, be greater than that created by his predecessors and colleagues. He had the great privilege and pleasure of consid- ering the Father as one of his warm, personal friends, and he regretted that it had been found necessary on account of increasing years that ho should be removed to a sphere where the respon- sibilities would be of a lighter kind. He was sure that all the committee joined in wishing the Father a long and happy life of considerable usefulness, and free, so far as human nature could be, from those frailties which came with advancing years. (Hear, hear.) The School Management Sub-committee's ex- pression of regret was unanimously endorsed. Helped By "The Pub." "If any man here," shouted the temperance speaker, "can name an honest business that has been helped by the pub. .1 will spend the rest of my life working for the liquor people." A man in the audience arose. "I consider my business an honest one," he said, "and it has been helped by the public." "What is your business?" "I, sir, am an undertaker." Good Advice. • A man advertised recently in a London paper to forward, on receipt of postage stamps, "sound practical advice, that would be applicable at any time and to all persons and conditions of life:* On receipt of the stamps, he sent his numer- ous victims the following : "-Never give a boy a penny% to hold your shadow while you climb a tree to look into the middle of next week."</p>
